IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `family-domain`
IMPORT `wills-act`
IMPORT `intestate-succession-act`
IMPORT `probate-administration-act`
IMPORT `guardianship-of-infants-act`

§ `Singapore family and succession — whole families, carried through all four Acts`

-- WHAT THIS FILE IS. Each of the four Act modules beside it encodes ONE Act
-- against ONE text, and each answers its own questions well. Nobody dies one
-- Act at a time. A death in a family raises the four Acts' questions in one
-- breath — my father died without a will, my brother is 16, who gets the
-- flat, who can apply for the grant, and who looks after my brother's share
-- — and the answer to each question is the input to the next. This file is
-- the composition: whole families, each carried through all four Acts in the
-- order the family meets them, with the HAND-OFFS asserted.
--
-- NOTHING HERE IS A RULE OF ANY ACT. Everything below is either a fixture, an
-- assertion about what the four modules say on that fixture, or one of the
-- composition-level readings in `What this corpus can and cannot say` and
-- `The joins, as expressions` — and every one of those is labelled as this
-- file's own and not as any Act's. Two of them CHECK a case file against a
-- module's verdict; none of them CHANGES one, because a bridge that recomputed
-- a recorded fact from a conclusion would be a rule of law written where no
-- lawyer would look for it. If a reader wants the law, the four Act modules are
-- where it is; this file is the regression test for the seams between them,
-- which nothing else tests.
--
-- WHY IT HAS TO EXIST, measured rather than asserted. The cross-module audit
-- of 2026-08-26 found that every join in this corpus was TYPED and none was
-- EXERCISED: each Act module's own constructors zeroed out the other three
-- Acts' fields, so no assertion anywhere could disagree with a neighbour.
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`'s own closing section says it in terms —
-- "NOTHING HERE IS YET EXERCISED ON A SHARED FILE ... The file that would
-- test them, `family-cases.l4` ... does not exist". This is that file.
--
-- WHAT HAS CHANGED SINCE THAT AUDIT, checked here rather than repeated from
-- it. `wills-act.l4` has since built a case file with facts on both sides of
-- its s 27(3) hand-off and asserts that the two agree — its
-- "THE JOIN, EXERCISED (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X4)" block. That is a check of
-- the Wills Act's own two halves against each other, and it is as far as any
-- ONE module can go: each of the four imports `prelude`, `daydate` and
-- `family-domain` and nothing else, so no Act module can call a neighbour's
-- decision at all. Every assertion in this file that names two Acts is
-- therefore something none of them could have written.
--
-- WHAT A HAND-OFF IS, AND THE TEST THIS FILE APPLIES TO ITS OWN CLAIMS. TWO
-- SINGLE-ACT ASSERTIONS SITTING NEXT TO EACH OTHER ARE NOT A JOIN. A join is
-- ONE EXPRESSION in which one Act's answer is an input to the other's, or in
-- which both are read together so that a disagreement between them is a
-- failing assertion. The test is: COULD THIS ASSERTION STILL PASS IF THE TWO
-- MODULES DISAGREED? If it could, it is adjacency, and calling it a hand-off
-- is a claim the file does not make out.
--
-- AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS FILE FAILED THAT TEST ON EVERY ONE OF ITS EIGHT
-- CLAIMED HAND-OFFS, and an adversarial review on 2026-08-26 measured it: of
-- 234 assertions, 210 named decisions of exactly one Act module, 24 named
-- none, and NONE named two. Seven of the eight claims were adjacency over a
-- shared input or a duplicated definition; the eighth — Intestate Succession
-- Act shares flowing into Probate Act ss 6(2) and 29(2) — was simply wrong,
-- because no sum reaches either provision and the infant's quarter share comes
-- from her being a legitimate child and not from her infancy.
--
-- WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOW. `The joins, as expressions` below declares a set of
-- composition-level decisions, each of which puts two Acts into one expression,
-- together with the few single-Act helpers they need; every scenario asserts
-- them, and each is asserted BOTH on a case where the two Acts agree and on a
-- case where they part company. Where a join genuinely
-- cannot be made — because no field carries the answer and no expression can
-- stand in for one — this file says which module and which fact, asserts what
-- CAN be asserted, and does not invent a bridge. The closing section
-- `Hand-offs that could not be made` lists all of those, and now also lists
-- what each of the eight claimed hand-offs actually turned out to be.
--
-- THE CASES THAT DIVIDE THE ACTS ARE THE POINT, and four of them are asserted:
--   a mother whom the Guardianship Act makes guardian by force of s 6(1) and
--     the Probate Act cannot see, because no `Guardian` record was typed
--     (scenario 1 step 4, and again in scenario 6);
--   an uncle whom the Probate Act can see and the Guardianship Act does not
--     count, because the surviving parent objects under s 7(3) (step 4);
--   a testamentary guardian the Guardianship Act counts under an appointment
--     Wills Act s 27(3) has NOT made good (scenario 3 step 3);
--   and a person whose age is nowhere in evidence, about whom the Probate Act
--     says "not an infant" and the Guardianship Act says "cannot be
--     determined" (§5A).
-- THE FIRST THREE ARE CASE FILES BUILT FOR THIS ROUND and did not exist here
-- before. The fourth did — Gerald Menon has been a fixture since the file was
-- written — but the divergence was only DESCRIBED; what is new is that it is
-- asserted, as the failure of an invariant that holds everywhere else.
--
-- THE FIXTURES ARE THIS FILE'S OWN, and that is the point rather than a
-- duplication. Each Act module's person constructor fills the other Acts'
-- fields with the neutral value — `a WA person` writes
-- `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` IS NOTHING,
-- `a PAA person in full` writes `date of birth` IS NOTHING, `an ISA person`
-- writes both the majority date and the three section 27 fields away. A
-- person built by any one of them therefore cannot answer all four Acts'
-- questions. The constructors below fill every field, so that ONE record
-- goes to all four modules and a disagreement between them is a disagreement
-- about the law and not about the file.
--
-- THE PEOPLE ARE INVENTED. No person is named in any of the four Acts.

§§ `What this corpus can and cannot say`

-- THIS FILE'S OWN READING, NOT ANY ACT'S, and the reason it is a value rather
-- than a comment: the most important thing a composition can do is DECLINE.
-- A consumer that put the four modules side by side and read whichever one
-- answered would report a distribution for an estate no Act in this corpus
-- distributes — and there are THREE ways for an estate to be in that position,
-- not one.
--
-- WHAT THIS DECISION IS AND IS NOT. It is a reading THIS FILE records, in this
-- file, with no `@export` (the cleanroom's inert style forbids one). Nothing
-- compels a consumer of the four Act modules to look at it, and an earlier
-- draft of this comment said it made the stop "something a consumer must
-- read", which it does not: `intestate-succession-act.l4`'s own
-- `this Act applies to the estate` already answers FALSE for a Muslim's
-- estate, and a consumer that never asks either question gets no stop from
-- either. If the stop is to be enforced rather than offered, its home is that
-- module's public surface or the shared ontology, and that is a proposal and
-- not something this file can do.
--
-- SIX MEMBERS, AND THREE OF THEM ARE STOPS. An earlier draft had three
-- members and reserved its only stop for s 2, so an estate whose movables
-- s 4(1) sends to a foreign law of domicile, and a death before the Act
-- commenced, both came back as "applies to the estate but has no effect on
-- these facts" — which suggests that other facts might have produced one, and
-- on those two it is another law that governs. The two members added are the
-- Act's own other two gates. (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW 2026-08-26.)
--
-- AND THE SIXTH IS THE REPAIR OF THAT REPAIR, because the s 4(1) member as
-- first written OVER-FIRED. It was keyed on the failure of both of s 5's limbs
-- and on nothing else, and that failure says NOTHING about domicile: s 5(a)
-- fails whenever the deceased was possessed beneficially of no property
-- situated in Singapore, however he was domiciled. So a deceased whom the case
-- file records as domiciled IN Singapore was handed a member whose own words
-- are "the deceased was domiciled outside Singapore" — an answer contradicting
-- the file it was computed from, while `intestate-succession-act.l4`'s own
-- `section 4(1) — the law of the country of domicile is the law of Singapore`
-- answered TRUE on the identical record. The gate now carries the domicile,
-- and the case that then falls through has a member of its own: s 4(1) names
-- the law of Singapore, which is this Act, and s 5 reaches no property for it
-- to distribute. §4B asserts both sides of it. (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW
-- 2026-08-26, the cross-module join audit.)
DECLARE `What this corpus can say about the distribution of an estate` IS ONE OF
    `the Intestate Succession Act 1967 governs the distribution, and its rules have been applied`
    `nothing in the Intestate Succession Act 1967 applies to this estate, and no other Act in this corpus distributes it`
    `the Intestate Succession Act 1967 applies to the estate but has no effect on these facts`
    `the deceased was domiciled outside Singapore and left no immovable property here, so section 4(1) sends the distribution of his movable property to the law of his domicile, and no Act in this corpus is that law`
    `the deceased was domiciled in Singapore and was possessed beneficially of no property situated here, so section 4(1) names the law of Singapore and section 5 reaches no property to distribute`
    `the death is not proved to be after 2 June 1967, so section 5 does not reach this estate, and no other Act in this corpus distributes it`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 2, s 3 ("intestate"), s 4(1), s 5
-- NOT A RULE OF ANY ACT. It reads the decisions of
-- `intestate-succession-act.l4` that make up that Act's gate, and reports which
-- of the five cases the estate is in.
--
-- THE ORDER IS THE ORDER OF THE GATES. s 2 is asked FIRST, because where it
-- bites there is no Act left to have an effect. Then the whole gate, which is
-- the only affirmative answer. Then the reasons the gate failed, taken in the
-- order the Act states them: whether there is an intestacy at all (s 3, and if
-- there is not, a will governs and the Wills Act is in this corpus, so it is
-- NOT a stop); whether the death is after the commencement (s 5); and whether
-- either of s 5's situs-and-domicile limbs is made out.
--
-- THAT LAST QUESTION IS ASKED TWICE, because the limbs can fail for two
-- opposite reasons and the answers differ. Where the deceased was domiciled
-- OUTSIDE Singapore, s 4(1) refers his movables to the law of that domicile,
-- no Act in this corpus is that law, and s 5(b) has found no immovable
-- property here to bring any of it back: that is a stop. Where he was
-- domiciled IN Singapore, s 4(1) refers his movables to the law of Singapore —
-- this Act — and s 5(a) has found no property of his situated here for it to
-- distribute: that is not a stop but an empty fund. So the domicile is
-- conjoined to each of the two gates, and the OTHERWISE below is a totality
-- guard that no case reaches.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A `What this corpus can say about the distribution of an estate`
`what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (`this Act applies to the estate` `the deceased person`)
           THEN `nothing in the Intestate Succession Act 1967 applies to this estate, and no other Act in this corpus distributes it`
           IF `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the deceased person`
           THEN `the Intestate Succession Act 1967 governs the distribution, and its rules have been applied`
           IF NOT (`section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the deceased person`)
           THEN `the Intestate Succession Act 1967 applies to the estate but has no effect on these facts`
           IF NOT (`section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` `the deceased person`)
           THEN `the death is not proved to be after 2 June 1967, so section 5 does not reach this estate, and no other Act in this corpus distributes it`
           IF     NOT (   `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `the deceased person`
                       OR `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` `the deceased person`)
              AND NOT (`section 4(1) — the law of the country of domicile is the law of Singapore` `the deceased person`)
           THEN `the deceased was domiciled outside Singapore and left no immovable property here, so section 4(1) sends the distribution of his movable property to the law of his domicile, and no Act in this corpus is that law`
           IF     NOT (   `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `the deceased person`
                       OR `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` `the deceased person`)
              AND `section 4(1) — the law of the country of domicile is the law of Singapore` `the deceased person`
           THEN `the deceased was domiciled in Singapore and was possessed beneficially of no property situated here, so section 4(1) names the law of Singapore and section 5 reaches no property to distribute`
           OTHERWISE `the Intestate Succession Act 1967 applies to the estate but has no effect on these facts`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 ss 6, 7, 10, 17
GIVEN `the infant concerned in the estate` IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF Guardian
-- NOT A RULE OF ANY ACT, AND THE HINGE OF THE FLAGSHIP DECISION BELOW. It
-- takes the Guardianship Act's OWN ANSWER to "who are the guardians of this
-- infant" — `the persons who are guardians of the infant`, a list of
-- references — and uses it to select the `Guardian` records that section 17
-- may be asked about. The Act's output is the input to the filter.
--
-- WHY THE RAW LIST WOULD NOT DO, and this is a repair (ADVERSARIAL REVIEW,
-- 2026-08-26, finding 2). An earlier draft of the flagship mapped over
-- `Infant`'s `the guardians of the infant` — the records a case file happens
-- to hold — and got the guardian set wrong in both directions. It OVER-COUNTED:
-- a mother the court has removed under s 10 still has a record, and
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` answers EMPTY about her while the raw
-- traversal answered about her anyway; so did an appointee of a living parent,
-- whose s 7(1)-(2) appointment operates only "after his death". And it
-- UNDER-COUNTED: s 6(1) makes the surviving mother guardian by force of the
-- section, so the commonest case of all — a mother with no `Guardian` record —
-- was a traversal over nobody. That is the same defect
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` records having repaired at its own s 6(3),
-- re-introduced one module up.
`the guardians the Guardianship of Infants Act counts, for whom the case file holds a record` MEANS
    filter (GIVEN `the guardian` YIELD
                elem (`the reference for the guardian` `the guardian`)
                     (`the persons who are guardians of the infant` `the infant concerned in the estate`))
           (`the infant concerned in the estate`'s `the guardians of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(2); s 17
GIVEN `the infant concerned in the estate` IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
-- NOT A RULE OF ANY ACT. The other half of the same measurement: the people
-- the Guardianship Act counts as guardians and for whom the file holds no
-- `Guardian` record at all. Ordinarily that is the surviving parent, whom
-- s 6(1)-(2) makes guardian without any appointment. Section 17 cannot be
-- asked about such a person, because every fact it reads —
-- `of the person or of the property`, and whether the court has otherwise
-- ordered — lives on a record that does not exist. That is G9 met at the
-- composition layer rather than argued in a comment.
`the guardians the Guardianship of Infants Act counts, for whom the case file holds no record` MEANS
    filter (GIVEN `the reference` YIELD
                NOT (elem `the reference`
                          (map `the reference for the guardian`
                               (`the infant concerned in the estate`'s `the guardians of the infant`))))
           (`the persons who are guardians of the infant` `the infant concerned in the estate`)

-- THIS FILE'S OWN READING, NOT ANY ACT'S. Three answers and not two, because
-- section 17 has a fact it needs and a case file may not carry it.
DECLARE `Whether a guardian could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` IS ONE OF
    `a guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`
    `no guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`
    `the Guardianship of Infants Act counts as guardian a person for whom the case file records no kind of guardianship, so section 17 cannot be asked about him`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(2), s 7, s 10, s 17
-- NOT A RULE OF ANY ACT, AND THE ONE THIS FILE WAS BUILT FOR. Every other
-- legal tool will tell a family what the infant is ENTITLED to. The question
-- they actually face is whether anybody can hand it over, and that is
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`'s section 17: "A guardian of the property
-- of an infant shall not, unless in any case the court otherwise orders, be
-- empowered to give a good discharge for any legacy or other capital moneys
-- payable to or receivable by an infant."
--
-- IT IS A COMPOSITION OF ONE ACT'S TWO ANSWERS AND NOT A THIRD OPINION. The
-- Guardianship Act decides WHO the guardians are, in ss 6, 7 and 10; it then
-- decides in s 17 what a guardian of the PROPERTY may do. This decision feeds
-- the first answer into the second, which is the thing no single decision in
-- that module does. The money side is joined to it separately, at
-- `the capital moneys of this infant that no guardian may give a good
-- discharge for` below, where the Intestate Succession Act's figure enters.
--
-- THE ORDER OF THE BRANCHES IS THE ORDER OF THE QUESTION. "Some guardian
-- could" is made out by ONE guardian who can, whatever is unknown about the
-- others, so the affirmative is tested first. Only where no recorded guardian
-- can does the unrecorded one matter, and then the honest answer is that the
-- section cannot be applied — not that nobody can.
GIVEN `the infant concerned in the estate` IS AN Infant
GIVETH A `Whether a guardian could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`
`whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` MEANS
    BRANCH IF any (GIVEN `the guardian` YIELD
                       NOT (`section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `the guardian`))
                  (`the guardians the Guardianship of Infants Act counts, for whom the case file holds a record`
                       `the infant concerned in the estate`)
           THEN `a guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`
           IF NOT (null (`the guardians the Guardianship of Infants Act counts, for whom the case file holds no record`
                             `the infant concerned in the estate`))
           THEN `the Guardianship of Infants Act counts as guardian a person for whom the case file records no kind of guardianship, so section 17 cannot be asked about him`
           OTHERWISE `no guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`


§§ `Constructors — one record, four Acts`

-- FIXTURES, NOT LAW. L4 has no record-update operator, so a scenario is
-- either a fully spelled literal or a constructor partially applied. These
-- take the fields the four Acts between them vary and default the rest.
--
-- WHY THEY ARE HERE AND NOT BORROWED. See the header: each Act module's own
-- constructor writes the OTHER Acts' fields away, which is exactly why no
-- assertion in any of them could ever exercise a join. These fill every field
-- that any of the four reads, so that one `Deceased person` can be put to all
-- four modules unchanged.

GIVEN `the name of the person`                     IS A STRING
      `the reference`                              IS A STRING
      `when he was born`                           IS A MAYBE DATE
      `when he attains the age of majority`        IS A MAYBE DATE
      `whether he is living`                       IS A `Vital status`
      `his children`                               IS A LIST OF Child
      `he is a soldier being in actual military service` IS A BOOLEAN
      `he is a mariner or seaman being at sea`     IS A BOOLEAN
      `he is so circumstanced`                     IS A BOOLEAN
      `he lacks mental capacity`                   IS A BOOLEAN
      `he is absent from Singapore`                IS A BOOLEAN
      `he is willing to act`                       IS A BOOLEAN
      `he so desires`                              IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Person
-- THE DATE OF BIRTH AND THE MAJORITY DATE ARE BOTH PARAMETERS, and that is
-- the whole reason this constructor exists. `wills-act.l4` computes its own
-- age bar from `date of birth`, because Wills Act section 4 states a number —
-- "No will made by any person under 21 years of age shall be valid." — and
-- section 27(1) disapplies it. `probate-administration-act.l4` and
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` both read
-- `the date on which the person attains the age of majority`, which
-- `family-domain.l4` declares as an already-construed input. A person who
-- carries only one of the two answers only some of the four Acts.
`a person in the case file, in full` MEANS Person WITH
    `name`                                            IS `the name of the person`
    `the reference for this person`                   IS `the reference`
    `date of birth`                                   IS `when he was born`
    `vital status`                                    IS `whether he is living`
    `children`                                        IS `his children`
    `at the date of the death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` IS FALSE
    `money or other property given, paid or settled by the intestate during his life to or for the advancement of the person` IS 0
    `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` IS `when he attains the age of majority`
    `a soldier being in actual military service`      IS `he is a soldier being in actual military service`
    `a mariner or seaman being at sea`                IS `he is a mariner or seaman being at sea`
    `a member of any naval or marine forces so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he would be in actual military service` IS `he is so circumstanced`
    `so desires that letters of administration be granted to such person as the court thinks fit` IS `he so desires`
    `lacks mental capacity (within the meaning of the Mental Capacity Act 2008) to make decisions with respect to the management and administration of the person's property and affairs` IS `he lacks mental capacity`
    `absent from Singapore`                           IS `he is absent from Singapore`
    `willing to act`                                  IS `he is willing to act`

GIVEN `the name of the person`              IS A STRING
      `the reference`                       IS A STRING
      `when he was born`                    IS A MAYBE DATE
      `when he attains the age of majority` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `whether he is living`                IS A `Vital status`
      `his children`                        IS A LIST OF Child
GIVETH A Person
-- The ordinary member of a family: no privilege under Wills Act section 27,
-- mental capacity not in issue, present in Singapore and willing to act.
`a person in the case file` MEANS
    `a person in the case file, in full` `the name of the person` `the reference`
        `when he was born` `when he attains the age of majority` `whether he is living`
        `his children` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE

GIVEN `when he was born` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
-- NOT A RULE OF ANY ACT, and it is the only place in this file where the age
-- of majority enters as a number. None of the four Acts fixes one:
-- `family-domain.l4` says so at `the date on which the person attains the age
-- of majority` and `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` says so again at
-- `The infant, and the age this Act does not fix`. Twenty-one is used here
-- because Wills Act section 4 is the only number any of the four contains and
-- a case file whose two age answers came from different numbers would
-- manufacture a disagreement the Acts do not have. Leap-safe by construction:
-- the year is incremented and the calendar does the rest.
`twenty-one years after` MEANS
    JUST (Date (DATE_DAY `when he was born`) (DATE_MONTH `when he was born`)
               (DATE_YEAR `when he was born` PLUS 21))

GIVEN `the offspring` IS A Person
GIVETH A Child
`a legitimate child in the case file` MEANS Child WITH
    `the child`                                          IS `the offspring`
    `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is` IS TRUE
    `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam` IS FALSE

GIVEN `the offspring` IS A Person
GIVETH A Child
-- The third corner of Intestate Succession Act section 3, kept because two of
-- the four modules decide the same question about it and the composition has
-- to be able to put them side by side.
`a child in the case file neither legitimate nor adopted` MEANS Child WITH
    `the child`                                          IS `the offspring`
    `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is` IS FALSE
    `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam` IS FALSE

GIVEN `the other party`        IS A Person
      `when it was solemnised` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `it subsists`            IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Marriage
`a marriage in the case file` MEANS Marriage WITH
    `the spouse`                                    IS `the other party`
    `the date of the solemnisation of the marriage` IS `when it was solemnised`
    `subsisting at the death of the deceased, in the sense of not dissolved or annulled before the death` IS `it subsists`

GIVEN `the marriages` IS A LIST OF Marriage
      `the father`    IS A MAYBE Person
      `the mother`    IS A MAYBE Person
GIVETH A Family
-- No collateral relatives: every scenario in this file is decided in the
-- first or second degree, and an invented uncle would be an invented fact.
`a family in the case file` MEANS Family WITH
    `the marriages of the deceased`            IS `the marriages`
    `the father of the deceased`               IS `the father`
    `the mother of the deceased`               IS `the mother`
    `the brothers and sisters of the deceased` IS EMPTY
    `the grandparents of the deceased`         IS EMPTY
    `the uncles and aunts of the deceased`     IS EMPTY

GIVEN `movable property in Singapore`                IS A Money
      `immovable property in Singapore`              IS A Money
      `the expenses of due administration`           IS A Money
      `the debts and liabilities`                    IS A Money
      `the value beneficially disposed of by the will` IS A Money
      `the creditors`                                IS A LIST OF Creditor
GIVETH AN Estate
-- THE DEBTS ARE A PARAMETER AND NOTHING READS THEM, which is one of the
-- findings this file is here to make visible. See scenario 5C.
--
-- THE CREDITORS ARE A SEPARATE PARAMETER BECAUSE THEY ARE A SEPARATE FIELD,
-- and the difference between the two is the whole of scenario 5C's repair.
-- `the debts and liabilities of the deceased` is a Money cell that no rule in
-- the corpus reads. `the creditors of the deceased` IS read, by exactly one
-- rule — Probate and Administration Act s 29(2), which deducts the secured
-- debts from the sworn amount of the bond. A pair that varies the first tests
-- the ABSENCE of a reader; only a pair that varies the second tests s 29(2).
`an estate in the case file, with creditors` MEANS Estate WITH
    `the value of the movable property situated in Singapore`        IS `movable property in Singapore`
    `the value of the immovable property situated in Singapore`      IS `immovable property in Singapore`
    `the value of the movable property situated outside Singapore`   IS 0
    `the value of the immovable property situated outside Singapore` IS 0
    `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore` IS 0
    `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated outside Singapore` IS 0
    `the value of any moneys payable by an appointed insurer pursuant to the Dependants’ Protection Insurance Scheme or any other equivalent scheme maintained by the Central Provident Fund Board` IS 0
    `the value of the property administered by the Public Trustee`   IS 0
    `the amount of the funds in court directed to be paid, transferred or delivered to him` IS 0
    `the value of land settled previously to the death of the deceased` IS 0
    `the trustees of the settlement`                                 IS EMPTY
    `the value of the interest in property which at the time of his death is charged with the payment of money` IS 0
    `the amount of the charge on that interest`                      IS 0
    `the deceased has by will, deed or other document signified a contrary or other intention` IS FALSE
    `the debts and liabilities of the deceased`                      IS `the debts and liabilities`
    `the funeral, testamentary and administration expenses`          IS `the expenses of due administration`
    `the creditors of the deceased`                                  IS `the creditors`
    `fully administered`                                             IS FALSE
    `the value of the property beneficially disposed of by the will` IS `the value beneficially disposed of by the will`

GIVEN `movable property in Singapore`                IS A Money
      `immovable property in Singapore`              IS A Money
      `the expenses of due administration`           IS A Money
      `the debts and liabilities`                    IS A Money
      `the value beneficially disposed of by the will` IS A Money
GIVETH AN Estate
-- The ordinary estate: no creditor has proved.
`an estate in the case file` MEANS
    `an estate in the case file, with creditors` `movable property in Singapore`
        `immovable property in Singapore` `the expenses of due administration`
        `the debts and liabilities` `the value beneficially disposed of by the will` EMPTY

GIVEN `the person owed`  IS A Person
      `the amount owed`  IS A Money
      `it is secured`    IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Creditor
`a creditor in the case file` MEANS Creditor WITH
    `the creditor`          IS `an individual` `the person owed`
    `the debt`              IS `the amount owed`
    `secured`               IS `it is secured`
    `residing in Singapore` IS TRUE

GIVEN `the appointee`                     IS A Person
      `the reference for the infant`      IS A STRING
      `how he was appointed`              IS A `How the guardian was appointed`
      `of the person or of the property`  IS A `Kind of guardianship`
      `alone, or jointly with`            IS AN `Alone or jointly`
      `he has been removed by the court`  IS A BOOLEAN
      `he purported to give a discharge`  IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court has otherwise ordered as to the discharge` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Guardian
-- THE KIND OF GUARDIANSHIP AND THE TWO SECTION 17 FACTS ARE ALL PARAMETERS,
-- and no constructor in the four Act modules varies all three.
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`'s property-case constructor fixes the kind
-- at `guardian of the property of the infant`, which a surviving parent under
-- section 6 is not; its ordinary constructor fixes both discharge facts FALSE.
-- Scenario 1 needs a guardian who is both — the mother, guardian of the person
-- and property by force of section 6(1), who has purported to discharge the
-- infant's share.
`a guardian in the case file` MEANS Guardian WITH
    `the guardian`                       IS `the appointee`
    `the reference for the infant`       IS `the reference for the infant`
    `how the guardian was appointed`     IS `how he was appointed`
    `of the person or of the property`   IS `of the person or of the property`
    `alone or jointly`                   IS `alone, or jointly with`
    `refuses to act`                     IS FALSE
    `the mother or father objects to his so acting` IS FALSE
    `considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant` IS FALSE
    `removed from his guardianship by the court` IS `he has been removed by the court`
    `has given security`                 IS TRUE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to security` IS FALSE
    `has sold, mortgaged, exchanged, or otherwise parted with the possession of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has leased land belonging to the infant` IS FALSE
    `the term of the lease, in years`    IS NOTHING
    `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has given a good discharge for a legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by the infant` IS `he purported to give a discharge`
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to giving a good discharge` IS `the court has otherwise ordered as to the discharge`
    `the provision made out of the income of the property of the infant for his maintenance and education`
        IS (`A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 0, `the period` IS `monthly`)
    `has the permission of the court to apply a sum exceeding $100 per month` IS FALSE

GIVEN `the appointee`                     IS A Person
      `the reference for the infant`      IS A STRING
      `how he was appointed`              IS A `How the guardian was appointed`
      `of the person or of the property`  IS A `Kind of guardianship`
GIVETH A Guardian
-- THE SURVIVING PARENT'S OBJECTION, WHICH ONLY ONE OF THE TWO ACTS CAN SEE.
-- Guardianship of Infants Act s 7(3) — "Any guardian so appointed shall act
-- jointly with the mother or father, as the case may be, of the infant so long
-- as the mother or father remains alive, unless the mother or father objects to
-- his so acting." Where the survivor objects, `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`
-- stops counting him as a guardian of the infant. Probate and Administration
-- Act s 21(2) names "the guardian of the person and property of the infant" and
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` reads the `Guardian` record's kind, removal
-- and identity and nothing else — so it goes on opening the grant to him.
-- Scenario 1 step 4 asserts the divergence in one expression.
`a guardian in the case file whom the surviving parent objects to` MEANS Guardian WITH
    `the guardian`                       IS `the appointee`
    `the reference for the infant`       IS `the reference for the infant`
    `how the guardian was appointed`     IS `how he was appointed`
    `of the person or of the property`   IS `of the person or of the property`
    `alone or jointly`                   IS `alone`
    `refuses to act`                     IS FALSE
    `the mother or father objects to his so acting` IS TRUE
    `considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant` IS FALSE
    `removed from his guardianship by the court` IS FALSE
    `has given security`                 IS TRUE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to security` IS FALSE
    `has sold, mortgaged, exchanged, or otherwise parted with the possession of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has leased land belonging to the infant` IS FALSE
    `the term of the lease, in years`    IS NOTHING
    `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has given a good discharge for a legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by the infant` IS FALSE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to giving a good discharge` IS FALSE
    `the provision made out of the income of the property of the infant for his maintenance and education`
        IS (`A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 0, `the period` IS `monthly`)
    `has the permission of the court to apply a sum exceeding $100 per month` IS FALSE

GIVEN `the child`                     IS A Person
      `the father, if in evidence`    IS A MAYBE Person
      `the mother, if in evidence`    IS A MAYBE Person
      `his guardians`                 IS A LIST OF Guardian
      `the capital of his property`   IS A Money
GIVETH AN Infant
`an infant in the case file` MEANS Infant WITH
    `the infant`                                                IS `the child`
    `the father of the infant`                                  IS `the father, if in evidence`
    `the mother of the infant`                                  IS `the mother, if in evidence`
    `the guardians of the infant`                               IS `his guardians`
    `some other person has parental rights with respect to him` IS FALSE
    `the income of the property of the infant`                  IS 0
    `the capital of the property of the infant`                 IS `the capital of his property`
    `of an age to express an independent opinion`               IS FALSE

GIVEN `the person or corporation` IS A `Person or corporation`
      `the capacity`              IS A `Capacity in which the grant is made`
      `for whose use and benefit` IS A MAYBE STRING
GIVETH A Grantee
`a grantee in the case file` MEANS Grantee WITH
    `the grantee`                             IS `the person or corporation`
    `the capacity in which the grant is made` IS `the capacity`
    `the reference for the person for whose use and benefit the grant is made` IS `for whose use and benefit`

GIVEN `what kind`      IS A `Kind of grant`
      `the grantees`   IS A LIST OF Grantee
      `when it was made` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the limitation on it` IS A `Limitation on the grant`
GIVETH A Grant
`a grant in the case file` MEANS Grant WITH
    `the kind of grant`             IS `what kind`
    `the grantees`                  IS `the grantees`
    `the date of the grant`         IS `when it was made`
    `the limitation`                IS `the limitation on it`
    `the court that made the grant` IS `a court within the meaning of these Acts` `the General Division of the High Court`
    `the exception subject to which the grant is made` IS NOTHING
    `expired by effluxion of time or by the happening of the event or contingency on which it was limited` IS FALSE
    `revoked`                       IS FALSE
    `has given such security as is lawfully required to be furnished` IS TRUE
    `has been extracted`            IS TRUE
    `sealed under section 47(1) of the Probate and Administration Act 1934` IS FALSE
    `the date of the sealing`       IS NOTHING

GIVEN `the person who died` IS A Person
      `his family`          IS A Family
      `his estate`          IS AN Estate
      `his will`            IS A MAYBE Will
      `he was a Muslim`     IS A BOOLEAN
      `he was domiciled in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the grants`          IS A LIST OF Grant
      `the infants concerned in the estate` IS A LIST OF Infant
GIVETH A `Deceased person`
-- THE DOMICILE IS A PARAMETER BECAUSE TWO OF THE INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT'S
-- THREE GATES TURN ON IT. s 4(1) — "The distribution of the movable property of
-- a person deceased shall be regulated by the law of the country in which he
-- was domiciled at the time of his death." — and s 5(a)-(b). An estate whose
-- deceased was domiciled abroad and left no immovable property in Singapore is
-- one this corpus must decline, and until this parameter existed no fixture
-- here could build one.
`a deceased person in the case file, in full` MEANS `Deceased person` WITH
    `the deceased`                                    IS `the person who died`
    `the family`                                      IS `his family`
    `the estate`                                      IS `his estate`
    `the will`                                        IS `his will`
    `domiciled in Singapore at the time of his death` IS `he was domiciled in Singapore`
    `carried on business or resided in Singapore within 12 months of his death` IS TRUE
    `a Muslim`                                        IS `he was a Muslim`
    `the grants made in respect of the estate`        IS `the grants`
    `the probate applications made in respect of the estate` IS EMPTY
    `a next of kin, creditor or other person has appeared and made out a claim to letters of administration` IS FALSE
    `the infants concerned in the estate`             IS `the infants concerned in the estate`

GIVEN `the person who died` IS A Person
      `his family`          IS A Family
      `his estate`          IS AN Estate
      `his will`            IS A MAYBE Will
      `he was a Muslim`     IS A BOOLEAN
      `the grants`          IS A LIST OF Grant
      `the infants concerned in the estate` IS A LIST OF Infant
GIVETH A `Deceased person`
-- The ordinary case file: the deceased was domiciled in Singapore.
`a deceased person in the case file` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file, in full` `the person who died` `his family`
        `his estate` `his will` `he was a Muslim` TRUE `the grants`
        `the infants concerned in the estate`

GIVEN `the deceased person`             IS A `Deceased person`
      `the will and its further facts`  IS A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`
GIVETH A `A case under this Act`
-- `wills-act.l4` takes a case rather than a will, because its section 28 asks
-- about an application for rectification and about the grants. No scenario
-- here is a section 28 case, so all four of those facts are the neutral value.
`the Wills Act case of` MEANS
    `a case` `the deceased person` `the will and its further facts` FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE


§§ `Reading the answers — helpers that decide nothing`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5, s 7 rules 1-9
GIVEN `the entitlements` IS A LIST OF Entitlement
      `the reference`    IS A STRING
GIVETH A Share
-- NOT A RULE OF ANY ACT. `intestate-succession-act.l4` returns the whole
-- distribution as a list; a scenario wants one person's fraction out of it.
-- Summed rather than picked, so that a person who appears twice is not
-- silently reported at his first share; nil for a person who takes nothing,
-- which is the same thing the Act says by not naming him.
`the fraction taken by the person whose reference is` MEANS
    sum (map (GIVEN `the entitlement` YIELD
                  `the entitlement`'s `the fraction of the property distributable under this Act`)
             (filter (GIVEN `the entitlement` YIELD
                          `the reference for the person entitled` (`the entitlement`'s `the person entitled`) EQUALS
                                  JUST `the reference`)
                     `the entitlements`))

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5, s 7
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
      `the reference`       IS A STRING
GIVETH A Money
-- NOT A RULE OF ANY ACT. The fraction, applied to the fund the fraction is a
-- fraction of — `intestate-succession-act.l4`'s
-- `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction`. It is
-- what a family asks for and the Act never states: a sum of money.
`the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` MEANS
        `the fraction taken by the person whose reference is`
            (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `the deceased person`)
            `the reference`
    TIMES `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `the deceased person`


§§ `The joins, as expressions`

-- WHAT THIS SECTION IS FOR, AND WHY IT WAS ADDED. An adversarial review of
-- this file on 2026-08-26 measured its assertions and found that not one of
-- them put two Acts into a single expression: every claimed hand-off was two
-- adjacent single-Act assertions, verified by a human reading them side by
-- side. Its test is the right one and this section adopts it — COULD THIS
-- ASSERTION STILL PASS IF THE TWO MODULES DISAGREED? Where the answer is yes,
-- the assertion is adjacency and not a join, however it is labelled.
--
-- SO THE DECISIONS BELOW EACH PUT TWO ACTS INTO ONE EXPRESSION, and each is
-- asserted both on a case where the two agree and on a case where they part
-- company. (Three of the definitions in this section are single-Act helpers
-- that exist only to keep the composed ones readable —
-- `the grantees taking as the guardian of the person and property of the
-- infant`, `the guardian holds under an appointment made by a parent by will`
-- and `the Wills Act leaves this will operative` — and each says so at its own
-- site.)
--
-- NONE OF THEM IS A RULE OF ANY ACT: they are readings this file records, or
-- integrity checks on a case file, and they are labelled as which. Nothing here
-- recomputes a fact of the ontology from another module's verdict — that would
-- be putting a rule of law where no lawyer would look for it — and the ones
-- that come closest, the Wills Act checks, say in terms that they check a file
-- rather than change one.

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5, s 7; Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 17
GIVEN `the deceased person`             IS A `Deceased person`
      `the infant concerned in the estate` IS AN Infant
GIVETH A Money
-- THE JOIN THIS FILE WAS BUILT FOR, AS ONE EXPRESSION. NOT A RULE OF EITHER
-- ACT. Guardianship of Infants Act s 17 speaks of "any legacy or other capital
-- moneys payable to or receivable by an infant" and does not say what they
-- come to; the Intestate Succession Act says what they come to and says
-- nothing about paying them over. This decision is the sentence a family
-- actually needs: HOW MUCH MONEY IS STUCK.
--
-- THE VALUE FLOWS. `intestate-succession-act.l4`'s distribution, applied to
-- its own fund, produces the sum; `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`'s answer
-- about the guardians decides whether that sum is the answer or nil. Change
-- either module's mind and the figure moves: give the daughter a different
-- fraction and the number changes, let the court authorise the discharge and
-- it drops to nil. That is what the previous draft's two adjacent assertions
-- could not do.
--
-- THE UNKNOWN CASE FALLS ON THE SIDE OF THE MONEY BEING STUCK, and it is the
-- statute that puts it there: s 17 withholds a power rather than conferring
-- one, so a guardian about whom the file records nothing is a guardian with no
-- power, and the moneys stay unpayable until somebody says otherwise.
`the capital moneys of this infant that no guardian may give a good discharge for` MEANS
    IF   `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`
             `the infant concerned in the estate`
         EQUALS `a guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`
    THEN 0
    ELSE `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is`
             `the deceased person`
             (`the infant concerned in the estate`'s `the infant`'s `the reference for this person`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(2); Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 ss 6, 7, 10
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
      `the grantee`         IS A Grantee
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT. The GUARDIANSHIP ACT'S answer, asked about a
-- PROBATE ACT grantee: is the person to whom this grant was made, for an
-- infant's use and benefit, one of the persons that Act counts as a guardian
-- of that infant? `the persons who are guardians of the infant` is the list
-- and it is the input to the search.
`the Guardianship of Infants Act counts this grantee as a guardian of the infant for whose use the grant is made` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the grantee`'s `the reference for the person for whose use and benefit the grant is made`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST `the reference for the infant` THEN
        any (GIVEN `the infant` YIELD
                     (`the infant`'s `the infant`'s `the reference for this person`) EQUALS `the reference for the infant`
                 AND any (GIVEN `the reference for a guardian` YIELD
                              (`the grantee`'s `the grantee`) `is the individual whose reference is` `the reference for a guardian`)
                         (`the persons who are guardians of the infant` `the infant`))
            (`the deceased person`'s `the infants concerned in the estate`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(2)
GIVEN `the grant` IS A Grant
GIVETH A LIST OF Grantee
-- NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT. The grantees this grant names in the capacity
-- s 21(2) opens — "the guardian of the person and property of the infant".
`the grantees taking as the guardian of the person and property of the infant` MEANS
    filter (GIVEN `the grantee` YIELD
                (`the grantee`'s `the capacity in which the grant is made`)
                    EQUALS `as the guardian of the person and property of the infant`)
           (`the grant`'s `the grantees`)

-- THIS FILE'S OWN READING, NOT ANY ACT'S. Four members, because two Acts each
-- answering yes or no about one grant have four possible pairs, and BOTH
-- mixed pairs happen on facts this file builds.
DECLARE `Whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this grant` IS ONE OF
    `both Acts are satisfied: the Probate Act opens section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act counts the grantee as a guardian of the infant`
    `the Probate and Administration Act opens section 21(2) on this grant, and the Guardianship of Infants Act does not count the grantee as a guardian of the infant`
    `the Guardianship of Infants Act counts the grantee as a guardian of the infant, and the Probate and Administration Act does not open section 21(2) on this grant`
    `neither Act is satisfied on this grant`

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(2); Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 ss 6(1)-(2), 7(1)-(3), 10
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
      `the grant`           IS A Grant
      `the date`            IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this grant`
-- NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT, AND THE JOIN THE PROBATE ACT ASKS FOR IN TERMS.
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` says at its s 21(2) that it "can decide
-- WHETHER a grant may be made to the infant's guardian and cannot decide WHO
-- that guardian is". This decision puts its WHETHER beside the Guardianship
-- Act's WHO in one expression, and reports which of the two opened.
--
-- THEY DIVERGE IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, AND THIS FILE BUILDS BOTH CASES.
--   THE PROBATE ACT OPENS AND THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT DOES NOT COUNT HIM. The
--     Probate Act reads three facts off the `Guardian` record — the kind, the
--     removal, the identity — and nothing else. The Guardianship Act reads
--     s 7(3) too: where the surviving parent "objects to his so acting" the
--     appointee is not a guardian of the infant at all. Scenario 1 step 4
--     builds it.
--   THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT COUNTS HER AND THE PROBATE ACT DOES NOT OPEN. s 6(1)
--     makes the surviving mother guardian by force of the section, with no
--     record needed; s 21(2) can only see a guardian somebody has typed a
--     `Guardian` record for. Delete the redundant record and the two Acts
--     answer differently about the same woman on the same day. Scenario 1
--     step 4 builds that too.
`whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this grant` MEANS
    BRANCH IF     `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant`
                      `the deceased person` `the grant` `the date`
              AND `the Guardianship Act counts every grantee taking as guardian`
           THEN `both Acts are satisfied: the Probate Act opens section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act counts the grantee as a guardian of the infant`
           IF `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant`
                  `the deceased person` `the grant` `the date`
           THEN `the Probate and Administration Act opens section 21(2) on this grant, and the Guardianship of Infants Act does not count the grantee as a guardian of the infant`
           IF `the Guardianship Act counts every grantee taking as guardian`
           THEN `the Guardianship of Infants Act counts the grantee as a guardian of the infant, and the Probate and Administration Act does not open section 21(2) on this grant`
           OTHERWISE `neither Act is satisfied on this grant`
    WHERE
        -- Vacuously true over no such grantee would report an agreement about
        -- nobody, so the non-emptiness is a conjunct and not an afterthought.
        `the Guardianship Act counts every grantee taking as guardian` MEANS
                NOT (null (`the grantees taking as the guardian of the person and property of the infant` `the grant`))
            AND all (GIVEN `the grantee` YIELD
                         `the Guardianship of Infants Act counts this grantee as a guardian of the infant for whose use the grant is made`
                             `the deceased person` `the grantee`)
                    (`the grantees taking as the guardian of the person and property of the infant` `the grant`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 27(3); Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(1)-(2)
GIVEN `the guardian` IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT: which of `How the guardian was appointed`'s eight
-- members are appointments BY WILL, and so are the ones Wills Act s 27(3)
-- speaks to.
`the guardian holds under an appointment made by a parent by will` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the guardian`'s `how the guardian was appointed`
    WHEN `appointed by the father by will` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `appointed by the mother by will` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 27(3); Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(1)-(2)
GIVEN `the will, put to the Wills Act`   IS A `A case under this Act`
      `the infant concerned in the estate` IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT — A CHECK ON A CASE FILE, and the bridge
-- `wills-act.l4` asks for in terms. That module writes, above its s 27(3)
-- rule: "the hand-off is a VALUE — the list-valued decision below — that a
-- module seeing both Acts can call directly, per appointment." This is a
-- module seeing both Acts, calling it.
--
-- THE VALUE FLOWS FROM THE WILLS ACT INTO THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT'S ANSWER.
-- `the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good`
-- is the Wills Act's output; it is the list every testamentary guardian that
-- the Guardianship Act counts must be found in. Change the testator from a
-- soldier to a civilian and the Wills Act's list empties while the
-- Guardianship Act's does not — which is exactly the disagreement scenario 3
-- step 3 used to describe in prose.
--
-- WHY IT IS A CHECK AND NOT A RULE. `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`'s
-- s 7(1)-(2) reads `appointed by the father by will` and asks nothing about
-- the instrument, and it is right not to: nothing in the shared ontology
-- carries a Wills Act verdict, and a rule here that struck an appointment out
-- on the Wills Act's say-so would be a rule of the Guardianship Act written
-- where no lawyer would look for it. What this decision does is report that
-- the case file has broken the discipline that module states — that
-- `appointed by the father by will` is "to be set only where the Wills Act
-- decision has answered TRUE".
`every testamentary guardian the Guardianship of Infants Act counts holds under an appointment the Wills Act has made good` MEANS
    all (GIVEN `the guardian` YIELD
             elem (`the reference for the guardian` `the guardian`)
                  (`the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good` `the will, put to the Wills Act`))
        (filter `the guardian holds under an appointment made by a parent by will`
                (`the guardians the Guardianship of Infants Act counts, for whom the case file holds a record`
                     `the infant concerned in the estate`))

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 4, s 5, s 6, s 13, s 15, s 27(1)
GIVEN `the will, put to the Wills Act` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- NOT A RULE OF THE WILLS ACT — A READING OF ITS TWO ANSWERS TOGETHER. The
-- module answers formal validity and revocation separately and is right to:
-- a revoked will is still formally valid, and the two sections say different
-- things. What a case file needs before it can be built is the conjunction,
-- and `wills-act.l4` deliberately does not state it, because "operative" is
-- not a word of the Act.
`the Wills Act leaves this will operative` MEANS
        (   `the formal validity of the will` `the will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS `valid`
         OR `the formal validity of the will` `the will, put to the Wills Act`
                EQUALS `valid, being the will of a soldier being in actual military service, or of a mariner or seaman being at sea, disposing of his personal estate (section 27(1))`)
    AND NOT (`s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the will, put to the Wills Act`)

-- THIS FILE'S OWN READING, NOT ANY ACT'S. Four members: the Wills Act says
-- the will operates or it does not, and the case file holds it or it does not.
DECLARE `Whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` IS ONE OF
    `the Wills Act leaves this will operative, and the case file holds it`
    `the Wills Act leaves no operative will, and the case file has struck it out`
    `the Wills Act leaves no operative will, and the case file still holds it, so the other three Acts answer as though it stood`
    `the Wills Act leaves this will operative, and the case file has struck it out, so the other three Acts answer as though there were none`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 4, s 5, s 6, s 13, s 15, s 27(1)
GIVEN `the case file`                  IS A `Deceased person`
      `the will, put to the Wills Act` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A `Whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will`
-- NOT A RULE OF ANY ACT — A CHECK ON A CASE FILE, AND THE G1 BRIDGE. Nothing
-- may recompute `Deceased person`'s `the will` from the Wills Act's verdict:
-- that field is a FACT — the document exists or it does not — and the verdict
-- is a CONCLUSION about it, so a rule here would be putting law in the
-- composition. That reasoning is unchanged and it is why this decision CHECKS
-- rather than changes.
--
-- BUT A CHECK IS NOT BLOCKED, and treating it as blocked was the mistake. The
-- file already accepts checks of exactly this shape —
-- `every guardian recorded for this infant is recorded against this infant`,
-- and `wills-act.l4`'s own two-halves equality — and this one converts three
-- narrative findings (scenarios 2A, 2B and 3 step 3) into machine-checked
-- ones. The third member is the one that costs money: it is the state in
-- which the Intestate Succession Act reports that its rules have no effect,
-- and the Probate Act reports that the deceased did not die intestate, on a
-- will the Wills Act has just defeated.
`whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` MEANS
    BRANCH IF     `the Wills Act leaves this will operative` `the will, put to the Wills Act`
              AND isJust (`the case file`'s `the will`)
           THEN `the Wills Act leaves this will operative, and the case file holds it`
           IF `the Wills Act leaves this will operative` `the will, put to the Wills Act`
           THEN `the Wills Act leaves this will operative, and the case file has struck it out, so the other three Acts answer as though there were none`
           IF isJust (`the case file`'s `the will`)
           THEN `the Wills Act leaves no operative will, and the case file still holds it, so the other three Acts answer as though it stood`
           OTHERWISE `the Wills Act leaves no operative will, and the case file has struck it out`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 15; Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 3
GIVEN `the case file`                  IS A `Deceased person`
      `the will, put to the Wills Act` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT — A CHECK, and the sharpest form of the G1 gap.
-- The Wills Act has defeated the will; the Intestate Succession Act, reading
-- the same file, still reports that the deceased is not an "intestate" within
-- its s 3 and therefore distributes nothing. Both are answering their own
-- question correctly and the file is telling the family the wrong thing.
`the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Intestate Succession Act still reads one` MEANS
        NOT (`the Wills Act leaves this will operative` `the will, put to the Wills Act`)
    AND NOT (`section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the case file`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 15; Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(1)
GIVEN `the case file`                  IS A `Deceased person`
      `the will, put to the Wills Act` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT — the same check from the Probate Act's side, and
-- it is a separate decision because the two Acts read the field for different
-- purposes: the Intestate Succession Act to decide whether it distributes,
-- the Probate Act to decide whether the grant is probate or letters of
-- administration.
`the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Probate Act still routes to probate` MEANS
        NOT (`the Wills Act leaves this will operative` `the will, put to the Wills Act`)
    AND NOT (`s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` `the case file`)

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(5); Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5, s 7
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`
-- NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT, AND THE ONE HAND-OFF IN THIS FILE THAT IS A
-- LITERAL OUTPUT-TO-INPUT. s 29(5) — "When the administrator is entitled to
-- the whole of the estate after payment of the debts, sureties in the bond may
-- ordinarily be dispensed with." `probate-administration-act.l4` takes that
-- antecedent as a SWORN BOOLEAN on `The further facts on which the grant
-- machinery turns`, and says at that field why: no field anywhere has type
-- `LIST OF Entitlement`, so no route runs from a `Deceased person` to who is
-- entitled to what. `intestate-succession-act.l4` computes the same fact and
-- offers it, at `one person takes the whole of the property distributable
-- under this Act`, saying at ITS site that it is offered "so that the sworn
-- boolean ... has something to be checked against".
--
-- THIS CONSTRUCTOR IS THE CHECKING. It builds the Probate Act's input record
-- with the Intestate Succession Act's computed answer in the field the
-- Probate Act would otherwise have taken on oath, and the Probate Act's s 29
-- decision is then evaluated on it. One Act's output IS the other Act's
-- input; nothing is asserted twice and read together by a human.
--
-- WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT SUPPLY, and the Intestate Succession Act module says
-- so at its own site: s 29(5)'s "after payment of the debts" is Probate Act
-- s 57's arithmetic, which is out of scope in that module, so an estate whose
-- debts exhaust it satisfies the computed antecedent and not the subsection.
-- The composition narrows the gap by one conjunct and does not close it.
`the further facts, the section 29(5) antecedent taken from the Intestate Succession Act` MEANS
    `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns` WITH
        `the administrator is entitled to the whole of the estate after payment of the debts`
            IS `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` `the deceased person`
        `the Family Court thinks fit to require such security`                          IS FALSE
        `there is danger that the property may be wasted`                               IS FALSE
        `it appears that such appointment would not be a sufficient protection for the estate` IS FALSE
        `the Public Trustee has by writing signed by him declared that he undertakes to administer such property` IS FALSE

@ref Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 21(1); Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 -- "infant"
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the date`   IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT — A CROSS-CHECK, and it is an INVARIANT where the
-- file previously had four pointwise assertions. Both modules read one field
-- of the shared ontology, `the date on which the person attains the age of
-- majority`, and each writes its own comparison: the Probate Act's
-- `is an infant on` and the Guardianship Act's `the person is an infant on the
-- date in question`. They are two independently written answers to one
-- question and neither feeds the other, so this is a cross-check and not a
-- hand-off — but stated as an equality it fails wherever they part company,
-- which four assertions on two dates could not.
--
-- AND THEY DO PART COMPANY, BY DESIGN, WHERE THE DATE IS NOT IN EVIDENCE. The
-- Probate Act folds the absence to FALSE, because "`is an infant on` feeds
-- four provisions ... and a gate has to open or close"; the Guardianship Act
-- returns NOTHING and propagates it. Both are argued at their own sites and
-- this file does not repair either; what it does is assert the divergence
-- rather than leave it to be discovered.
`the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` MEANS
        (`the person is an infant on the date in question` `the person` `the date`)
    EQUALS
        JUST (`the person` `is an infant on` `the date`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 3 — "“child”"; Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(a)
GIVEN `the relation` IS A Child
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT — A CROSS-CHECK OVER A DUPLICATED DEFINITION. The
-- Probate Act module does not import the Intestate Succession Act module; it
-- RESTATES s 3's two limbs over `Child`'s own fields and says so, adding that
-- "If the two ever disagree the shared ontology is the place to reconcile
-- them". Nothing hands off here and nothing could: there are two texts of one
-- definition. What a composition can do is assert that they still say the same
-- thing, in one expression that fails if either is edited alone.
`the two modules agree whether this child is a “child” within the meaning of section 3` MEANS
        (`section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` `the relation`)
    EQUALS
        (`a “child” within the meaning of section 3 of the Intestate Succession Act 1967` `the relation`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 3; Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 18(4)(a)
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- The same cross-check over every child of one deceased, so that a case file
-- rather than a hand-picked `Child` value is what is put to the two modules.
`the two modules agree about every child of this deceased` MEANS
    all `the two modules agree whether this child is a “child” within the meaning of section 3`
        (`the deceased person`'s `the deceased`'s `children`)


§ `Scenario 1 — the intestacy with an infant child`

-- THE FAMILY. Sivakumar Ramanathan died on 15 March 2025 without a will,
-- leaving a widow, an adult son and a daughter of twelve. The flat comes to
-- $500,000 and the bank balances to $700,000; the funeral, testamentary and
-- administration expenses come to $200,000. Letters of administration are
-- applied for on 15 January 2026.
--
-- THE FAMILY'S FOUR QUESTIONS, and the Act that answers each:
--   is there an operative will?                Wills Act 1838
--   who takes, and in what shares?             Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7
--   who may take the grant, and does the
--     daughter's minority force a second
--     administrator?                           Probate and Administration Act 1934 ss 6, 21
--   who is the daughter's guardian, and can
--     that guardian give a good discharge for
--     her share?                               Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 ss 6, 17
--
-- THE LAST ONE IS WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Every other tool will tell this
-- family that Divya is entitled to a quarter of a million dollars. What they
-- face on the day is whether anybody can hand it over.

§§ `Scenario 1 — the people, the estate and the grant`

`the day letters of administration are applied for` MEANS YMD 2026 1 15

`Meera Sivakumar` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Meera Sivakumar" "meera" (JUST (YMD 1968 7 22))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1968 7 22)) `living` EMPTY

`Arjun Sivakumar` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Arjun Sivakumar" "arjun" (JUST (YMD 2000 4 10))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 2000 4 10)) `living` EMPTY

`Divya Sivakumar` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Divya Sivakumar" "divya" (JUST (YMD 2012 11 2))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 2012 11 2)) `living` EMPTY

`Sivakumar Ramanathan` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Sivakumar Ramanathan" "sivakumar" (JUST (YMD 1965 2 10))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1965 2 10)) (`died on` (YMD 2025 3 15))
        (LIST `a legitimate child in the case file` `Arjun Sivakumar`
            , `a legitimate child in the case file` `Divya Sivakumar`)

-- THE MOTHER'S OWN GUARDIAN RECORD. Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)
-- makes her guardian BY FORCE OF THE SECTION — "On the death of the father of
-- an infant, the mother, if surviving, shall, subject to the provisions of
-- this Act, be guardian of the infant" — so no appointment and no record is
-- needed to make her one. The record exists because two other things have to
-- be written down somewhere: whether the court has removed her under s 10,
-- and, for s 17, whether she has purported to give a discharge for her
-- daughter's share.
`Meera, guardian of Divya by virtue of surviving the father` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Meera Sivakumar` "divya"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant` `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Meera, having purported to discharge Divya’s share` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Meera Sivakumar` "divya"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant` `alone` FALSE TRUE FALSE

`Meera, the court having authorised the discharge` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Meera Sivakumar` "divya"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant` `alone` FALSE TRUE TRUE

`Meera, since removed from her guardianship by the court` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Meera Sivakumar` "divya"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant` `alone` TRUE FALSE FALSE

GIVEN `the mother’s guardianship` IS A Guardian
GIVETH AN Infant
`Divya, an infant concerned in the estate, the mother holding` MEANS
    `an infant in the case file` `Divya Sivakumar` (JUST `Sivakumar Ramanathan`) (JUST `Meera Sivakumar`)
        (LIST `the mother’s guardianship`) 250_000

`the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan` MEANS `an estate in the case file` 700_000 500_000 200_000 0 0

`the family of Sivakumar Ramanathan` MEANS
    `a family in the case file`
        (LIST `a marriage in the case file` `Meera Sivakumar` (JUST (YMD 1994 6 4)) TRUE)
        NOTHING NOTHING

-- THE GRANT. Letters of administration to the widow and the adult son: the
-- widow taking `as the guardian of the person and property of the infant` for
-- Divya's use and benefit, which is the capacity Probate and Administration
-- Act 1934 s 21(2) opens to her, and the son as administrator. Two
-- individuals, which is what s 6(2) requires where there is a minority.
`the grant to the widow and the son` MEANS
    `a grant in the case file` `letters of administration`
        (LIST `a grantee in the case file` (`an individual` `Meera Sivakumar`)
                  `as the guardian of the person and property of the infant` (JUST "divya")
            , `a grantee in the case file` (`an individual` `Arjun Sivakumar`) `as administrator` NOTHING)
        (JUST (YMD 2026 1 15))
        (`limited in some other way` "limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself")

-- THE SAME GRANT TO THE WIDOW ALONE, which is the counterfactual s 6(2) is
-- about. Nothing else about it differs.
`the grant to the widow alone` MEANS
    `a grant in the case file` `letters of administration`
        (LIST `a grantee in the case file` (`an individual` `Meera Sivakumar`)
                  `as the guardian of the person and property of the infant` (JUST "divya"))
        (JUST (YMD 2026 1 15))
        (`limited in some other way` "limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself")

-- A GRANT TO THE SON ALONE, IN NO SPECIAL CAPACITY. Neither s 21(2) nor
-- s 6(2) is satisfied by it, and it is here so that each of those two
-- failures has a fixture that fails only it.
`the grant to the son alone` MEANS
    `a grant in the case file` `letters of administration`
        (LIST `a grantee in the case file` (`an individual` `Arjun Sivakumar`) `as administrator` NOTHING)
        (JUST (YMD 2026 1 15)) `not limited`

GIVEN `the mother’s guardianship` IS A Guardian
      `the grants`                IS A LIST OF Grant
GIVETH A `Deceased person`
`the Sivakumar intestacy, with` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Sivakumar Ramanathan` `the family of Sivakumar Ramanathan`
        `the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan` NOTHING FALSE `the grants`
        (LIST `Divya, an infant concerned in the estate, the mother holding` `the mother’s guardianship`)

`the Sivakumar intestacy` MEANS
    `the Sivakumar intestacy, with` `Meera, guardian of Divya by virtue of surviving the father`
        (LIST `the grant to the widow and the son`)

`Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` MEANS
    `Divya, an infant concerned in the estate, the mother holding`
        `Meera, guardian of Divya by virtue of surviving the father`


§§ `Scenario 1, step 1 — is there an operative will? (Wills Act 1838)`

-- AND THIS IS THE FIRST HAND-OFF THAT CANNOT BE MADE, at the very first step.
-- `wills-act.l4`'s entry point is `the formal validity of the will`, which
-- takes a `A case under this Act`, which holds a
-- `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`, which holds a
-- `Will`. Where the deceased left no will there is no `Will` to build one
-- from, so THE WILLS ACT CANNOT BE ASKED THE QUESTION AT ALL. What answers it
-- is the shared ontology's `Deceased person`'s `the will` being NOTHING, read
-- directly — which is what the other two Acts do:
-- `intestate-succession-act.l4`'s section 3 and
-- `probate-administration-act.l4`'s s 18(1) both read that field themselves
-- and neither consults the Wills Act.
--
-- SO THE STEP IS REAL AND THE JOIN IS NOT. Recorded here rather than papered
-- over with a Wills Act call that would have to invent a will to make.
#ASSERT isNothing (`the Sivakumar intestacy`'s `the will`)

§§ `Scenario 1, step 2 — who takes, and in what shares? (Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7)`

-- s 3 — "“intestate” includes any person who leaves a will but dies intestate
-- as to some beneficial interest in his property". No will at all is the
-- plainest case of it.
#ASSERT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT `this Act applies to the estate` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT `section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` `the Sivakumar intestacy` EQUALS
                `the Intestate Succession Act 1967 governs the distribution, and its rules have been applied`

-- s 5 — the property "after payment thereout of the expenses of due
-- administration": $1,200,000 less $200,000.
#ASSERT `section 5 — that property` `the Sivakumar intestacy` EQUALS 1_200_000
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `the Sivakumar intestacy` EQUALS
                1_000_000

-- s 7 rule 2 — "If an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue, the
-- spouse shall be entitled to one-half of the estate." Rule 3 then divides the
-- other half "by equal portions per stirpes to and amongst the children of the
-- person dying intestate": two children, a quarter each.
#ASSERT `rule 2 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 1 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT `the share of the estate to which the surviving spouse is entitled` `the Sivakumar intestacy` EQUALS (1 DIVIDED BY 2)
#ASSERT `rule 3 — the share of the estate distributed among the issue, subject to the rights of the surviving spouse` `the Sivakumar intestacy` EQUALS (1 DIVIDED BY 2)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Sivakumar intestacy` EQUALS
                LIST "Meera Sivakumar takes 0.5", "Arjun Sivakumar takes 0.25", "Divya Sivakumar takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `the Sivakumar intestacy` EQUALS 1

-- THE SAME ANSWER AS A SUM OF MONEY, which is what the family asked for and
-- what the Act never states.
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Sivakumar intestacy` "meera" EQUALS 500_000
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Sivakumar intestacy` "arjun" EQUALS 250_000
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Sivakumar intestacy` "divya" EQUALS 250_000

-- THE INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT'S HALF OF THE SECTION 17 JOIN, AND ONLY THAT
-- HALF. `intestate-succession-act.l4` filters its own answer down to the
-- entitlements of a person the case file records as an infant concerned in the
-- estate, matching by `the reference for this person`. This assertion is
-- single-Act and is labelled as such: nothing in it could fail because the
-- Guardianship Act changed its mind. The two Acts are put into one expression
-- at step 5, where the figure below becomes the money nobody can receipt for.
#ASSERT `the shares taken by an infant concerned in the estate, read out` `the Sivakumar intestacy` EQUALS
                LIST "Divya Sivakumar takes 0.25"

§§ `Scenario 1, step 3 — who may take the grant? (Probate and Administration Act 1934 ss 6, 18, 21)`

-- s 18(1) — "When a person has died intestate, the court may grant letters of
-- administration of his estate." AMBIGUITY A7 of `probate-administration-act.l4`
-- reads "intestate" here as no will at all, which is this case either way.
#ASSERT `s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` `the Sivakumar intestacy`

-- s 18(4)(a) — "the husband or widow or next of kin or any of them". The widow
-- first, then the next of kin in the order the classes are built.
#ASSERT `the persons entitled under section 18(4)(a)` `the Sivakumar intestacy` EQUALS
                LIST `Meera Sivakumar`, `Arjun Sivakumar`, `Divya Sivakumar`
#ASSERT     `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` `the Sivakumar intestacy` "meera"
#ASSERT     `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` `the Sivakumar intestacy` "divya"
#ASSERT NOT `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` `the Sivakumar intestacy` "nobody"

-- NOT A HAND-OFF: A DUPLICATED DEFINITION, AND THE HALF OF THE AUDIT'S
-- DISAGREEMENT ROW THAT HAS SINCE BEEN REPAIRED. The Probate Act's "next of
-- kin" and the Intestate Succession Act's takers are the same three people on
-- these facts, and that is not a coincidence of the fixture:
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` builds the class through
-- `a “child” within the meaning of section 3 of the Intestate Succession Act
-- 1967`. But nothing FLOWS from one module to the other. That module does not
-- import this one; it RESTATES s 3's two limbs over `Child`'s own fields and
-- says so, adding that "If the two ever disagree the shared ontology is the
-- place to reconcile them". There are two texts of one definition, and the
-- most a composition can do is assert in one expression that they still agree
-- — which is done at §5E, on the family where the definition bites. Scenario 5B
-- asserts the case where the two Acts genuinely part company, which is a
-- difference about the LADDER and not about the definition.
#ASSERT `the two modules agree about every child of this deceased` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Sivakumar intestacy` "meera" GREATER THAN 0
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Sivakumar intestacy` "arjun" GREATER THAN 0
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Sivakumar intestacy` "divya" GREATER THAN 0

-- s 6(2) — "Letters of administration shall, if there is a minority or if a
-- life interest arises under the will, be granted either to a trust
-- corporation, with or without an individual, or to not less than 2
-- individuals." The minority is Divya's.
--
-- AND IT IS NOT A HAND-OFF, WHICH AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS COMMENT SAID IT
-- WAS. The claim it made — that "a fact found under one Act changes an answer
-- under another", the ISA share flowing into s 6(2) and s 29(2) — is wrong
-- twice over, and the correction is worth stating because it is the kind of
-- thing a composition is for.
--   NO SUM REACHES s 6(2). `s 6(2) — there is a minority` is an age test over
--     `the infants concerned in the estate`; it never looks at a fraction or
--     at a fund. Nor does s 29(2), which reads `Estate` values and the secured
--     creditors.
--   AND THE INFANCY DOES NOT PRODUCE THE SHARE. Rule 3 divides the issue's
--     half "by equal portions per stirpes to and amongst the children of the
--     person dying intestate": Divya's quarter is hers as a legitimate child
--     and she would take a quarter at forty. §5C asserts exactly this
--     independence.
-- WHAT IS ACTUALLY HERE is one recorded fact — `the date on which the person
-- attains the age of majority` — read by two Acts for two unrelated purposes:
-- the Probate Act counts the administrators, the Guardianship Act decides
-- whether it has an infant to govern. That is a shared input, and §5A asserts
-- the agreement as an invariant rather than claiming a flow.
#ASSERT     `s 6(2) — there is a minority` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — a life interest arises under the will` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT     `s 6(2) — the requirement as to the grantees is engaged` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT     `s 6(2) — the grant complies with section 6(2)` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — the grant complies with section 6(2)` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the widow alone` `the day letters of administration are applied for`

-- s 21(1) — "No probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a
-- person while he is an infant." The daughter is barred; her brother is not.
#ASSERT     `s 21(1) — no probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a person while he is an infant` `Divya Sivakumar` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT NOT `s 21(1) — no probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a person while he is an infant` `Arjun Sivakumar` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT NOT `s 21(1) — no probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a person while he is an infant` `Meera Sivakumar` `the day letters of administration are applied for`

-- s 21(2) — "Where an infant would, but for his infancy, be entitled to
-- probate or letters of administration, letters of administration with or
-- without the will annexed may, subject to section 6(1), be granted to the
-- guardian of the person and property of the infant". She would be entitled
-- but for her infancy BECAUSE she is one of the s 18(4)(a) persons — which is
-- the previous step's answer being used as this step's input.
#ASSERT `s 21(2) — this infant would, but for his infancy, be entitled to probate or letters of administration`
            `the Sivakumar intestacy` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT `the infant executors or persons so entitled` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
                LIST `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate`
#ASSERT NOT `s 21(3) — there are 2 or more infant executors or persons so entitled` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT `s 21 — the limitation on a grant made under subsection (2)` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
                `limited in some other way` "limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself"
#ASSERT     `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT NOT `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the son alone` `the day letters of administration are applied for`

-- THE TENSION BETWEEN s 21(2) AND s 6(2), WHICH IS INSIDE ONE ACT AND IS
-- VISIBLE ONLY WHEN THE WHOLE FAMILY IS CARRIED THROUGH. s 21(2) opens the
-- grant to the guardian and makes it "subject to section 6(1)" — s 6(1)
-- alone, the four-person cap, and NOT s 6(2). So a grant to the guardian
-- ALONE satisfies s 21(2) in terms and still breaches s 6(2), because the
-- same infancy that opened s 21(2) is the minority that engages s 6(2). The
-- family's answer is that the widow may take the grant as guardian but not by
-- herself. Both halves are asserted on the identical grant.
#ASSERT     `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the widow alone` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — the grant complies with section 6(2)` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the widow alone` `the day letters of administration are applied for`

§§ `Scenario 1, step 4 — who is the infant's guardian? (Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 ss 6, 10)`

-- s 6(1) — "On the death of the father of an infant, the mother, if surviving,
-- shall, subject to the provisions of this Act, be guardian of the infant,
-- either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father." The
-- father here is the intestate, so his death is the same fact the Intestate
-- Succession Act read to open its distribution and the Probate Act read to
-- open its grant.
--
-- ONE DEATH, THREE ACTS — AND THAT IS A SHARED INPUT AND NOT A HAND-OFF, said
-- plainly because an earlier draft of this file listed it among the hand-offs
-- it had MADE. `Divya, an infant concerned in the estate, the mother holding`
-- carries `the father of the infant` IS (JUST `Sivakumar Ramanathan`) — the
-- identical `Person` value the other two Acts read as the deceased — so the
-- claim that one `vital status` drives all three is true. What is not true is
-- that any of the three answers is an input to any other: they are three
-- readings of one fact, and the assertion below and the two above could each
-- pass while the others failed.
#ASSERT     `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate`
#ASSERT NOT `the father is guardian of the infant under section 6(2)` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate`
#ASSERT     `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS LIST "meera"

-- THE ACT'S OWN GATE OVER THE ANSWER, which is where
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` puts the infancy test it deliberately does
-- not thread through its sections.
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
            `the person is an infant on that date, and the guardians of the infant are` (LIST "meera")

-- AND THE SAME QUESTION AFTER SHE COMES OF AGE. Divya attains majority on
-- 2 November 2033; on that day this Act has no infant to govern, and the
-- answer is not "no guardians" but "no infant".
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` (YMD 2033 11 2) EQUALS
            `the person has attained the age of majority on that date, so this Act has no infant to govern`
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` (YMD 2033 11 1) EQUALS
            `the person is an infant on that date, and the guardians of the infant are` (LIST "meera")

-- THE INTEGRITY CHECK ON THE FILE, which `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`
-- writes as a check and not as a rule: every guardian recorded in this
-- infant's list names this infant in his own back-reference.
#ASSERT `every guardian recorded for this infant is recorded against this infant` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate`

-- THE SECOND JOIN, AND IT IS A COMPOSITION AND NOT AN ADJACENCY.
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` records that it "can decide WHETHER a grant
-- may be made to the infant's guardian and cannot decide WHO that guardian
-- is"; `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` answers WHO. The grant above was made
-- to Meera in the capacity `as the guardian of the person and property of the
-- infant` for the use and benefit of `divya`.
--
-- WHAT THIS FILE USED TO DO, AND WHY IT WAS NOT ENOUGH. It asserted
-- `elem "meera" (the persons who are guardians of the infant ...)` — the
-- Guardianship Act alone — beside the s 21(2) assertion two steps up — the
-- Probate Act alone — and a reader put the two together. NEITHER ASSERTION
-- COULD FAIL BECAUSE OF THE OTHER. The decision below puts both Acts in one
-- expression, so a disagreement is a failing assertion and not a thing a
-- reader might notice.
#ASSERT elem "meera" (`the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate`)
#ASSERT `whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this grant` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
            `both Acts are satisfied: the Probate Act opens section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act counts the grantee as a guardian of the infant`

-- AND THE TWO AGREE WHEN THEY BOTH SAY NO. The court removes Meera under
-- s 10. The Guardianship Act stops naming her; the Probate Act stops opening
-- s 21(2) to her, because its `is this guardian of the person and property of
-- the infant` reads the same removal field. Two independently written paths
-- through one recorded fact, asserted as one value.
`Divya, her mother having been removed from the guardianship` MEANS
    `Divya, an infant concerned in the estate, the mother holding` `Meera, since removed from her guardianship by the court`
`the Sivakumar intestacy, the widow having been removed from the guardianship` MEANS
    `the Sivakumar intestacy, with` `Meera, since removed from her guardianship by the court`
        (LIST `the grant to the widow and the son`)
#ASSERT NOT `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` `Divya, her mother having been removed from the guardianship`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Divya, her mother having been removed from the guardianship` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT NOT `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant`
                `the Sivakumar intestacy, the widow having been removed from the guardianship`
                `the grant to the widow and the son` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT `whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this grant` `the Sivakumar intestacy, the widow having been removed from the guardianship` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
            `neither Act is satisfied on this grant`

-- AND NOW THE TWO CASES WHERE THEY PART COMPANY, WHICH IS WHAT AN ADJACENCY
-- COULD NEVER HAVE SHOWN. Both were found by an adversarial review of this
-- file on 2026-08-26 and neither was built here before; on facts the corpus
-- can hold, the two Acts give different answers about who the infant's
-- guardian is, and until now nothing would have noticed.

-- FIRST DIRECTION: THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT COUNTS HER AND THE PROBATE ACT DOES
-- NOT OPEN. s 6(1) — "On the death of the father of an infant, the mother, if
-- surviving, shall, subject to the provisions of this Act, be guardian of the
-- infant" — makes Meera guardian BY FORCE OF THE SECTION, and
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` says in terms at its s 6 block that a parent
-- "is guardian by force of the section and needs no `Guardian` record to be
-- one". The Probate Act can only see a guardian somebody has typed a record
-- for: its `is this guardian of the person and property of the infant` reads
-- `of the person or of the property` off a `Guardian`, and there is none.
--
-- SO THIS FILE DIFFERS FROM SCENARIO 1 IN NOTHING BUT A REDUNDANT RECORD, and
-- the widow's grant stops being one s 21(2) authorises.
`Divya, no Guardian record having been made for her mother` MEANS
    `an infant in the case file` `Divya Sivakumar` (JUST `Sivakumar Ramanathan`) (JUST `Meera Sivakumar`)
        EMPTY 250_000
`the Sivakumar intestacy, no record having been made for the widow` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Sivakumar Ramanathan` `the family of Sivakumar Ramanathan`
        `the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan` NOTHING FALSE
        (LIST `the grant to the widow and the son`)
        (LIST `Divya, no Guardian record having been made for her mother`)
#ASSERT `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` `Divya, no Guardian record having been made for her mother`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Divya, no Guardian record having been made for her mother` EQUALS LIST "meera"
#ASSERT NOT `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant`
                `the Sivakumar intestacy, no record having been made for the widow`
                `the grant to the widow and the son` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT `whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this grant` `the Sivakumar intestacy, no record having been made for the widow` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
            `the Guardianship of Infants Act counts the grantee as a guardian of the infant, and the Probate and Administration Act does not open section 21(2) on this grant`

-- SECOND DIRECTION: THE PROBATE ACT OPENS AND THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT DOES NOT
-- COUNT HIM. s 7(3) — "Any guardian so appointed shall act jointly with the
-- mother or father, as the case may be, of the infant so long as the mother or
-- father remains alive, unless the mother or father objects to his so acting."
-- Sivakumar appointed his brother Rajan guardian of Divya by his will; Meera,
-- who survives, objects. `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` therefore does not
-- count Rajan among the guardians of the infant at all. The Probate Act reads
-- three facts off his `Guardian` record — the kind, the removal, the identity
-- — and the objection is not one of them, so s 21(2) opens the grant to him.
`Rajan Ramanathan` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Rajan Ramanathan" "rajan" (JUST (YMD 1970 5 5))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1970 5 5)) `living` EMPTY
`Rajan, appointed guardian of Divya by her father’s will, the widow objecting` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file whom the surviving parent objects to` `Rajan Ramanathan` "divya"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
`Divya, her uncle appointed by the will and her mother objecting` MEANS
    `an infant in the case file` `Divya Sivakumar` (JUST `Sivakumar Ramanathan`) (JUST `Meera Sivakumar`)
        (LIST `Meera, guardian of Divya by virtue of surviving the father`
            , `Rajan, appointed guardian of Divya by her father’s will, the widow objecting`) 250_000
`the grant to the uncle as guardian` MEANS
    `a grant in the case file` `letters of administration`
        (LIST `a grantee in the case file` (`an individual` `Rajan Ramanathan`)
                  `as the guardian of the person and property of the infant` (JUST "divya"))
        (JUST (YMD 2026 1 15))
        (`limited in some other way` "limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself")
`the Sivakumar intestacy, the uncle appointed and the widow objecting` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Sivakumar Ramanathan` `the family of Sivakumar Ramanathan`
        `the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan` NOTHING FALSE
        (LIST `the grant to the uncle as guardian`)
        (LIST `Divya, her uncle appointed by the will and her mother objecting`)
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Rajan, appointed guardian of Divya by her father’s will, the widow objecting` `Divya, her uncle appointed by the will and her mother objecting` EQUALS
            `the mother or father objects to his so acting, and he does not act jointly`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Divya, her uncle appointed by the will and her mother objecting` EQUALS LIST "meera"
#ASSERT `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant`
            `the Sivakumar intestacy, the uncle appointed and the widow objecting`
            `the grant to the uncle as guardian` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT `whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this grant` `the Sivakumar intestacy, the uncle appointed and the widow objecting` `the grant to the uncle as guardian` `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
            `the Probate and Administration Act opens section 21(2) on this grant, and the Guardianship of Infants Act does not count the grantee as a guardian of the infant`

-- WHICH ACT IS RIGHT, AND THIS FILE DOES NOT DECIDE. Each is right about its
-- own question: the Guardianship Act decides who is a guardian and the Probate
-- Act decides to whom a grant may be made, and neither purports to settle the
-- other. What the composition can say — and what nothing in the corpus said
-- before — is that on two buildable case files the answers are inconsistent,
-- and that a consumer putting the four modules side by side and reading
-- whichever one answered would report a grant to a person the Guardianship Act
-- says is not the guardian, or refuse one to a person it says is.

§§ `Scenario 1, step 5 — can the guardian give a good discharge? (Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 17)`

-- s 17 — "A guardian of the property of an infant shall not, unless in any
-- case the court otherwise orders, be empowered to give a good discharge for
-- any legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by an infant."
--
-- THIS IS THE STEP THE FILE WAS BUILT FOR. The Intestate Succession Act made
-- Divya entitled to $250,000. The Probate and Administration Act put the
-- grant in her mother's hands as guardian of her person and property. The
-- Guardianship of Infants Act now says that the mother is NOT EMPOWERED to
-- give a good discharge for that quarter of a million — so the personal
-- representative who pays it to her remains accountable — unless the court
-- has ordered otherwise in this case.
--
-- THE DEFAULT IS NO POWER, which is why the assertion is positive: a guardian
-- about whom nothing is recorded is one about whom no order has been made.
#ASSERT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `Meera, guardian of Divya by virtue of surviving the father`
#ASSERT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `Meera, having purported to discharge Divya’s share`
#ASSERT NOT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `Meera, the court having authorised the discharge`

-- AND WHAT FOLLOWS FROM HER HAVING PAID HERSELF ANYWAY. The ontology's field
-- records that she PURPORTED to discharge; s 17 decides whether the discharge
-- is good.
#ASSERT NOT `the discharge the guardian purported to give is not a good discharge` `Meera, guardian of Divya by virtue of surviving the father`
#ASSERT     `the discharge the guardian purported to give is not a good discharge` `Meera, having purported to discharge Divya’s share`
#ASSERT NOT `the discharge the guardian purported to give is not a good discharge` `Meera, the court having authorised the discharge`

-- THE COMPOSITION'S OWN SENTENCE, over the whole family: nobody can hand
-- Divya's share over until the court says so. The decision asks section 17
-- only of the guardians the Guardianship Act itself counts — here, her mother
-- and nobody else.
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS LIST "meera"
#ASSERT `the guardians the Guardianship of Infants Act counts, for whom the case file holds no record` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS
            `no guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`
`Divya, the court having authorised the discharge` MEANS
    `Divya, an infant concerned in the estate, the mother holding` `Meera, the court having authorised the discharge`
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Divya, the court having authorised the discharge` EQUALS
            `a guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`

-- AND THE GUARDIAN THE COURT HAS REMOVED IS NOT ASKED AT ALL. Under the raw
-- traversal this file used to run, a removed mother with a s 17 order would
-- have answered that somebody could give a good discharge — naming a person
-- the Guardianship Act says is not a guardian. She is now filtered out before
-- s 17 is reached, and the answer is that the Act counts nobody.
`Meera, removed by the court, the court having authorised a discharge` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Meera Sivakumar` "divya"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant` `alone` TRUE FALSE TRUE
`Divya, her mother removed but authorised to discharge` MEANS
    `Divya, an infant concerned in the estate, the mother holding` `Meera, removed by the court, the court having authorised a discharge`
#ASSERT NOT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `Meera, removed by the court, the court having authorised a discharge`
#ASSERT `the guardians the Guardianship of Infants Act counts, for whom the case file holds a record` `Divya, her mother removed but authorised to discharge` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Divya, her mother removed but authorised to discharge` EQUALS
            `no guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`

-- THE JOIN THIS FILE WAS BUILT FOR, AS ONE EXPRESSION AND NOT AS TWO
-- ASSERTIONS A READER PUTS TOGETHER. `the capital moneys of this infant that
-- no guardian may give a good discharge for` takes the Intestate Succession
-- Act's figure and the Guardianship Act's verdict into one number. On these
-- facts it is a quarter of a million dollars that nobody can receipt for.
--
-- IT WOULD FAIL IF EITHER MODULE MOVED, which is the whole point of writing it
-- this way: change the fraction rule 3 gives a child and the figure changes;
-- let the court authorise the discharge and it falls to nil. The three
-- assertions below are the same family under both Acts at once.
`the Sivakumar intestacy, the court having authorised the discharge` MEANS
    `the Sivakumar intestacy, with` `Meera, the court having authorised the discharge`
        (LIST `the grant to the widow and the son`)
#ASSERT `the capital moneys of this infant that no guardian may give a good discharge for` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS 250_000
#ASSERT `the capital moneys of this infant that no guardian may give a good discharge for` `the Sivakumar intestacy, the court having authorised the discharge` `Divya, the court having authorised the discharge` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the capital moneys of this infant that no guardian may give a good discharge for` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `Divya, her mother removed but authorised to discharge` EQUALS 250_000

-- AND WHAT THE MONEY JOIN STILL DOES NOT DO. It is an expression in this
-- file, not a field in the ontology: `family-domain.l4` declares `Entitlement`
-- and no record has a field of that type, so nothing a consumer builds carries
-- the share from one module to the other. What has changed since the previous
-- draft is that the join is now COMPUTED rather than verified by a reader
-- comparing two assertions — see `Hand-offs that could not be made` G4(a).
--
-- THE TWO ANSWERS, SIDE BY SIDE. The first restates an assertion made above
-- and is kept because the pair IS the answer this family came for: what she is
-- entitled to, and whether anyone can hand it over.
#ASSERT `the shares taken by an infant concerned in the estate, read out` `the Sivakumar intestacy` EQUALS
            LIST "Divya Sivakumar takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS
            `no guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`


§ `Scenario 2 — the will that fails, and what takes over`

-- TWO WAYS A WILL STOPS OPERATING, and the Wills Act treats them quite
-- differently. One is a defect in its EXECUTION (s 6), which means it was
-- never a valid will; the other is REVOCATION by a later marriage (s 13),
-- which unmakes a will that was validly made. `wills-act.l4` answers the two
-- with different decisions, and its aggregate verdict
-- `the formal validity of the will` answers only the first — a revoked will
-- is still formally valid, and the reader who stops at the aggregate is told
-- the wrong thing about the family's position.
--
-- AND THE THING THE COMPOSITION FINDS, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE SCENARIO:
-- NEITHER ANSWER TRAVELS. `intestate-succession-act.l4` and
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` both read `Deceased person`'s `the will`
-- field and neither of them asks the Wills Act anything. A will that the
-- Wills Act has just declared not valid still counts, in the other two
-- modules, as a will that beneficially disposes of the whole estate — so the
-- Intestate Succession Act reports that its own rules have no effect, and the
-- Probate Act reports that the deceased did not die intestate. Both are
-- wrong, and both are wrong for the same reason: no field in
-- `family-domain.l4` carries a Wills Act verdict.
--
-- WHAT THIS FILE DOES ABOUT IT. It asserts the answers on BOTH case files —
-- with the will in the record, and with the will struck out — and it now also
-- CHECKS the file against the Wills Act's verdict, in one expression, at
-- `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` and
-- `the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Intestate Succession Act
-- still reads one`.
--
-- THE LINE BETWEEN A CHECK AND A RULE, AND WHY THE CHECK IS ON THE RIGHT SIDE
-- OF IT. A decision here that STRUCK THE WILL OUT on the Wills Act's say-so
-- would be putting a rule in the composition that belongs in the ontology, and
-- it would be believed. That is still not done and must not be. A decision
-- that READS the verdict and the field and reports that they disagree changes
-- nothing and decides nothing; it is the same shape as
-- `every guardian recorded for this infant is recorded against this infant`,
-- which `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` writes as a check and names as one.
-- An earlier draft of this file treated the two as one prohibition and so
-- declined the check as well as the rule, which left three findings in prose
-- that a machine can hold.

§§ `Scenario 2A — a will bad for want of execution (Wills Act 1838 s 6)`

`Anthony Alphonso` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Anthony Alphonso" "anthony" (JUST (YMD 1955 3 12))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1955 3 12)) `living` EMPTY

`Marianne Alphonso` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Marianne Alphonso" "marianne" (JUST (YMD 1985 8 1))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1985 8 1)) `living` EMPTY

`Josephine Alphonso` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Josephine Alphonso" "josephine" (JUST (YMD 1988 2 14))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1988 2 14)) `living` EMPTY

`Chiam Lai Fong` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Chiam Lai Fong" "laifong" (JUST (YMD 1958 1 30))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1958 1 30)) (`died on` (YMD 2024 9 4))
        (LIST `a legitimate child in the case file` `Marianne Alphonso`
            , `a legitimate child in the case file` `Josephine Alphonso`)

-- THE DEFECT IS s 6(2)'s THIRD LIMB AND NOTHING ELSE. The will is in writing;
-- the testatrix signed it at the foot; two witnesses subscribed in her
-- presence. What never happened is the middle limb — "the signature shall be
-- made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil
-- in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time". She signed
-- at home and took the paper to the witnesses one at a time.
`the will of Chiam Lai Fong, the signature never acknowledged before the witnesses` MEANS
    `a will, in full` `Chiam Lai Fong` (JUST (YMD 2015 5 20)) TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
        (LIST `an attesting witness` `Marianne Alphonso` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
            , `an attesting witness` `Josephine Alphonso` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
        (LIST `a gift` `Marianne Alphonso` `a residuary gift` 800_000 TRUE TRUE)
        EMPTY FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE TRUE
        (LIST `an executor appointed by the will` `Anthony Alphonso`)

`the family of Chiam Lai Fong` MEANS
    `a family in the case file`
        (LIST `a marriage in the case file` `Anthony Alphonso` (JUST (YMD 1983 12 3)) TRUE)
        NOTHING NOTHING

`the estate of Chiam Lai Fong, the will counted` MEANS `an estate in the case file` 800_000 0 0 0 800_000
`the estate of Chiam Lai Fong, the will disregarded` MEANS `an estate in the case file` 800_000 0 0 0 0

-- THE SAME FAMILY, TWICE. The first file is what the registry holds before
-- anybody has applied the Wills Act to the paper; the second is what it holds
-- after the will has been rejected. Everything else about them is identical.
`the Chiam estate, the will in the record` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Chiam Lai Fong` `the family of Chiam Lai Fong`
        `the estate of Chiam Lai Fong, the will counted`
        (JUST `the will of Chiam Lai Fong, the signature never acknowledged before the witnesses`)
        FALSE EMPTY EMPTY

`the Chiam estate, the will struck out` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Chiam Lai Fong` `the family of Chiam Lai Fong`
        `the estate of Chiam Lai Fong, the will disregarded` NOTHING FALSE EMPTY EMPTY

`the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act` MEANS
    `the Wills Act case of` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record`
        (`the plain facts about` `the will of Chiam Lai Fong, the signature never acknowledged before the witnesses`)

-- THE WILLS ACT'S ANSWER. s 6(1) — "No will shall be valid unless it is in
-- writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)." The
-- prohibition-with-an-exception is encoded that way round, so the propounder
-- fails on the limb he cannot make out.
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` (`the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act`'s `the will in question`)
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` (`the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act`'s `the will in question`)
#ASSERT NOT `the will is properly executed` `the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT NOT `s 4 — will of infant invalid` (`the will under consideration` `the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act`)
#ASSERT     `s 4 — the testator is proved to have been 21 years of age or over` (`the will under consideration` `the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act`)
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5`

-- THE OTHER TWO ACTS, ON THE FILE THAT STILL HOLDS THE WILL. Both answer as
-- though the will were good, because neither of them asks.
#ASSERT NOT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record`
#ASSERT `the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record` EQUALS
            `the Intestate Succession Act 1967 applies to the estate but has no effect on these facts`
#ASSERT NOT `s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record`
#ASSERT `s 8(1) — probate may be granted to this person as an executor appointed by a will` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record` "anthony"
#ASSERT NOT `s 13(1)(a) — no executor is appointed by a will` `the will of Chiam Lai Fong, the signature never acknowledged before the witnesses`

-- AND THE SAME TWO ACTS ONCE THE WILL IS OUT. s 7 rule 2 gives the husband
-- one-half and rule 3 divides the other half between the two daughters; s 18
-- opens letters of administration instead of probate.
#ASSERT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out`
#ASSERT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out` EQUALS
            LIST "Anthony Alphonso takes 0.5", "Marianne Alphonso takes 0.25", "Josephine Alphonso takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out` EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out`
#ASSERT NOT `s 8(1) — probate may be granted to this person as an executor appointed by a will` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out` "anthony"
#ASSERT `the persons entitled under section 18(4)(a)` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out` EQUALS
            LIST `Anthony Alphonso`, `Marianne Alphonso`, `Josephine Alphonso`

-- THE GAP, STATED AS A MEASUREMENT AND NOT AS AN OPINION, AND THE MEASUREMENT
-- CORRECTED. The two case files above differ in TWO fields, not one, and the
-- second is load-bearing: `Deceased person`'s `the will` goes from
-- JUST the will to NOTHING, and `Estate`'s
-- `the value of the property beneficially disposed of by the will` goes from
-- 800_000 to 0. Without the second change, Intestate Succession Act s 10 would
-- net the fund to nothing and `the distribution, read out` on the struck-out
-- file would be a distribution of nothing at all. The 2B pair does the same,
-- 600_000 against 0.
--
-- THAT STRENGTHENS THE FINDING RATHER THAN WEAKENING IT. Nothing anywhere in
-- the four modules computes either field from the Wills Act's verdict, so
-- whoever builds a case file must keep TWO derived facts in step with a
-- conclusion nothing computes — and get both right, or get an answer that is
-- wrong in a way no assertion in the four Act modules can see. (An earlier
-- draft of this comment, and of G1, said "exactly one field"; the second was
-- found by an adversarial review of this file on 2026-08-26.)

-- AND THE CHECK, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE MACHINE-READABLE.
-- The Wills Act has held this will bad for want of execution. The file that
-- still holds it is in the third state below; the file that has struck it out
-- is in the second, which is the state a file-builder who applied the Wills
-- Act first would have produced.
#ASSERT `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record` `the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the Wills Act leaves no operative will, and the case file still holds it, so the other three Acts answer as though it stood`
#ASSERT `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out` `the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the Wills Act leaves no operative will, and the case file has struck it out`

-- AND THE COST OF THE THIRD STATE, ASSERTED RATHER THAN NARRATED. One
-- expression, two Acts: the Wills Act says there is no operative will and the
-- Intestate Succession Act says the deceased is not an "intestate" within
-- its s 3. It fails on the file that has been put right, which is what makes
-- it a test and not a label.
#ASSERT     `the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Intestate Succession Act still reads one` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record` `the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT NOT `the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Intestate Succession Act still reads one` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out` `the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT     `the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Probate Act still routes to probate` `the Chiam estate, the will in the record` `the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT NOT `the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Probate Act still routes to probate` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out` `the Chiam will, put to the Wills Act`

§§ `Scenario 2B — a will revoked by marriage (Wills Act 1838 s 13)`

-- s 13(1) — "Every will made by a man or woman shall be revoked by his or her
-- marriage, except a will made in exercise of a power of appointment, when the
-- real or personal estate thereby appointed would not in default of such
-- appointment pass to his or her heir, executor or administrator or the person
-- entitled under the Intestate Succession Act 1967."
--
-- Lucas Pereira made a will in 2012 leaving everything to his daughter by his
-- first marriage, married Carmen in 2018, and died in 2025 without making
-- another. The will is impeccably executed and it is gone.

`Carmen Pereira` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Carmen Pereira" "carmen" (JUST (YMD 1975 9 9))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1975 9 9)) `living` EMPTY

`Vanessa Pereira` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Vanessa Pereira" "vanessa" (JUST (YMD 1998 4 17))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1998 4 17)) `living` EMPTY

`Lucas Pereira` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Lucas Pereira" "lucas" (JUST (YMD 1970 6 15))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1970 6 15)) (`died on` (YMD 2025 2 20))
        (LIST `a legitimate child in the case file` `Vanessa Pereira`)

`the will of Lucas Pereira, made in 2012` MEANS
    `a will, in full` `Lucas Pereira` (JUST (YMD 2012 3 8)) TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE
        (LIST `an attesting witness` `Marianne Alphonso` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
            , `an attesting witness` `Josephine Alphonso` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
        (LIST `a gift` `Vanessa Pereira` `a residuary gift` 600_000 TRUE TRUE)
        EMPTY FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE TRUE EMPTY

`the family of Lucas Pereira, having married Carmen in 2018` MEANS
    `a family in the case file`
        (LIST `a marriage in the case file` `Carmen Pereira` (JUST (YMD 2018 11 11)) TRUE)
        NOTHING NOTHING

`the family of Lucas Pereira, having married Carmen in 2009` MEANS
    `a family in the case file`
        (LIST `a marriage in the case file` `Carmen Pereira` (JUST (YMD 2009 1 5)) TRUE)
        NOTHING NOTHING

`the estate of Lucas Pereira, the will counted` MEANS `an estate in the case file` 600_000 0 0 0 600_000
`the estate of Lucas Pereira, the will disregarded` MEANS `an estate in the case file` 600_000 0 0 0 0

`the Pereira estate, the will in the record` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Lucas Pereira` `the family of Lucas Pereira, having married Carmen in 2018`
        `the estate of Lucas Pereira, the will counted` (JUST `the will of Lucas Pereira, made in 2012`)
        FALSE EMPTY EMPTY

`the Pereira estate, the will struck out` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Lucas Pereira` `the family of Lucas Pereira, having married Carmen in 2018`
        `the estate of Lucas Pereira, the will disregarded` NOTHING FALSE EMPTY EMPTY

`the Pereira estate, the marriage having preceded the will` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Lucas Pereira` `the family of Lucas Pereira, having married Carmen in 2009`
        `the estate of Lucas Pereira, the will counted` (JUST `the will of Lucas Pereira, made in 2012`)
        FALSE EMPTY EMPTY

`the Pereira will, put to the Wills Act` MEANS
    `the Wills Act case of` `the Pereira estate, the will in the record`
        (`the plain facts about` `the will of Lucas Pereira, made in 2012`)

`the Pereira will, the marriage having preceded it, put to the Wills Act` MEANS
    `the Wills Act case of` `the Pereira estate, the marriage having preceded the will`
        (`the plain facts about` `the will of Lucas Pereira, made in 2012`)

-- THE WILL IS FORMALLY VALID AND IT IS REVOKED, and both halves have to be
-- read. A consumer that asked `wills-act.l4` only for its aggregate verdict
-- would be told `valid` about a will that has no operation at all.
#ASSERT `the will is properly executed` `the Pereira will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the Pereira will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS `valid`
#ASSERT `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the Pereira will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the Pereira will, put to the Wills Act`

-- THE ORDER OF THE TWO EVENTS IS WHAT DOES IT, and it is a gloss and not the
-- subsection's own words — `wills-act.l4` says so at
-- `the marriage was solemnised after the will was made`. The minimal pair:
-- the identical will and the identical marriage, solemnised three years
-- BEFORE the will instead of six years after.
#ASSERT NOT `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the Pereira will, the marriage having preceded it, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT NOT `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the Pereira will, the marriage having preceded it, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the Pereira will, the marriage having preceded it, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS `valid`

-- AND THE SAME SILENCE AS IN 2A, from a different direction. The Wills Act
-- has revoked the will; the other two modules still read it out of the record
-- and answer as though it stood.
#ASSERT NOT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the Pereira estate, the will in the record`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Pereira estate, the will in the record`
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `the Pereira estate, the will in the record` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT NOT `s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` `the Pereira estate, the will in the record`

-- ONCE THE REVOCATION IS GIVEN EFFECT IN THE FILE, the answer is the one the
-- family actually gets: rule 2 halves the estate between the widow and the
-- daughter the will was written for, and the Probate Act routes to letters of
-- administration.
#ASSERT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the Pereira estate, the will struck out`
#ASSERT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Pereira estate, the will struck out`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Pereira estate, the will struck out` EQUALS
            LIST "Carmen Pereira takes 0.5", "Vanessa Pereira takes 0.5"
#ASSERT `s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` `the Pereira estate, the will struck out`
#ASSERT `s 18(4) — the classes to which letters of administration may be granted` `the Pereira estate, the will struck out` EQUALS
            LIST `the husband or widow or next of kin or any of them`, `such person as the court thinks fit`

-- AND THE CHECK, ON A REVOCATION RATHER THAN AN EXECUTION DEFECT. This is the
-- half of the Wills Act's answer that its aggregate verdict does not carry:
-- `the formal validity of the will` says `valid` and s 15 says the will is
-- revoked, so `the Wills Act leaves this will operative` — which reads both —
-- is FALSE while the aggregate alone would have said the will was good. A
-- consumer that checked only the aggregate against the file would find them in
-- agreement and be wrong.
#ASSERT NOT `the Wills Act leaves this will operative` `the Pereira will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT     `the Wills Act leaves this will operative` `the Pereira will, the marriage having preceded it, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` `the Pereira estate, the will in the record` `the Pereira will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the Wills Act leaves no operative will, and the case file still holds it, so the other three Acts answer as though it stood`
#ASSERT `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` `the Pereira estate, the will struck out` `the Pereira will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the Wills Act leaves no operative will, and the case file has struck it out`
#ASSERT `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` `the Pereira estate, the marriage having preceded the will` `the Pereira will, the marriage having preceded it, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the Wills Act leaves this will operative, and the case file holds it`
#ASSERT     `the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Intestate Succession Act still reads one` `the Pereira estate, the will in the record` `the Pereira will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT NOT `the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Intestate Succession Act still reads one` `the Pereira estate, the will struck out` `the Pereira will, put to the Wills Act`

-- AND THE FOURTH STATE, WHICH IS THE MIRROR OF THE OTHER TWO AND IS JUST AS
-- BUILDABLE: a file that has struck out a will the Wills Act leaves standing.
-- Here the Pereira family with the earlier marriage — the will is good and is
-- not revoked — put beside the case file that dropped it. The Intestate
-- Succession Act then distributes an estate the will disposes of.
#ASSERT `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` `the Pereira estate, the will struck out` `the Pereira will, the marriage having preceded it, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the Wills Act leaves this will operative, and the case file has struck it out, so the other three Acts answer as though there were none`

-- THE CROSS-REFERENCE THAT IS IN THE WILLS ACT'S OWN TEXT, and the only one
-- there is. s 13(1)'s exception names "the person entitled under the Intestate
-- Succession Act 1967" — but it asks whether the appointed estate would pass
-- to that person "in default of such appointment", which is a question about a
-- counterfactual instrument and not about this estate. `family-domain.l4`
-- records it as a proved fact for that reason, so even the one place where one
-- of these Acts cites another by name is not a computation across the join.
#ASSERT NOT (`the will of Lucas Pereira, made in 2012`'s `made in exercise of a power of appointment`)


§ `Scenario 3 — the privileged will that appoints a guardian`

-- s 27(1) — "Notwithstanding anything in this Act, any soldier being in actual
-- military service, or any mariner or seaman being at sea, may dispose of his
-- personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act and may
-- do so even though under 21 years of age."
-- s 27(3) — "Where any person dies after 29 August 1938 having made a will
-- which is, or which, if it had been a disposition of property, would have
-- been rendered valid by this section, any appointment contained in that will
-- of any person as guardian of the infant children of the testator shall be of
-- full force and effect."
--
-- Danial Iskandar was nineteen, a serving soldier, and had a year-old son. He
-- wrote out a will on operations, unwitnessed, leaving what he had to the boy
-- and asking his sister Zarina to look after him. He was killed on 10 June
-- 2025.
--
-- THIS IS THE ONE JOIN IN THE CORPUS THAT ONE SIDE STATES IN TERMS.
-- `wills-act.l4` marks its s 27(3) rule "***** A JOIN TO THE GUARDIANSHIP OF
-- INFANTS ACT 1934. *****" and writes the hand-off contract out: "So what
-- guardianship-of-infants-act.l4 must read from here is: which of the
-- appointments the will contains are in the list below."
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` never picks
-- it up — its s 7(1)-(2) treats `appointed by the father by will` exactly as
-- it treats `appointed by the father by deed` and imposes no condition on the
-- instrument at all. The hand-off is TYPED on both sides and taken on
-- neither: `wills-act.l4` can and does check its own two halves against each
-- other, and nothing anywhere can put the Wills Act's answer beside the
-- Guardianship Act's, because no Act module imports another. That is what
-- step 3 below does, and what it finds.

§§ `Scenario 3 — the soldier, his son, and the two appointments`

`Adam Iskandar` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Adam Iskandar" "adam" (JUST (YMD 2024 2 20))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 2024 2 20)) `living` EMPTY

