§§ `Scenario 3, step 1 — the Wills Act saves the will and the appointment`

-- s 4 WOULD HAVE VOIDED IT. "No will made by any person under 21 years of age
-- shall be valid." He was nineteen when he made it, and the decision says so.
#ASSERT `the age of the testator when the will was made` (`the will under consideration` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act`) EQUALS `under 21 years of age`
#ASSERT `s 4 — will of infant invalid` (`the will under consideration` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act`)

-- AND s 6 WOULD HAVE VOIDED IT TOO: nobody witnessed it.
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` (`the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act`'s `the will in question`)
#ASSERT NOT `the will is properly executed` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act`

-- s 27(1) OPENS "Notwithstanding anything in this Act", and the aggregate
-- tests it FIRST for that reason. Two provisions that would each defeat the
-- will are both displaced by one subsection.
#ASSERT `s 27 — this section applies to the person` `Danial Iskandar`
#ASSERT `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT `s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `valid, being the will of a soldier being in actual military service, or of a mariner or seaman being at sea, disposing of his personal estate (section 27(1))`

-- s 27(3) — the appointment of Zarina is "of full force and effect". The
-- decision returns the appointments themselves, and the boolean is their
-- non-emptiness.
#ASSERT `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            LIST "zarina"

-- THE WILLS ACT'S TWO HALVES, CHECKED AGAINST EACH OTHER — AND THIS IS A
-- FILE-INTEGRITY CROSS-CHECK, NOT A HAND-OFF, said plainly because an earlier
-- draft of this file listed it among the hand-offs it had MADE. Both decisions
-- below are inside `wills-act.l4`, which labels them "NEITHER OF THE TWO
-- DECISIONS BELOW IS A RULE OF THE WILLS ACT", and both read data this file
-- typed: the same `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will`
-- value appears in the will's appointment list and in the estate's infants
-- list. The equality therefore checks that the author wrote one name twice —
-- worth checking, and not evidence that two Acts compose. The Wills Act's
-- answer is put to the GUARDIANSHIP Act in step 3, where it is a composition.
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians recorded against the infants concerned in the estate` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            LIST "zarina"
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the references for the guardians recorded against the infants concerned in the estate` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act`

§§ `Scenario 3, step 2 — what the Guardianship of Infants Act does with the appointment`

`Adam, his mother surviving` MEANS
    `Adam, an infant concerned in his father’s estate` (JUST `Aisyah Kamarudin`)
        (LIST `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will`)

`Adam, both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian` MEANS
    `Adam, an infant concerned in his father’s estate` (JUST `Aisyah Kamarudin, having died after the father`)
        (LIST `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will`
            , `Nadhirah, appointed guardian of Adam by his mother’s will`)

-- s 7(1) — "The father of an infant may by deed or will appoint any person to
-- be guardian of the infant after his death." The appointment takes effect on
-- the father's death, which has happened.
#ASSERT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will` `Adam, his mother surviving`

-- s 7(3) — "Any guardian so appointed shall act jointly with the mother or
-- father, as the case may be, of the infant so long as the mother or father
-- remains alive, unless the mother or father objects to his so acting."
-- Aisyah is alive and has not objected, so Zarina acts WITH her and not
-- instead of her. The default is joint action and the objection is the
-- exception, which is the way round the subsection puts it.
#ASSERT `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father under section 7(3)` `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will` `Adam, his mother surviving`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will` `Adam, his mother surviving` EQUALS
            `he acts jointly with the mother or father, as the case may be`

-- WHO THE GUARDIANS ARE, all together: the mother by force of s 6(1), and
-- Zarina by the appointment the Wills Act made good.
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` `Adam, his mother surviving`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Adam, his mother surviving` EQUALS LIST "aisyah", "zarina"
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Adam, his mother surviving` (YMD 2026 1 15) EQUALS
            `the person is an infant on that date, and the guardians of the infant are` (LIST "aisyah", "zarina")

-- s 7(5) — "Where guardians are appointed by both parents, the guardians so
-- appointed shall after the death of the surviving parent act jointly." Aisyah
-- dies on 1 December 2025, six months after Danial, having appointed Nadhirah
-- by her own will. Now both appointees act together.
#ASSERT NOT `guardians are appointed by both parents` `Adam, his mother surviving`
#ASSERT NOT `section 7(5) is engaged` `Adam, his mother surviving`
#ASSERT `the guardians who act jointly under section 7(5)` `Adam, his mother surviving` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `guardians are appointed by both parents` `Adam, both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian`
#ASSERT `the infant has no parent` `Adam, both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian`
#ASSERT `section 7(5) is engaged` `Adam, both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian`
#ASSERT `the guardians who act jointly under section 7(5)` `Adam, both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian` EQUALS
            LIST "zarina", "nadhirah"
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will` `Adam, both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian` EQUALS
            `after the death of the surviving parent the guardians appointed by both parents act jointly`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Adam, both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian` EQUALS
            LIST "zarina", "nadhirah"

§§ `Scenario 3, step 3 — the Wills Act's verdict, put to the Guardianship Act`

-- THE MEASUREMENT. Take the identical family and change one fact about the
-- testator: Danial was not a soldier. The will, its date, its want of
-- witnesses, the appointment of Zarina, the boy and his mother are all
-- unchanged.
--
-- ONE FACT, AND WHERE IT HAD TO BE CHANGED TWICE. `a soldier being in actual
-- military service` sits on a `Person`, and Danial appears in this file in two
-- places: as the deceased, and as `the father of the infant` on Adam's own
-- record. An earlier draft changed the first and reused `Adam, his mother
-- surviving` — which is built from the SOLDIER value — for the second, so the
-- civilian case file said in one place that he was a soldier and in another
-- that he was not. No decision asserted here reads that field off the father,
-- so nothing failed; it was still a case file contradicting itself, and it made
-- the "identical `Infant` value" claim below true by construction rather than
-- by measurement. The civilian file now carries a civilian father throughout,
-- and BOTH `Infant` records are put to the Guardianship Act below.
--
-- THE WILLS ACT'S ANSWER FLIPS. Without s 27 the will is void under s 4, and
-- s 27(3) makes no appointment good: `the references for the guardians whose
-- appointment this Act has made good` goes from LIST "zarina" to EMPTY.
--
-- THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT'S ANSWER DOES NOT MOVE. It still makes Zarina a
-- guardian of Adam acting jointly with his mother, because its s 7(1)-(2)
-- reads `How the guardian was appointed`'s `appointed by the father by will`
-- and asks nothing about the will. Nothing in the shared ontology carries a
-- Wills Act verdict, so there is no field it COULD read.
--
-- AND THAT DISAGREEMENT IS NOW AN ASSERTION AND NOT A PARAGRAPH.
-- `wills-act.l4` invites the bridge in terms, above its own s 27(3) rule: "the
-- hand-off is a VALUE — the list-valued decision below — that a module seeing
-- both Acts can call directly, per appointment." This file is a module seeing
-- both Acts. `every testamentary guardian the Guardianship of Infants Act
-- counts holds under an appointment the Wills Act has made good` takes the
-- Wills Act's list as the input to a test over the Guardianship Act's counted
-- guardians, and it is TRUE on the soldier's file and FALSE on the civilian's.
--
-- IT IS A CHECK ON A CASE FILE AND NOT A RULE OF EITHER ACT.
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` says at its s 7(1)-(2) that
-- `appointed by the father by will` is "to be set only where the Wills Act
-- decision has answered TRUE" — a discipline on whoever writes the case file.
-- What is new is that breaking the discipline is now visible.

`Danial Iskandar, had he not been a soldier` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Danial Iskandar" "danial" (JUST (YMD 2005 10 3))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 2005 10 3)) (`died on` (YMD 2025 6 10))
        (LIST `a legitimate child in the case file` `Adam Iskandar`)

`the will of Danial Iskandar, had he not been a soldier` MEANS
    `a will, in full` `Danial Iskandar, had he not been a soldier` (JUST (YMD 2025 1 15)) TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
        EMPTY (LIST `a gift` `Adam Iskandar` `a residuary gift` 40_000 TRUE TRUE)
        (LIST `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will`)
        FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE TRUE EMPTY

`Adam, his mother surviving, his father not having been a soldier` MEANS
    `an infant in the case file` `Adam Iskandar` (JUST `Danial Iskandar, had he not been a soldier`)
        (JUST `Aisyah Kamarudin`) (LIST `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will`) 40_000

`the Iskandar estate, he not having been a soldier` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Danial Iskandar, had he not been a soldier` `the family of Danial Iskandar`
        `the estate of Danial Iskandar` (JUST `the will of Danial Iskandar, had he not been a soldier`)
        FALSE EMPTY (LIST `Adam, his mother surviving, his father not having been a soldier`)

`the Iskandar will, he not having been a soldier, put to the Wills Act` MEANS
    `the Wills Act case of` `the Iskandar estate, he not having been a soldier`
        (`the facts about a will` `the will of Danial Iskandar, had he not been a soldier`
             `no conformity to any law outside this Act` `not revoked in any of those ways` `no revival`
             FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE)

#ASSERT NOT `s 27 — this section applies to the person` `Danial Iskandar, had he not been a soldier`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the Iskandar will, he not having been a soldier, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the Iskandar will, he not having been a soldier, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `not valid, being a will made by a person under 21 years of age (section 4)`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the Iskandar will, he not having been a soldier, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good` `the Iskandar will, he not having been a soldier, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS EMPTY

-- AND THE ESTATE STILL RECORDS ZARINA AS ADAM'S GUARDIAN, so the two halves
-- of the join now DISAGREE on one file — the check `wills-act.l4` built, run
-- on a case where it fails.
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians recorded against the infants concerned in the estate` `the Iskandar will, he not having been a soldier, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            LIST "zarina"

-- THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT, ASKED THE SAME QUESTION ABOUT THE SAME BOY, IS
-- UNMOVED — and it is unmoved on BOTH `Infant` records, the one built from the
-- soldier father and the one built from the civilian father. The first three
-- are the assertions made in step 2; the second three are the same questions
-- put to the civilian file, which is the file the Wills Act has just emptied.
#ASSERT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will` `Adam, his mother surviving`
#ASSERT `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father under section 7(3)` `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will` `Adam, his mother surviving`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Adam, his mother surviving` EQUALS LIST "aisyah", "zarina"
#ASSERT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will` `Adam, his mother surviving, his father not having been a soldier`
#ASSERT `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father under section 7(3)` `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will` `Adam, his mother surviving, his father not having been a soldier`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Adam, his mother surviving, his father not having been a soldier` EQUALS LIST "aisyah", "zarina"

-- THE BRIDGE, RUN. ONE EXPRESSION, TWO ACTS: every guardian the Guardianship
-- Act counts who holds under a parent's WILL must be one whose appointment
-- Wills Act s 27(3) has made good. On the soldier's file the Wills Act's list
-- is LIST "zarina" and Zarina is in it; on the civilian's the list is EMPTY and
-- she is not, so the file records as a guardian a person no operative
-- instrument appointed.
--
-- IT FAILS BECAUSE THE TWO ACTS DISAGREE, which is the property no pair of
-- single-Act assertions above it could have. Change the Wills Act's s 27(3)
-- gate and the first assertion breaks; change the Guardianship Act's s 7(1)-(2)
-- route and the second does.
#ASSERT     `every testamentary guardian the Guardianship of Infants Act counts holds under an appointment the Wills Act has made good` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act` `Adam, his mother surviving`
#ASSERT NOT `every testamentary guardian the Guardianship of Infants Act counts holds under an appointment the Wills Act has made good` `the Iskandar will, he not having been a soldier, put to the Wills Act` `Adam, his mother surviving, his father not having been a soldier`

-- AND THE SAME BRIDGE ON THE OTHER FILE IN THIS SCENARIO, where both parents
-- are dead and each appointed a guardian by will. The Guardianship Act counts
-- Zarina and Nadhirah under s 7(5); the Wills Act has been shown only the
-- father's will, and it has made good only his appointment. So the check
-- reports what is true — the mother's appointment is good under HER will,
-- which is a document this corpus was never handed, and the file is asserting
-- something no Wills Act answer in it supports.
#ASSERT NOT `every testamentary guardian the Guardianship of Infants Act counts holds under an appointment the Wills Act has made good` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act` `Adam, both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian`

-- AND THE G1 CHECK ON THE SAME PAIR, which is the third of the three findings
-- this file used to make in prose. The soldier's file and the Wills Act agree;
-- the civilian's does not, and the corpus goes on treating a void will as one
-- that disposes of the whole estate.
#ASSERT `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` `the Iskandar estate` `the Iskandar will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the Wills Act leaves this will operative, and the case file holds it`
#ASSERT `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` `the Iskandar estate, he not having been a soldier` `the Iskandar will, he not having been a soldier, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the Wills Act leaves no operative will, and the case file still holds it, so the other three Acts answer as though it stood`
#ASSERT `the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Intestate Succession Act still reads one` `the Iskandar estate, he not having been a soldier` `the Iskandar will, he not having been a soldier, put to the Wills Act`

§§ `Scenario 3, step 4 — the guardian the Act names and the file cannot describe`

-- G9 MET IN A FAMILY RATHER THAN IN A COMMENT. Adam has two guardians: Zarina,
-- whom his father appointed by will and for whom the file holds a `Guardian`
-- record, and his mother Aisyah, whom s 6(1) makes guardian BY FORCE OF THE
-- SECTION and for whom it holds none. `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` says so
-- at its own s 6 block: a parent is guardian by force of the section and needs
-- no record to be one.
--
-- SO SECTION 17 CANNOT BE ASKED ABOUT AISYAH AT ALL. Every fact it reads lives
-- on a `Guardian` record — `of the person or of the property`, and whether the
-- court has otherwise ordered — and there is no record to read them off. This
-- is not a defect in the case file: it is s 6(1) saying "be guardian of the
-- infant" unqualified while s 17 speaks only of "A guardian of the property of
-- an infant", which is G9, and G9 is a fact about the Act.
--
-- WHAT AN EARLIER DRAFT DID INSTEAD, and why it was worse than saying nothing.
-- It ran `any` over the recorded guardians, so on this family it answered
-- about Zarina and said nothing about the woman the Act names — and on a
-- family where the only recorded guardian had been removed by the court under
-- s 10, it answered TRUE, reporting that somebody could give a good discharge
-- and naming a person the Act says is not a guardian at all.
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Adam, his mother surviving` EQUALS LIST "aisyah", "zarina"
#ASSERT `the guardians the Guardianship of Infants Act counts, for whom the case file holds no record` `Adam, his mother surviving` EQUALS LIST "aisyah"
#ASSERT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `Zarina, appointed guardian of Adam by his father’s will`
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Adam, his mother surviving` EQUALS
            `the Guardianship of Infants Act counts as guardian a person for whom the case file records no kind of guardianship, so section 17 cannot be asked about him`

-- AND WHEN THE MOTHER DIES, THE FILE CAN DESCRIBE EVERY GUARDIAN IT HAS. Both
-- parents are dead and each appointed a guardian by will; s 7(5) makes the two
-- appointees act jointly, both have records, and s 17 can be asked of both. The
-- answer becomes a plain no.
#ASSERT `the guardians the Guardianship of Infants Act counts, for whom the case file holds no record` `Adam, both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Adam, both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian` EQUALS
            `no guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`

-- AND THE SECOND WAY THE RAW TRAVERSAL OVER-COUNTED, which is worth its own
-- fixture because it has nothing to do with removal. s 7(1) — "The father of an
-- infant may by deed or will appoint any person to be guardian of the infant
-- AFTER HIS DEATH." A father may make the appointment in his lifetime, and the
-- appointee is not a guardian of anything until he dies. Here Danial lives, the
-- will appointing Zarina is already written, and the court has made an order
-- under s 17 authorising her to give a discharge.
--
-- THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT COUNTS NOBODY: the appointment has not taken effect, and
-- neither parent is guardian under s 6 because s 6(1) needs the father dead and
-- s 6(2) needs the mother dead. So no guardian exists to be asked, and the
-- answer is that no guardian the Act counts can give a good discharge — even
-- though the one recorded person has an order that would let her. An earlier
-- draft ran `any` over the recorded list, found her order, and answered that
-- somebody could hand the money over.
`Danial Iskandar, in the counterfactual in which he lived` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Danial Iskandar" "danial" (JUST (YMD 2005 10 3))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 2005 10 3)) `living`
        (LIST `a legitimate child in the case file` `Adam Iskandar`)

`Zarina, appointed by a living father, the court having authorised a discharge` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Zarina Iskandar` "adam" `appointed by the father by will`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant` `alone` FALSE FALSE TRUE

`Adam, his father living and the appointment by will already made` MEANS
    `an infant in the case file` `Adam Iskandar`
        (JUST `Danial Iskandar, in the counterfactual in which he lived`) (JUST `Aisyah Kamarudin`)
        (LIST `Zarina, appointed by a living father, the court having authorised a discharge`) 40_000

#ASSERT NOT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Zarina, appointed by a living father, the court having authorised a discharge` `Adam, his father living and the appointment by will already made`
#ASSERT NOT `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` `Adam, his father living and the appointment by will already made`
#ASSERT NOT `the father is guardian of the infant under section 6(2)` `Adam, his father living and the appointment by will already made`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Adam, his father living and the appointment by will already made` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT NOT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `Zarina, appointed by a living father, the court having authorised a discharge`
#ASSERT `the guardians the Guardianship of Infants Act counts, for whom the case file holds a record` `Adam, his father living and the appointment by will already made` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Adam, his father living and the appointment by will already made` EQUALS
            `no guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`


§ `Scenario 4 — the Muslim estate: where the corpus must stop`

-- s 2 — "Nothing in this Act shall apply to the estate of any Muslim or shall
-- affect any rules of the Muslim law in respect of the distribution of the
-- estate of any such person."
--
-- Muhammad Faizal died on 2 August 2025 leaving a widow, an adult son and a
-- daughter of fourteen, and $900,000 in Singapore. The Intestate Succession Act
-- does not reach a line of it.
--
-- WHERE IT GOES INSTEAD, and the Act that says so is not one of the four. The
-- Administration of Muslim Law Act 1966 s 112(1): "In the case of any Muslim
-- person domiciled in Singapore dying intestate, the estate and effects must
-- be distributed according to the Muslim law as modified, where applicable, by
-- Malay custom." (Quoted from source/AMLA1966.txt, which is in this
-- directory and is NOT encoded anywhere in this cleanroom.) So the honest
-- answer for this family is a pointer out of the corpus, and the composition's
-- job is to give the pointer rather than an arithmetic.
--
-- WHY A TOOL THAT ANSWERS HERE IS WORSE THAN ONE THAT DECLINES. Three of the
-- four modules keep running on this estate, and they are RIGHT to: the Probate
-- and Administration Act still governs the grant (its own s 18(5) merely saves
-- the other law, and AMLA s 113 assumes that Muslims apply for probate and
-- letters of administration), and the Guardianship of Infants Act still
-- appoints the daughter's guardian. Only the DISTRIBUTION is switched off.
-- A consumer that put the four modules side by side and read whichever one
-- answered would report a distribution the Act expressly does not make.
-- `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` exists so
-- that the stop is a value and not a footnote.
--
-- AND IT IS OFFERED, NOT ENFORCED — which an earlier draft of this comment
-- overstated by saying the stop was one "the consumer has to look at". Nothing
-- compels a consumer to look at it: it is a decision in this file, with no
-- `@export`, and a consumer that imports the four Act modules never sees it.
-- What that consumer does have is `intestate-succession-act.l4`'s own
-- `this Act applies to the estate`, which already answers FALSE here — so the
-- stop exists in the corpus and the pointer out of it does not. Making the
-- stop unavoidable is a change to that module's public surface or to the
-- shared ontology, and it is proposed here rather than made.

§§ `Scenario 4 — the family, and the four answers`

`Rohani Sulaiman` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Rohani Sulaiman" "rohani" (JUST (YMD 1980 4 4))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1980 4 4)) `living` EMPTY

`Hafiz Faizal` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Hafiz Faizal" "hafiz" (JUST (YMD 2001 3 3))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 2001 3 3)) `living` EMPTY

`Sofea Faizal` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Sofea Faizal" "sofea" (JUST (YMD 2010 12 12))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 2010 12 12)) `living` EMPTY

`Muhammad Faizal` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Muhammad Faizal" "faizal" (JUST (YMD 1978 1 1))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1978 1 1)) (`died on` (YMD 2025 8 2))
        (LIST `a legitimate child in the case file` `Hafiz Faizal`
            , `a legitimate child in the case file` `Sofea Faizal`)

`Rohani, guardian of Sofea by virtue of surviving the father` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Rohani Sulaiman` "sofea"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant` `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Sofea, an infant concerned in the estate` MEANS
    `an infant in the case file` `Sofea Faizal` (JUST `Muhammad Faizal`) (JUST `Rohani Sulaiman`)
        (LIST `Rohani, guardian of Sofea by virtue of surviving the father`) 0

`the family of Muhammad Faizal` MEANS
    `a family in the case file`
        (LIST `a marriage in the case file` `Rohani Sulaiman` (JUST (YMD 1999 8 8)) TRUE)
        NOTHING NOTHING

`the estate of Muhammad Faizal` MEANS `an estate in the case file` 900_000 0 0 0 0

`the Faizal estate` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Muhammad Faizal` `the family of Muhammad Faizal`
        `the estate of Muhammad Faizal` NOTHING TRUE EMPTY (LIST `Sofea, an infant concerned in the estate`)

-- THE SAME FAMILY, NOT MUSLIM. It exists so that every assertion below has a
-- minimal pair: one field differs, `Deceased person`'s `a Muslim`, and it is
-- the only thing that could be producing the difference.
`the Faizal estate, the deceased not being a Muslim` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Muhammad Faizal` `the family of Muhammad Faizal`
        `the estate of Muhammad Faizal` NOTHING FALSE EMPTY (LIST `Sofea, an infant concerned in the estate`)

-- THE INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT STOPS, AND STOPS COMPLETELY. Not "distributes
-- nothing" — has no application. In particular rule 9 does NOT fire: a
-- Muslim's estate does not escheat to the Government by way of an Act that
-- does not apply to it.
#ASSERT `section 2 — nothing in this Act applies to the estate` `the Faizal estate`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act applies to the estate` `the Faizal estate`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Faizal estate`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 9 — in default of distribution under rules 1 to 8` `the Faizal estate`
#ASSERT `rule 9 — the entitlement of the Government` `the Faizal estate` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `the Faizal estate` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Faizal estate` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `the Faizal estate` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `the Faizal estate` EQUALS 0

-- THE COMPOSITION SAYS SO IN WORDS, and this is the assertion a consumer of
-- this corpus should be made to read before it reports anything at all.
#ASSERT `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` `the Faizal estate` EQUALS
            `nothing in the Intestate Succession Act 1967 applies to this estate, and no other Act in this corpus distributes it`

-- THE MINIMAL PAIR. Change nothing but s 2's fact and the same family takes
-- one-half and a quarter each under rules 2 and 3.
#ASSERT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Faizal estate, the deceased not being a Muslim`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Faizal estate, the deceased not being a Muslim` EQUALS
            LIST "Rohani Sulaiman takes 0.5", "Hafiz Faizal takes 0.25", "Sofea Faizal takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` `the Faizal estate, the deceased not being a Muslim` EQUALS
            `the Intestate Succession Act 1967 governs the distribution, and its rules have been applied`

-- THE OTHER THREE ACTS DO NOT STOP, AND SHOULD NOT. The Probate and
-- Administration Act's own saving is narrower than the Intestate Succession
-- Act's exclusion — s 18(5), "Nothing in this section shall affect any law by
-- which special provision is made regarding the estates of persons of a
-- particular religion or race", leaves the other law unaffected rather than
-- switching this one off — and `probate-administration-act.l4` says so in
-- terms. So the grant machinery runs on identical facts.
--
-- AND THE NEXT OF KIN ARE FOUND ON A MUSLIM ESTATE BY A CONSTRUCTION BORROWED
-- FROM THE ACT THAT DOES NOT APPLY TO IT, which is worth recording where it
-- happens rather than leaving to be noticed. AMBIGUITY A3 of
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` takes "next of kin" as "the classes to whom
-- the Intestate Succession Act 1967 distributes on an intestacy — issue,
-- parents, brothers and sisters and their issue, grandparents, uncles and
-- aunts — taken as a flat class", and its own note beside that reading says "A
-- module cannot borrow a class by the name of the Act that defines it and then
-- populate it against that Act." Here it does exactly that: the Intestate
-- Succession Act distributes to nobody on this estate — asserted four lines
-- above — and the same three people are nonetheless the persons entitled under
-- s 18(4)(a).
--
-- THAT IS RIGHT, AND THE REASON IS THE SHAPE OF THE BORROWING. What A3 borrows
-- is the Intestate Succession Act's CLASSES — a structural description of who
-- counts as kin — and not its s 2 application gate, which is a rule about
-- whose estate that Act distributes and has nothing to say about who a man's
-- relatives are. Muslims do apply for letters of administration under the
-- Probate and Administration Act, and the Administration of Muslim Law Act
-- 1966 s 113 assumes it in terms: "In all applications for probate or letters
-- of administration the affidavit supporting the application must, in the case
-- of a deceased Muslim, state the school of law (Mazhab) which the deceased
-- professed in addition to the particulars required by any other written law."
-- (Quoted from source/AMLA1966.txt.) A section that legislates for the form of
-- a Muslim's application presupposes that he makes one, and a corpus in which
-- no Muslim could be granted letters of administration would be the defective
-- one. AMLA s 111(2)(c) points the same way, saving "the provisions of the
-- Probate and Administration Act 1934" from s 111 entirely.
#ASSERT `s 18(5) — a law making special provision regarding the estates of persons of a particular religion or race is in evidence` `the Faizal estate`
#ASSERT NOT `s 18(5) — a law making special provision regarding the estates of persons of a particular religion or race is in evidence` `the Faizal estate, the deceased not being a Muslim`
#ASSERT `s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` `the Faizal estate`
#ASSERT `the persons entitled under section 18(4)(a)` `the Faizal estate` EQUALS
            LIST `Rohani Sulaiman`, `Hafiz Faizal`, `Sofea Faizal`
#ASSERT `s 6(2) — there is a minority` `the Faizal estate` (YMD 2026 1 15)
#ASSERT `s 21(1) — no probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a person while he is an infant` `Sofea Faizal` (YMD 2026 1 15)

-- AND THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT IS INDIFFERENT TO THE WHOLE QUESTION: it contains
-- no analogue of s 2, and the daughter's guardian is her mother either way.
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` `Sofea, an infant concerned in the estate`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Sofea, an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS LIST "rohani"

-- A MUSLIM'S WILL, AND WHY THE `valid` ANSWER IS SAFE. An earlier draft of
-- this comment called it "the one place the corpus answers where it has no
-- business answering". THAT WAS WRONG, AND IT WAS WRONG ABOUT THE LAW rather
-- than about the code, which is the worse kind of thing for a deliverable to
-- say. (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW 2026-08-26.)
--
-- AMLA s 111(1) does restrict a Muslim's testamentary disposition: "Despite
-- anything in the provisions of the English law or in any other written law,
-- no Muslim domiciled in Singapore may, after 1 July 1968, dispose of his or
-- her property by will except in accordance with the provisions of and subject
-- to the restrictions imposed by the school of Muslim law professed by him or
-- her." BUT s 111(2) CARVES THE WILLS ACT OUT BY NAME: "Nothing in this
-- section affects — (a) the provisions of the Wills Act 1838, other than
-- section 3 thereof; (b) the provisions of the Insurance Act 1966; (c) the
-- provisions of the Probate and Administration Act 1934; or (d) the will of a
-- Muslim dying before 1 July 1968." (Both quoted from source/AMLA1966.txt.)
--
-- AND THE CARVE-OUT REACHES EXACTLY WHAT THIS CORPUS COMPUTES.
-- `the formal validity of the will` decides ss 4, 5, 6 and 27 — how old the
-- testator was, how the will was executed, whether a foreign law or the
-- soldier's privilege saves it. None of those is Wills Act s 3, whose marginal
-- heading in source/WA1838.txt is "Property disposable by will". So s 111(2)(a)
-- leaves the formal-validity answer standing, and `wills-act.l4` is right to
-- have no gate for s 111 — the Wills Act 1838 contains none, and the module
-- does not invent one.
--
-- WHAT THE CORPUS GENUINELY CANNOT SAY, which is a real limit and a smaller
-- one: how far a Muslim's testamentary DISPOSITION is cut down by the school
-- of law he professed. That is s 111(1)'s subject and it needs the
-- Administration of Muslim Law Act 1966, which this cleanroom does not encode.
-- The corpus never computes a disposition for a Muslim's estate anyway — the
-- Intestate Succession Act is switched off by its s 2 and nothing else
-- distributes — so it does not answer the question it cannot answer.
`the will of Muhammad Faizal` MEANS
    `a will, in full` `Muhammad Faizal` (JUST (YMD 2020 5 5)) TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE
        (LIST `an attesting witness` `Marianne Alphonso` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
            , `an attesting witness` `Josephine Alphonso` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
        (LIST `a gift` `Hafiz Faizal` `a residuary gift` 900_000 TRUE TRUE)
        EMPTY FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE TRUE EMPTY

`the Faizal estate, a will having been left` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Muhammad Faizal` `the family of Muhammad Faizal`
        (`an estate in the case file` 900_000 0 0 0 900_000) (JUST `the will of Muhammad Faizal`)
        TRUE EMPTY (LIST `Sofea, an infant concerned in the estate`)

`the Faizal will, put to the Wills Act` MEANS
    `the Wills Act case of` `the Faizal estate, a will having been left`
        (`the plain facts about` `the will of Muhammad Faizal`)

#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the Faizal will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS `valid`
#ASSERT `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` `the Faizal estate, a will having been left` EQUALS
            `nothing in the Intestate Succession Act 1967 applies to this estate, and no other Act in this corpus distributes it`

-- AND THE WILLS ACT'S ANSWER AGREES WITH THE CASE FILE HERE, which is the
-- point of running the G1 check on a Muslim's estate too: the corpus is not
-- refusing to answer because it doubts the will; it is refusing to distribute
-- because s 2 says it may not.
#ASSERT `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` `the Faizal estate, a will having been left` `the Faizal will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the Wills Act leaves this will operative, and the case file holds it`


§§ `Scenario 4B — the two other stops, which the Intestate Succession Act states itself`

-- THE MUSLIM ESTATE IS NOT THE ONLY PLACE THIS CORPUS MUST DECLINE, and an
-- earlier draft of `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the
-- estate` behaved as though it were: it keyed its stop on s 2 alone, so the
-- two families below came back as "applies to the estate but has no effect on
-- these facts". That is the answer for a testate estate, where the Wills Act
-- IS in this corpus and does govern. It is the wrong answer here, where
-- another body of law governs and this corpus is not it.
--
-- FIRST: s 4(1) SENDS THE MOVABLES TO THE LAW OF THE DOMICILE. "The
-- distribution of the movable property of a person deceased shall be regulated
-- by the law of the country in which he was domiciled at the time of his
-- death." Chandran Nair died domiciled in Malaysia, leaving $700,000 in a
-- Singapore bank and no land here. s 5(a) needs a Singapore domicile and
-- s 5(b) needs immovable property in Singapore, and he has neither, so this
-- Act does not distribute a dollar of it — and s 4(1) names the law that does,
-- which is not one of these four.

`Chandran Nair` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Chandran Nair" "chandran" (JUST (YMD 1955 6 6))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1955 6 6)) (`died on` (YMD 2025 7 7))
        (LIST `a legitimate child in the case file` `Kiran Devan`)

`the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with only movables here` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file, in full` `Chandran Nair`
        (`a family in the case file` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING)
        (`an estate in the case file` 700_000 0 0 0 0) NOTHING FALSE FALSE EMPTY EMPTY

#ASSERT `this Act applies to the estate` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with only movables here`
#ASSERT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with only movables here`
#ASSERT `section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with only movables here`
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(1) — the law of the country of domicile is the law of Singapore` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with only movables here`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with only movables here`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with only movables here` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with only movables here` EQUALS
            `the deceased was domiciled outside Singapore and left no immovable property here, so section 4(1) sends the distribution of his movable property to the law of his domicile, and no Act in this corpus is that law`

-- AND THE MINIMAL PAIR: the identical estate with $1 of Singapore land in it
-- brings s 5(b) into play, and the Act distributes — the immovable property
-- alone, because s 4(1) still sends the movables away. `the property of which
-- every share under section 7 is a fraction` is the measure of the difference.
`the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with land here` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file, in full` `Chandran Nair`
        (`a family in the case file` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING)
        (`an estate in the case file` 700_000 300_000 0 0 0) NOTHING FALSE FALSE EMPTY EMPTY
#ASSERT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with land here`
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with land here` EQUALS 300_000
#ASSERT `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with land here` EQUALS
            `the Intestate Succession Act 1967 governs the distribution, and its rules have been applied`

-- AND THE OTHER MINIMAL PAIR, WHICH IS THE ONE THIS FILE USED TO GET WRONG.
-- Flip the domicile and take the Singapore property away. Chandran Nair dies
-- domiciled in Singapore, possessed beneficially of nothing situated here:
-- s 5(a) fails for want of property and s 5(b) fails because he was domiciled
-- here, so BOTH LIMBS FAIL EXACTLY AS THEY DO IN THE MALAYSIAN CASE ABOVE and
-- the reason is the opposite one. s 4(1) refers the distribution of his
-- movable property to the law of the country of his domicile, which here IS
-- Singapore and IS this Act; what this Act does not have is any property of
-- his that s 5 reaches. The member the Malaysian file gets says "the deceased
-- was domiciled outside Singapore", which of this file is false — and before
-- the repair it was the answer this file gave, because the member was keyed on
-- the failure of the two limbs and read no domicile at all. (REPAIR,
-- ADVERSARIAL REVIEW 2026-08-26, the cross-module join audit.)
`the Nair estate, the same man domiciled in Singapore with nothing here` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file, in full` `Chandran Nair`
        (`a family in the case file` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING)
        (`an estate in the case file` 0 0 0 0 0) NOTHING FALSE TRUE EMPTY EMPTY

#ASSERT `this Act applies to the estate` `the Nair estate, the same man domiciled in Singapore with nothing here`
#ASSERT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the Nair estate, the same man domiciled in Singapore with nothing here`
#ASSERT `section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` `the Nair estate, the same man domiciled in Singapore with nothing here`
#ASSERT `section 4(1) — the law of the country of domicile is the law of Singapore` `the Nair estate, the same man domiciled in Singapore with nothing here`
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `the Nair estate, the same man domiciled in Singapore with nothing here`
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` `the Nair estate, the same man domiciled in Singapore with nothing here`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Nair estate, the same man domiciled in Singapore with nothing here`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Nair estate, the same man domiciled in Singapore with nothing here` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` `the Nair estate, the same man domiciled in Singapore with nothing here` EQUALS
            `the deceased was domiciled in Singapore and was possessed beneficially of no property situated here, so section 4(1) names the law of Singapore and section 5 reaches no property to distribute`

-- SECOND: s 5 REACHES ONLY DEATHS AFTER 2 JUNE 1967. "If a person dies
-- intestate after 2 June 1967, he being at the time of his death — ..." Tan
-- Boon Hock died on 15 March 1960 and his estate has never been administered.
-- `intestate-succession-act.l4` reads the commencement as a condition to be
-- proved and not as one to be presumed, and argues that reading at its own
-- site; the consequence here is that no Act in this corpus distributes his
-- estate either.

`Tan Boon Hock` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Tan Boon Hock" "boonhock" (JUST (YMD 1900 1 1))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1900 1 1)) (`died on` (YMD 1960 3 15))
        (LIST `a legitimate child in the case file` `Kiran Devan`)

`the Tan Boon Hock estate, the death predating the Act` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Tan Boon Hock`
        (`a family in the case file` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING)
        (`an estate in the case file` 100_000 0 0 0 0) NOTHING FALSE EMPTY EMPTY

#ASSERT `this Act applies to the estate` `the Tan Boon Hock estate, the death predating the Act`
#ASSERT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the Tan Boon Hock estate, the death predating the Act`
#ASSERT NOT `section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` `the Tan Boon Hock estate, the death predating the Act`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Tan Boon Hock estate, the death predating the Act`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Tan Boon Hock estate, the death predating the Act` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` `the Tan Boon Hock estate, the death predating the Act` EQUALS
            `the death is not proved to be after 2 June 1967, so section 5 does not reach this estate, and no other Act in this corpus distributes it`

-- AND THE PROBATE ACT DOES NOT STOP ON EITHER OF THEM, exactly as it does not
-- stop on the Muslim estate. Neither s 4(1) nor s 5 is a rule of that Act, and
-- a grant may be taken out here in respect of assets here whatever law
-- distributes them.
#ASSERT `s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` `the Nair estate, domiciled in Malaysia with only movables here`
#ASSERT `s 18(1) — the person has died intestate` `the Tan Boon Hock estate, the death predating the Act`


§ `Scenario 5 — where the four Acts disagree, and where one of them runs out`

-- SEAMS, EXHIBITED RATHER THAN DESCRIBED. The cross-module audit of
-- 2026-08-26 mapped seven joins between the four modules — one on which they
-- agree, five on which one side is silent, and one on which they disagree.
-- Three of the silent five are joined by expression where the families meet
-- them: Guardianship of Infants Act s 17 against entitlement in scenario 1,
-- Wills Act s 27(3) against Guardianship of Infants Act s 7 in scenario 3, and
-- Probate and Administration Act s 21(2) against the office of guardian in
-- scenarios 1 and 6. §§5A to 5E take the rest — the agreement, the
-- disagreement from both its ends, and the two remaining silences — and each
-- is asserted on a family rather than argued in a comment, so that a later
-- change to any of the four modules moves an assertion in this file rather
-- than moving silently. §5F is a sixth seam, which was not on the audit's map
-- and was found by building this file.
--
-- AND EACH SUBSECTION BELOW SAYS WHICH OF THE FOUR KINDS ITS SEAM IS, which a
-- later review had to work out for itself: §5A is a CROSS-CHECK stated as an
-- invariant, §5B is a genuine divergence that needs no closing, §5C measures an
-- ABSENCE, §5D makes the corpus's one literal output-to-input HAND-OFF, §5E is
-- a DUPLICATED DEFINITION, and §5F is a gap in the ontology that neither Act
-- can fill. `The joins, audited` sets the four kinds out and says why the
-- labels are load-bearing.
--
-- ONE OF THE FIVE IS NO LONGER A DISAGREEMENT, and §5E says so: the audit's
-- disagreement row about the Intestate Succession Act's "child" travelling
-- into the Probate Act's "next of kin" was repaired in
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` after the audit ran. The assertions there
-- record the agreement, which is what a regression test is for.

§§ `Scenario 5A — one silence, three shapes: the age that is not in evidence`

-- THREE OF THE FOUR MODULES ASK WHETHER A PERSON IS UNDER AGE, AND ALL THREE
-- RESOLVE THE NOT-IN-EVIDENCE CASE DIFFERENTLY. Each argues its answer at its
-- own site; `wills-act.l4`'s AMBIGUITY A1 sets all three out side by side.
-- What no module could do is put a single person to all three at once.
--
--   WILLS ACT               a THIRD VALUE. `Whether the testator had attained
--                           21 years of age when the will was made` has a
--                           member for it and the aggregate verdict carries a
--                           matching one: neither limb is made out.
--   PROBATE ACT             FALSE. `is an infant on` folds a missing majority
--                           date to FALSE, so the s 21(1) bar does not bite.
--   GUARDIANSHIP ACT        NOTHING. Its predicate returns `MAYBE BOOLEAN`
--                           and propagates the absence.
--
-- NONE OF THE THREE IS WRONG, and this file does not repair any of them. What
-- it does is make the divergence a fact of record: on ONE person, the Probate
-- Act reports that he is not an infant and the Guardianship Act reports that
-- it cannot be determined, about the same day.

`Gerald Menon, whose age is nowhere in evidence` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Gerald Menon" "gerald" NOTHING NOTHING `living` EMPTY

`Gerald Menon, guardian of nobody` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Gerald Menon, whose age is nowhere in evidence` "gerald"
        `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant` `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Gerald Menon, put forward as an infant` MEANS
    `an infant in the case file` `Gerald Menon, whose age is nowhere in evidence` NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY 0

`the will of Gerald Menon, undated` MEANS
    `a will, in full` `Gerald Menon, whose age is nowhere in evidence` NOTHING TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE
        (LIST `an attesting witness` `Marianne Alphonso` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
            , `an attesting witness` `Josephine Alphonso` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
        (LIST `a gift` `Marianne Alphonso` `a residuary gift` 1_000 TRUE TRUE)
        EMPTY FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE TRUE EMPTY

`the Menon estate` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Gerald Menon, whose age is nowhere in evidence`
        (`a family in the case file` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING)
        (`an estate in the case file` 1_000 0 0 0 1_000) (JUST `the will of Gerald Menon, undated`)
        FALSE EMPTY (LIST `Gerald Menon, put forward as an infant`)

`the Menon will, put to the Wills Act` MEANS
    `the Wills Act case of` `the Menon estate` (`the plain facts about` `the will of Gerald Menon, undated`)

-- THE WILLS ACT: a third value, and a verdict that says the section could not
-- be applied rather than that it was applied and passed.
#ASSERT `the age of the testator when the will was made` (`the will under consideration` `the Menon will, put to the Wills Act`) EQUALS
            `not in evidence, the date of birth of the testator or the date of execution of the will not being known`
#ASSERT NOT `s 4 — will of infant invalid` (`the will under consideration` `the Menon will, put to the Wills Act`)
#ASSERT NOT `s 4 — the testator is proved to have been 21 years of age or over` (`the will under consideration` `the Menon will, put to the Wills Act`)
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the Menon will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS
            `the age of the testator when the will was made not being in evidence, section 4 cannot be applied`

-- THE PROBATE ACT: FALSE, so the s 21(1) bar does not bite and nothing is
-- said about the doubt.
#ASSERT NOT (`Gerald Menon, whose age is nowhere in evidence` `is an infant on` (YMD 2026 1 15))
#ASSERT NOT `s 21(1) — no probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a person while he is an infant` `Gerald Menon, whose age is nowhere in evidence` (YMD 2026 1 15)
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — there is a minority` `the Menon estate` (YMD 2026 1 15)

-- THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT: NOTHING, and a named outcome for it downstream.
#ASSERT `the person is an infant on the date in question` `Gerald Menon, whose age is nowhere in evidence` (YMD 2026 1 15) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Gerald Menon, put forward as an infant` (YMD 2026 1 15) EQUALS
            `whether the person is an infant on that date cannot be determined, the date on which he attains the age of majority not being in evidence`

-- AND WHERE THE DATE IS IN EVIDENCE THE TWO THAT SHARE THE FIELD AGREE
-- EXACTLY, BOUNDARY INCLUDED. Divya attains majority on 2 November 2033. Both
-- modules read `family-domain.l4`'s
-- `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` and both compare
-- it strictly; the Probate Act writes the comparison `is before` and the
-- Guardianship Act writes it `LESS THAN`. Each of the four assertions below
-- pins one module's half on one date.
#ASSERT     `Divya Sivakumar` `is an infant on` (YMD 2033 11 1)
#ASSERT NOT (`Divya Sivakumar` `is an infant on` (YMD 2033 11 2))
#ASSERT `the person is an infant on the date in question` `Divya Sivakumar` (YMD 2033 11 1) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `the person is an infant on the date in question` `Divya Sivakumar` (YMD 2033 11 2) EQUALS JUST FALSE

-- AND HERE IS THE SAME AGREEMENT AS AN INVARIANT, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME THING.
-- Four assertions on two dates pin the two modules POINTWISE: each says what
-- one module answers on one day, and a divergence anywhere else in the input
-- space goes unnoticed. `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on
-- this date` is one expression naming both modules, so it FAILS wherever they
-- part company — and it is asserted on both sides of the boundary, on a date
-- long before it, and on a date long after.
--
-- IT IS A CROSS-CHECK AND NOT A HAND-OFF, and the distinction is the whole
-- lesson of the review that added it: two independently written answers to one
-- question, neither of which is an input to the other. Calling it a hand-off,
-- as an earlier draft did, made it sound as though the Probate Act got its
-- answer from the Guardianship Act. It does not; it computes its own.
#ASSERT `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` `Divya Sivakumar` (YMD 2033 11 1)
#ASSERT `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` `Divya Sivakumar` (YMD 2033 11 2)
#ASSERT `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` `Divya Sivakumar` (YMD 2026 1 15)
#ASSERT `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` `Divya Sivakumar` (YMD 2099 12 31)
#ASSERT `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` `Meera Sivakumar` (YMD 2026 1 15)
#ASSERT `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` `Ethan Sim` (YMD 2029 9 8)
#ASSERT `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` `Ethan Sim` (YMD 2029 9 9)

-- AND WHERE THEY PART COMPANY THE INVARIANT SAYS SO, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT A
-- TEST. Gerald Menon's majority date is nowhere in evidence: the Probate Act
-- folds the absence to FALSE and the Guardianship Act returns NOTHING, so the
-- equality is false for every date. Both are right at their own sites and this
-- file repairs neither — G6 — but the divergence is now asserted rather than
-- described.
#ASSERT NOT `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` `Gerald Menon, whose age is nowhere in evidence` (YMD 2026 1 15)
#ASSERT NOT `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` `Gerald Menon, whose age is nowhere in evidence` (YMD 1900 1 1)

§§ `Scenario 5B — the Probate Act's flat class against the Intestate Succession Act's ladder`

-- THE AUDIT'S DISAGREEMENT ROW, IN THE FORM IT SURVIVES IN. The Probate Act
-- takes "next of kin" as, in `probate-administration-act.l4`'s own words,
-- "the classes to whom the Intestate Succession Act 1967
-- distributes on an intestacy — issue, parents, brothers and sisters and
-- their issue, grandparents, uncles and aunts — taken as a flat class" — its
-- AMBIGUITY A3, reading (i) — while the Intestate Succession Act's rules 4 to
-- 8 are a LADDER: rule 5 gives the parents the estate only "If there are no
-- descendants".
--
-- SO ON A FAMILY WITH A SPOUSE, ISSUE AND SURVIVING PARENTS THE TWO ACTS PART
-- COMPANY, and both are right on their own subject matter: a grant may be made
-- to a person who takes nothing. The composition's job is to make sure nobody
-- reads the Probate Act's class as a list of beneficiaries.

`Ramanathan Pillai` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Ramanathan Pillai" "ramanathan" (JUST (YMD 1940 1 1))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1940 1 1)) `living` EMPTY

`Kamatchi Pillai` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Kamatchi Pillai" "kamatchi" (JUST (YMD 1943 6 6))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1943 6 6)) `living` EMPTY

`the family of Sivakumar Ramanathan, his parents surviving` MEANS
    `a family in the case file`
        (LIST `a marriage in the case file` `Meera Sivakumar` (JUST (YMD 1994 6 4)) TRUE)
        (JUST `Ramanathan Pillai`) (JUST `Kamatchi Pillai`)

`the Sivakumar intestacy, his parents surviving` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Sivakumar Ramanathan`
        `the family of Sivakumar Ramanathan, his parents surviving`
        `the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan` NOTHING FALSE
        (LIST `the grant to the widow and the son`)
        (LIST `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate`)

-- THE INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT PAYS THE PARENTS NOTHING. Rule 2 gives the
-- spouse one-half and rule 3 gives the issue the other half; rule 5's
-- antecedent — "If there are no descendants" — is not made out.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 5 — there are no descendants, and there are a parent or parents of the intestate` `the Sivakumar intestacy, his parents surviving`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Sivakumar intestacy, his parents surviving` EQUALS
            LIST "Meera Sivakumar takes 0.5", "Arjun Sivakumar takes 0.25", "Divya Sivakumar takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Sivakumar intestacy, his parents surviving` "ramanathan" EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Sivakumar intestacy, his parents surviving` "kamatchi" EQUALS 0

-- THE PROBATE ACT PUTS THEM IN THE CLASS TO WHICH A GRANT MAY BE MADE.
#ASSERT `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` `the Sivakumar intestacy, his parents surviving` "ramanathan"
#ASSERT `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` `the Sivakumar intestacy, his parents surviving` "kamatchi"
#ASSERT `the persons entitled under section 18(4)(a)` `the Sivakumar intestacy, his parents surviving` EQUALS
            LIST `Meera Sivakumar`, `Arjun Sivakumar`, `Divya Sivakumar`, `Ramanathan Pillai`, `Kamatchi Pillai`

§§ `Scenario 5C — the debts nobody reads`

-- THE AUDIT'S `one-side-silent` ROW ABOUT THE EXPENSES SEAM, MEASURED. The
-- Intestate Succession Act s 5 distributes the property "after payment thereout
-- of the expenses of due administration as prescribed by the Probate and
-- Administration Act 1934", and `intestate-succession-act.l4` deducts exactly
-- that — `Estate`'s `the funeral, testamentary and administration expenses` —
-- and says at its own site why it does not deduct the debts. The Act that
-- would apply the debts is the Probate and Administration Act, whose ss 57-58
-- and both Schedules `probate-administration-act.l4` puts out of scope.
--
-- SO `the debts and liabilities of the deceased` HAS NO READER IN THE CORPUS.
-- The pair below differs in that one field alone — nil against $900,000 on a
-- $1,000,000 fund — and every answer in every module is identical.

`the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan, owing $900,000` MEANS `an estate in the case file` 700_000 500_000 200_000 900_000 0

`the Sivakumar intestacy, owing $900,000` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Sivakumar Ramanathan` `the family of Sivakumar Ramanathan`
        `the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan, owing $900,000` NOTHING FALSE
        (LIST `the grant to the widow and the son`)
        (LIST `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate`)

#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `the Sivakumar intestacy, owing $900,000` EQUALS 1_000_000
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Sivakumar intestacy, owing $900,000` EQUALS
            `the distribution, read out` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Sivakumar intestacy, owing $900,000` "divya" EQUALS 250_000
#ASSERT `the capital moneys of this infant that no guardian may give a good discharge for` `the Sivakumar intestacy, owing $900,000` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS 250_000

-- THE s 29(2) PAIR BELOW IS A PIN ON THE ABSENCE AND NOT A TEST OF s 29(2),
-- and saying which is the difference between a measurement and a decoration.
-- s 29(2) reads two things: `the value of the property in Singapore`, which
-- this pair holds constant, and the SECURED CREDITORS, which this pair does
-- not vary because it varies `the debts and liabilities of the deceased`
-- instead. The subsection says so itself — "without deduction of any debts due
-- by the deceased, other than debts secured by mortgage" — so the bond COULD
-- NOT have moved, and the assertion that it did not is evidence that the
-- Money cell has no reader, which is the section's point.
#ASSERT `s 29(2) — the amount at which the estate within the jurisdiction is sworn` `the Sivakumar intestacy, owing $900,000` EQUALS
            `s 29(2) — the amount at which the estate within the jurisdiction is sworn` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT `s 29(2) — the amount at which the estate within the jurisdiction is sworn` `the Sivakumar intestacy, owing $900,000` EQUALS 1_200_000

-- AND THE PAIR THAT DOES TEST s 29(2)'s ONE LIVE CONJUNCT. The same
-- $1,200,000 estate, and this time the $300,000 is a debt a CREDITOR has
-- proved and holds security for. Now the sworn amount moves, by exactly the
-- secured debt and by nothing else. (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW 2026-08-26:
-- the pair above was the only one here, and it could not have failed.)
--
-- AND THE INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT STILL DOES NOT MOVE, which is the finding
-- of this section restated from the other end: the fund it distributes is the
-- s 5 property less the expenses of due administration, and a secured creditor
-- is not an expense of administration. So the same $300,000 that shrinks the
-- administration bond by a quarter, to $900,000, leaves Divya's $250,000
-- exactly where it was — the corpus's two money answers moving independently
-- because ss 57-58 are out of scope.
`Lim Wei Sheng, the mortgagee` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Lim Wei Sheng" "weisheng" (JUST (YMD 1960 3 3))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1960 3 3)) `living` EMPTY

`the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan, $300,000 of it secured by mortgage` MEANS
    `an estate in the case file, with creditors` 700_000 500_000 200_000 300_000 0
        (LIST `a creditor in the case file` `Lim Wei Sheng, the mortgagee` 300_000 TRUE)

`the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan, $300,000 of it unsecured` MEANS
    `an estate in the case file, with creditors` 700_000 500_000 200_000 300_000 0
        (LIST `a creditor in the case file` `Lim Wei Sheng, the mortgagee` 300_000 FALSE)

`the Sivakumar intestacy, a mortgagee having proved` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Sivakumar Ramanathan` `the family of Sivakumar Ramanathan`
        `the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan, $300,000 of it secured by mortgage` NOTHING FALSE
        (LIST `the grant to the widow and the son`)
        (LIST `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate`)

`the Sivakumar intestacy, an unsecured creditor having proved` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Sivakumar Ramanathan` `the family of Sivakumar Ramanathan`
        `the estate of Sivakumar Ramanathan, $300,000 of it unsecured` NOTHING FALSE
        (LIST `the grant to the widow and the son`)
        (LIST `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate`)

#ASSERT `s 29(2) — the amount at which the estate within the jurisdiction is sworn` `the Sivakumar intestacy, a mortgagee having proved` EQUALS 900_000
#ASSERT `s 29(2) — the amount at which the estate within the jurisdiction is sworn` `the Sivakumar intestacy, an unsecured creditor having proved` EQUALS 1_200_000
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `the Sivakumar intestacy, a mortgagee having proved` EQUALS 1_000_000
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Sivakumar intestacy, a mortgagee having proved` "divya" EQUALS 250_000

-- WHAT THAT MEANS FOR A FAMILY, and it is not a rounding error: the corpus
-- will tell Divya she is entitled to $250,000 out of an estate that owes
-- $900,000 against $1,200,000 of assets. The figure is right as far as the
-- Intestate Succession Act goes — that Act deducts the expenses of
-- administration and nothing else — and it is not what she will receive.
-- Neither module is wrong; the corpus is incomplete, and this is where a
-- reader is owed the fact.

§§ `Scenario 5D — one fact under two names: the administrator who "is entitled to the whole"`

-- THE AUDIT'S LAST `one-side-silent` ROW, AND THE ONE WITH A PRICE ON IT.
-- Probate and Administration Act s 29(5) — "When the administrator is entitled
-- to the whole of the estate after payment of the debts, sureties in the bond
-- may ordinarily be dispensed with." Whether the administrator IS so entitled
-- is a statement about the distribution, which is the Intestate Succession
-- Act's subject; `intestate-succession-act.l4` computes it and offers it, at
-- `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act`.
--
-- THE TWO MEET NOWHERE. `family-domain.l4` declares `Entitlement` and no
-- record anywhere has a field of that type, so `probate-administration-act.l4`
-- takes s 29(5)'s antecedent as a SWORN BOOLEAN on its own
-- `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`, and reports the gap
-- from its own side in as many words. Nothing checks the oath against the
-- computation.
--
-- SO THE FILE BELOW CONTRADICTS ITSELF AND NOTHING OBJECTS. On the Sivakumar
-- estate the Intestate Succession Act divides the fund three ways; the further
-- facts swear that the administrator takes the whole; and the sureties on the
-- administration bond are dispensed with on an estate where s 29(5) does not
-- permit it. Both assertions below hold, together, on one family.

`the widow as grantee` MEANS
    `a grantee in the case file` (`an individual` `Meera Sivakumar`)
        `as the guardian of the person and property of the infant` (JUST "divya")

`the further facts, the administrator swearing he takes the whole` MEANS
    `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns` WITH
        `the administrator is entitled to the whole of the estate after payment of the debts` IS TRUE
        `the Family Court thinks fit to require such security` IS FALSE
        `there is danger that the property may be wasted` IS FALSE
        `it appears that such appointment would not be a sufficient protection for the estate` IS FALSE
        `the Public Trustee has by writing signed by him declared that he undertakes to administer such property` IS FALSE

-- THE INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT'S ANSWER: three people take, so no one person
-- takes the whole.
#ASSERT NOT `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` `the Sivakumar intestacy`

-- THE PROBATE ACT'S ANSWER, ON THE SAME ESTATE, ON THE SWORN BOOLEAN: the
-- sureties go.
#ASSERT `s 29 — the security ordinarily required of this grantee` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the widow as grantee` `the further facts, the administrator swearing he takes the whole` `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
            `security by bond in the prescribed form by the grantee, sureties being dispensed with` 1_200_000

-- AND WHAT IT WOULD HAVE BEEN HAD THE OATH MATCHED THE COMPUTATION. The
-- difference between the two assertions is two sureties on a $1,200,000 bond,
-- and the only thing standing between them is a boolean nobody checks.
#ASSERT `s 29 — the security ordinarily required of this grantee` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the widow as grantee` `the further facts, nothing being made out` `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
            `security by bond in the prescribed form by the grantee and 2 sureties` 1_200_000

-- AND NOW THE JOIN ITSELF, WHICH IS THE ONE PLACE IN THIS FILE WHERE ONE ACT'S
-- OUTPUT IS LITERALLY THE OTHER ACT'S INPUT. `the further facts, the section
-- 29(5) antecedent taken from the Intestate Succession Act` builds the Probate
-- Act's own input record with `one person takes the whole of the property
-- distributable under this Act` — the Intestate Succession Act's computed
-- answer — in the field the Probate Act would otherwise have taken on oath.
-- The Probate Act's s 29 decision is then evaluated on that record.
--
-- BOTH MODULES SAID THE JOIN SHOULD BE MADE HERE AND NEITHER COULD MAKE IT.
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` writes at that field: "a case file may swear
-- this boolean TRUE while that Act's rules give the administrator half, and
-- nothing in this cleanroom would object".
-- `intestate-succession-act.l4` writes at its own: the decision is offered "so
-- that the sworn boolean `probate-administration-act.l4` declares for that
-- subsection has something to be checked against". Neither module can call the
-- other. This file can.
--
-- COULD THE THREE ASSERTIONS BELOW PASS IF THE TWO MODULES DISAGREED? No. On
-- the Sivakumar estate the Intestate Succession Act divides the fund three
-- ways, so the antecedent is FALSE and the sureties stay — change rule 2 or
-- rule 3 and the security changes. On the Devan intestacy Kiran takes the whole
-- under rule 3, the antecedent is TRUE and the sureties go — change s 3's
-- definition of "child" so that Nithya takes too, and the sureties come back.
`Kiran as grantee` MEANS
    `a grantee in the case file` (`an individual` `Kiran Devan`) `as administrator` NOTHING

`the grant of letters of administration to Kiran` MEANS
    `a grant in the case file` `letters of administration`
        (LIST `Kiran as grantee`) (JUST (YMD 2026 3 1)) `not limited`

#ASSERT NOT `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` `the Sivakumar intestacy`
#ASSERT `s 29 — the security ordinarily required of this grantee` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the widow as grantee` (`the further facts, the section 29(5) antecedent taken from the Intestate Succession Act` `the Sivakumar intestacy`) `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
            `security by bond in the prescribed form by the grantee and 2 sureties` 1_200_000
#ASSERT `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` `the Devan intestacy`
#ASSERT `s 29 — the security ordinarily required of this grantee` `the Devan intestacy` `the grant of letters of administration to Kiran` `Kiran as grantee` (`the further facts, the section 29(5) antecedent taken from the Intestate Succession Act` `the Devan intestacy`) (YMD 2026 3 1) EQUALS
            `security by bond in the prescribed form by the grantee, sureties being dispensed with` 400_000

-- AND THE SAME GRANT WITH THE OATH LEFT UNSWORN, so that the difference the
-- computed antecedent makes is visible as a difference and not just as a
-- value: on the Devan intestacy the two sureties are the price of not asking
-- the Intestate Succession Act.
#ASSERT `s 29 — the security ordinarily required of this grantee` `the Devan intestacy` `the grant of letters of administration to Kiran` `Kiran as grantee` `the further facts, nothing being made out` (YMD 2026 3 1) EQUALS
            `security by bond in the prescribed form by the grantee and 2 sureties` 400_000

-- WHAT THE JOIN STILL DOES NOT SUPPLY, and it is one conjunct rather than the
-- whole. s 29(5) reads "When the administrator is entitled to the whole of the
-- estate AFTER PAYMENT OF THE DEBTS"; `intestate-succession-act.l4` says at
-- its own site that it does not compute that half, because the debts are
-- Probate Act ss 57-58's and those are out of scope — G5 again, from a third
-- direction. So an estate whose debts exhaust it satisfies the computed
-- antecedent and not the subsection. The composition narrows the gap by one
-- conjunct and does not close it, and a file-builder who reads this as
-- "s 29(5) is now computed" has read it wrong.
--
-- NOR DOES IT ASK WHETHER THE SOLE TAKER IS THE ADMINISTRATOR.
-- `intestate-succession-act.l4` says so too: "who obtained the grant is the
-- Probate and Administration Act's question, over `the grants made in respect
-- of the estate`, and this Act never looks at a grant". On the Devan fixture
-- the sole taker and the grantee are both Kiran, and nothing above checks that
-- they are the same man.

§§ `Scenario 5E — a row the audit called a disagreement, which no longer is one`

-- THE AUDIT OF 2026-08-26 RECORDED THAT the Probate Act's "next of kin"
-- walked the raw `children` edge and ignored Intestate Succession Act s 3's
-- definition of "child", so that a grant could be opened to, and a s 6(4)
-- beneficiary found in, a person the Intestate Succession Act pays nothing.
-- That was repaired in
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` after the audit ran: its
-- `the child and his descendants` now gates on
-- `a “child” within the meaning of section 3 of the Intestate Succession Act
-- 1967`, which is its AMBIGUITY A12.
--
-- IT IS A DUPLICATED DEFINITION AND NOT A HAND-OFF, and the label matters
-- because the two are repaired in different places. Nothing flows from one
-- module to the other and nothing could: `probate-administration-act.l4` does
-- not import `intestate-succession-act.l4` — an Act module in this cleanroom
-- imports the shared ontology and nothing else — so what it has is a SECOND
-- TEXT of one definition, written over `Child`'s own fields, with its own note
-- saying "If the two ever disagree the shared ontology is the place to
-- reconcile them". A hand-off would be closed by carrying a value; this can
-- only be closed by moving the definition into `family-domain.l4`, and that is
-- the domain gap both modules report.
--
-- ASSERTED HERE BECAUSE A DEFINITION WITH TWO TEXTS DRIFTS SILENTLY. Priyanka
-- Devan leaves two children: Kiran, her legitimate son, and Nithya, who is
-- neither legitimate nor adopted. On s 3's definition Nithya is not a "child",
-- and BOTH modules now say so.

`Kiran Devan` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Kiran Devan" "kiran" (JUST (YMD 1995 2 2))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1995 2 2)) `living` EMPTY

`Nithya Devan` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Nithya Devan" "nithya" (JUST (YMD 1997 3 3))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1997 3 3)) `living` EMPTY

`Priyanka Devan` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Priyanka Devan" "priyanka" (JUST (YMD 1970 7 7))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1970 7 7)) (`died on` (YMD 2025 4 1))
        (LIST `a legitimate child in the case file` `Kiran Devan`
            , `a child in the case file neither legitimate nor adopted` `Nithya Devan`)

`the Devan intestacy` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Priyanka Devan`
        (`a family in the case file` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING)
        (`an estate in the case file` 400_000 0 0 0 0) NOTHING FALSE EMPTY EMPTY

-- s 3 — "“child” means a legitimate child and includes any child adopted by
-- virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in
-- force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam". Asserted on the
-- definition itself, in both modules, over the same `Child` value.
#ASSERT     `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` (`a legitimate child in the case file` `Kiran Devan`)
#ASSERT NOT `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` (`a child in the case file neither legitimate nor adopted` `Nithya Devan`)
#ASSERT     `a “child” within the meaning of section 3 of the Intestate Succession Act 1967` (`a legitimate child in the case file` `Kiran Devan`)
#ASSERT NOT `a “child” within the meaning of section 3 of the Intestate Succession Act 1967` (`a child in the case file neither legitimate nor adopted` `Nithya Devan`)

-- AND THE SAME FOUR FACTS AS ONE EXPRESSION, so that an edit to either text
-- alone fails an assertion here rather than being caught by a reader comparing
-- two pairs. The four above pin each module against its own text; the two
-- below pin the two texts against each other, and the third runs the check
-- over every child of a whole case file rather than over `Child` values chosen
-- by hand.
#ASSERT `the two modules agree whether this child is a “child” within the meaning of section 3` (`a legitimate child in the case file` `Kiran Devan`)
#ASSERT `the two modules agree whether this child is a “child” within the meaning of section 3` (`a child in the case file neither legitimate nor adopted` `Nithya Devan`)
#ASSERT `the two modules agree about every child of this deceased` `the Devan intestacy`
#ASSERT `the two modules agree about every child of this deceased` `the Chiam estate, the will struck out`
#ASSERT `the two modules agree about every child of this deceased` `the Faizal estate`

-- AND ON THE ESTATE. The Intestate Succession Act pays Kiran the whole and
-- Nithya nothing; the Probate Act opens the grant to Kiran and not to Nithya.
-- The two Acts name the same person.
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `the Devan intestacy` EQUALS LIST "Kiran Devan takes 1"
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Devan intestacy` "kiran" EQUALS 400_000
#ASSERT `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession Act by the person whose reference is` `the Devan intestacy` "nithya" EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the next of kin of the deceased` `the Devan intestacy` EQUALS LIST `Kiran Devan`
#ASSERT     `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` `the Devan intestacy` "kiran"
#ASSERT NOT `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` `the Devan intestacy` "nithya"
#ASSERT `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` `the Devan intestacy`


§§ `Scenario 5F — the field that decides whether section 17 bites, and nothing computes it`

-- FOUND BY BUILDING THE COMPOSITION, AND NOT IN THE AUDIT. Guardianship of
-- Infants Act s 6(1) makes the surviving mother "guardian of the infant" —
-- unqualified. It does not say guardian OF THE PERSON, or OF THE PROPERTY, or
-- of both. Every provision that could bite on her property powers is
-- qualified: ss 16(1), 17 and 18(1) each open "A guardian of the property of
-- an infant", and s 15(1) speaks of "the guardian of an infant’s property".
--
-- SO `family-domain.l4`'s `Kind of guardianship` IS AN INPUT ON THE `Guardian`
-- RECORD, and for a parent who becomes guardian by force of s 6 there is no
-- appointment anywhere to fix it. Nothing in `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`
-- derives it: its s 6 decisions answer WHETHER she is guardian and its
-- `a guardian of the property of the infant` reads the field it is given.
--
-- THE CONSEQUENCE, MEASURED BELOW: whether the mother can hand over her
-- daughter's quarter of a million turns on which of three enum members
-- whoever typed the case file chose. The identical woman, the identical
-- infant, the identical share — recorded as guardian of the PERSON only, and
-- s 17 does not reach her at all.
--
-- WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT is not this file's to write, and it is not obviously the
-- ontology's either: it may be that the Act leaves the kind to the court, in
-- which case the honest fix is a fourth member of `Kind of guardianship`
-- meaning that no appointment fixed it, and a ruling in
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` about what ss 15 to 18 do with it. Recorded
-- here as a question for that module rather than answered here.

`Meera, recorded as guardian of the person only` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Meera Sivakumar` "divya"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person of the infant` `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Divya, her mother recorded as guardian of the person only` MEANS
    `Divya, an infant concerned in the estate, the mother holding` `Meera, recorded as guardian of the person only`

-- THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT STILL MAKES HER GUARDIAN. s 6(1) does not care which
-- kind, so the answer to "who is the guardian" is unchanged.
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` `Divya, her mother recorded as guardian of the person only`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Divya, her mother recorded as guardian of the person only` EQUALS LIST "meera"

-- AND s 17 NOW HAS NOTHING TO BITE ON, so the composition reports that
-- somebody CAN give a good discharge for the share — on facts that differ from
-- scenario 1 in one enum member.
#ASSERT NOT `a guardian of the property of the infant` `Meera, recorded as guardian of the person only`
#ASSERT NOT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `Meera, recorded as guardian of the person only`
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Divya, her mother recorded as guardian of the person only` EQUALS
            `a guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS
            `no guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`

-- AND THE MONEY MOVES WITH IT, WHICH IS THE PRICE OF THE UNFIXED FIELD IN ONE
-- FIGURE. The identical woman, the identical infant, the identical quarter
-- share: recorded as guardian of the person and property, a quarter of a
-- million dollars cannot be receipted for; recorded as guardian of the person
-- only, all of it can.
#ASSERT `the capital moneys of this infant that no guardian may give a good discharge for` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `Divya as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS 250_000
#ASSERT `the capital moneys of this infant that no guardian may give a good discharge for` `the Sivakumar intestacy, the widow being guardian of the person only` `Divya, her mother recorded as guardian of the person only` EQUALS 0

-- AND THE PROBATE ACT MOVES THE OTHER WAY ON THE SAME FIELD, which is what
-- makes it worth a section of its own rather than a footnote: s 21(2) names
-- "the guardian of the person and property of the infant", and
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` tests the conjunction expressly. So the one
-- unfixed enum member decides BOTH whether the mother may take the grant and
-- whether she may give a discharge — and it decides them in opposite
-- directions.
`the Sivakumar intestacy, the widow being guardian of the person only` MEANS
    `the Sivakumar intestacy, with` `Meera, recorded as guardian of the person only`
        (LIST `the grant to the widow and the son`)
#ASSERT NOT `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant` `the Sivakumar intestacy, the widow being guardian of the person only` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the day letters of administration are applied for`
#ASSERT `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant` `the Sivakumar intestacy` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the day letters of administration are applied for`

-- AND THE TWO ACTS THEREFORE DISAGREE ABOUT HER, WHICH THE COMPOSED VALUE
-- SAYS IN ONE WORD: the Guardianship Act goes on counting her as Divya's
-- guardian, because s 6(1) does not care which kind, and the Probate Act stops
-- opening s 21(2) to her, because s 21(2) names the conjunction. That is a
-- THIRD case file on which the two part company, and unlike the two in
-- scenario 1 step 4 it does so on a field the ontology carries and nothing
-- computes.
#ASSERT `whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this grant` `the Sivakumar intestacy, the widow being guardian of the person only` `the grant to the widow and the son` `the day letters of administration are applied for` EQUALS
            `the Guardianship of Infants Act counts the grantee as a guardian of the infant, and the Probate and Administration Act does not open section 21(2) on this grant`


§ `Scenario 6 — the testate estate whose executor is a child`

-- THE SAME FOUR-ACT CHAIN AS SCENARIO 1, TAKEN BY THE OTHER ROUTE. There the
-- estate was intestate and s 21(2)'s counterfactual — "would, but for his
-- infancy, be entitled" — was made out through s 18(4)(a); here there is a
-- valid will and it is made out through s 8(1), "Probate may be granted to any
-- executor appointed by a will". And where scenario 1 exercised the "other
-- capital moneys" limb of Guardianship of Infants Act s 17 on an intestate
-- share, this one exercises the word s 17 uses first — "any legacy".
--
-- Bernard Sim died on 5 May 2025 leaving a properly executed will that
-- appointed his son Ethan, then sixteen, sole executor and left him the
-- residue, $600,000. Ethan's mother Grace survives.

`Grace Sim` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Grace Sim" "grace" (JUST (YMD 1974 2 2))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1974 2 2)) `living` EMPTY

`Ethan Sim` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Ethan Sim" "ethan" (JUST (YMD 2008 9 9))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 2008 9 9)) `living` EMPTY

`Bernard Sim` MEANS
    `a person in the case file` "Bernard Sim" "bernard" (JUST (YMD 1970 11 11))
        (`twenty-one years after` (YMD 1970 11 11)) (`died on` (YMD 2025 5 5))
        (LIST `a legitimate child in the case file` `Ethan Sim`)

`Grace, guardian of Ethan by virtue of surviving the father` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Grace Sim` "ethan"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant` `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Grace, the court having authorised her to discharge the legacy` MEANS
    `a guardian in the case file` `Grace Sim` "ethan"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant` `alone` FALSE FALSE TRUE

GIVEN `the mother’s guardianship` IS A Guardian
GIVETH AN Infant
`Ethan, an infant concerned in the estate` MEANS
    `an infant in the case file` `Ethan Sim` (JUST `Bernard Sim`) (JUST `Grace Sim`)
        (LIST `the mother’s guardianship`) 600_000

`Ethan as an infant concerned in the estate` MEANS
    `Ethan, an infant concerned in the estate` `Grace, guardian of Ethan by virtue of surviving the father`

-- THE EXECUTOR IS BUILT WITH THE PROBATE ACT'S OWN CONSTRUCTOR, so that the
-- five facts s 13(1) reads off him can be set. He is not otherwise incapable,
-- has not renounced, has not extracted probate (he cannot: s 21(1) bars the
-- grant) and has not administered the estate.
`Ethan Sim, appointed sole executor` MEANS `a PAA executor` `Ethan Sim` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

`the will of Bernard Sim` MEANS
    `a will, in full` `Bernard Sim` (JUST (YMD 2021 6 1)) TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE
        (LIST `an attesting witness` `Marianne Alphonso` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
            , `an attesting witness` `Josephine Alphonso` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
        (LIST `a gift` `Ethan Sim` `a residuary gift` 600_000 TRUE TRUE)
        EMPTY FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE TRUE
        (LIST `Ethan Sim, appointed sole executor`)

`the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company` MEANS
    `a grant in the case file` `letters of administration with the will annexed`
        (LIST `a grantee in the case file` (`an individual` `Grace Sim`)
                  `as the guardian of the person and property of the infant` (JUST "ethan")
            , `a grantee in the case file` `Acme Trust Company` `as administrator` NOTHING)
        (JUST (YMD 2026 2 2))
        (`limited in some other way` "limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself")

`the Sim testacy` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Bernard Sim`
        (`a family in the case file` (LIST `a marriage in the case file` `Grace Sim` (JUST (YMD 1999 4 4)) TRUE)
             NOTHING NOTHING)
        (`an estate in the case file` 600_000 0 0 0 600_000) (JUST `the will of Bernard Sim`)
        FALSE (LIST `the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company`)
        (LIST `Ethan as an infant concerned in the estate`)

`the Sim will, put to the Wills Act` MEANS
    `the Wills Act case of` `the Sim testacy` (`the plain facts about` `the will of Bernard Sim`)

`the day the Sim grant is applied for` MEANS YMD 2026 2 2

-- STEP 1, THE WILLS ACT: the will is good.
#ASSERT `the will is properly executed` `the Sim will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT NOT `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the Sim will, put to the Wills Act`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the Sim will, put to the Wills Act` EQUALS `valid`

-- STEP 2, THE INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT: it has nothing to do here, and says
-- so. A residuary gift of the whole means the testator is not an "intestate"
-- within s 3.
#ASSERT NOT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the Sim testacy`
#ASSERT `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` `the Sim testacy` EQUALS
            `the Intestate Succession Act 1967 applies to the estate but has no effect on these facts`

-- STEP 3, THE PROBATE ACT: s 8(1) would give Ethan probate, and s 21(1) will
-- not let him have it. Both are true of the same boy on the same day, and the
-- pair is the whole of s 21(2)'s antecedent.
#ASSERT `s 8(1) — probate may be granted to this person as an executor appointed by a will` `the Sim testacy` "ethan"
#ASSERT `s 21(1) — no probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a person while he is an infant` `Ethan Sim` `the day the Sim grant is applied for`

-- s 13(1)(b) — "the executor or all the executors appointed by will are
-- legally incapable of acting as such". His incapacity is s 21(1)'s and is
-- computed by this module rather than sworn: `family-domain.l4` carries only
-- the residue of "legally incapable", precisely so that a file cannot say a
-- man is an infant and not incapable.
#ASSERT `s 13(1)(b) — this executor is legally incapable of acting as such` `Ethan Sim, appointed sole executor` `the day the Sim grant is applied for`
#ASSERT `s 13(1)(b) — the executors are legally incapable of acting as such, or have renounced the right to act as such` `the will of Bernard Sim` `the day the Sim grant is applied for`
-- OVER-DETERMINED, AND SAID SO. Limb (e) also fires on these facts — he has
-- not appeared and extracted probate, because s 21(1) stops him — so the gate
-- below does not by itself test limb (b). Limb (b) is tested on its own,
-- immediately above.
#ASSERT `s 13(1)(e) — the executors appointed by any will do not appear and extract probate` `the will of Bernard Sim`
#ASSERT `s 13(1) — letters of administration with the will annexed may be granted on failure of executors` `the Sim testacy` `the will of Bernard Sim` `the day the Sim grant is applied for`

-- AMBIGUITY RECORDED, AND IT IS THIS FILE'S AND NOT THE PROBATE ACT MODULE'S
-- (ADVERSARIAL REVIEW 2026-08-26). The three assertions above and the s 21(2)
-- assertion below open TWO DIFFERENT GRANTS on one estate, and they are
-- alternatives rather than cumulative:
--   s 13(1) opens an UNLIMITED grant of letters of administration with the will
--     annexed "to such person or persons as the court considers the fittest";
--   s 21(2) opens one to the guardian, "limited until the infant obtains a
--     grant to himself".
-- The grant this family actually took out is the s 21(2) one — the fixture's
-- limitation says so in terms — and both routes are asserted on it without the
-- tension being named. It is named now.
--
-- THE CONSTRUCTION THAT PRODUCES BOTH is `probate-administration-act.l4`'s
-- reading of s 13(1)(b), which folds infancy into "legally incapable of acting
-- as such". READING (i): infancy is a legal incapacity, so an infant executor
-- is one and s 13(1) is open. READING (ii): it is not, and s 21 governs an
-- infant executor to the exclusion of s 13(1).
--
-- THE ACT TELLS AGAINST (i) IN ITS OWN WORDS, and this file records the
-- counter-argument rather than picking the winner, because the construction
-- belongs to the module that made it. s 21(3): "Where there are 2 or more
-- infant executors or persons so entitled, any grant made under subsection (2)
-- shall be limited until any of them obtains a grant." The Act therefore
-- contemplates "infant executors" who remain executors; the italic heading
-- over s 21 in source/PAA1934.txt is "Letters of administration during
-- infancy"; and if s 13(1)(b) reached infancy, s 21(2)'s route for an infant
-- entitled to PROBATE would be redundant and the s 13(1) grant would
-- extinguish the reserved right s 21(2) exists to preserve.
--
-- WHAT THIS FILE ASSERTS, THEREFORE: both routes as the module computes them,
-- and — as the operative statement about this family — that the grant actually
-- made is the s 21(2) grant, carrying s 21(2)'s limitation and not an
-- unlimited one. That is the same treatment scenario 1 gives the s 21(2)/s 6(2)
-- interaction: assert both halves on the identical grant and say which governs.
#ASSERT (`the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company`'s `the limitation`) EQUALS
            `s 21 — the limitation on a grant made under subsection (2)` `the Sim testacy` `the day the Sim grant is applied for`
#ASSERT (`the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company`'s `the limitation`) EQUALS
            `limited in some other way` "limited until the infant obtains a grant to himself"

-- s 6(2) — the minority is Ethan's, and it is satisfied here by the OTHER
-- limb of the subsection: a trust corporation, "with or without an
-- individual". Scenario 1 satisfied it by counting two individuals.
#ASSERT `s 6(2) — there is a minority` `the Sim testacy` `the day the Sim grant is applied for`
#ASSERT `s 6(2) — granted either to a trust corporation, with or without an individual, or to not less than 2 individuals` `the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company`
#ASSERT `s 6(2) — the grant complies with section 6(2)` `the Sim testacy` `the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company` `the day the Sim grant is applied for`

-- s 21(2), REACHED THROUGH s 8(1) AND NOT THROUGH s 18(4)(a). This is the
-- limb scenario 1 could not exercise, because on an intestacy there is no
-- will to appoint an executor.
#ASSERT `s 21(2) — this infant would, but for his infancy, be entitled to probate or letters of administration` `the Sim testacy` `Ethan as an infant concerned in the estate` `the day the Sim grant is applied for`
#ASSERT `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant` `the Sim testacy` `the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company` `the day the Sim grant is applied for`

-- STEP 4, THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT: who the guardian the Probate Act just granted
-- to actually is. The same two-module agreement as scenario 1, on a testacy —
-- and asserted the same way, as one value that depends on both Acts rather
-- than as two assertions a reader puts together.
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` `Ethan as an infant concerned in the estate`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Ethan as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS LIST "grace"
#ASSERT elem "grace" (`the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Ethan as an infant concerned in the estate`)
#ASSERT `whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this grant` `the Sim testacy` `the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company` `the day the Sim grant is applied for` EQUALS
            `both Acts are satisfied: the Probate Act opens section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act counts the grantee as a guardian of the infant`

-- AND THE SAME DIVERGENCE AS SCENARIO 1, ON A TESTACY: strike out the
-- redundant `Guardian` record for the mother and the Guardianship Act still
-- makes her guardian under s 6(1) while the Probate Act stops opening s 21(2)
-- to her. Two families, two routes into s 21(2) — one through s 18(4)(a) and
-- one through s 8(1) — and the same disagreement at the end of both.
`Ethan, no Guardian record having been made for his mother` MEANS
    `an infant in the case file` `Ethan Sim` (JUST `Bernard Sim`) (JUST `Grace Sim`) EMPTY 600_000
`the Sim testacy, no record having been made for the mother` MEANS
    `a deceased person in the case file` `Bernard Sim`
        (`a family in the case file` (LIST `a marriage in the case file` `Grace Sim` (JUST (YMD 1999 4 4)) TRUE)
             NOTHING NOTHING)
        (`an estate in the case file` 600_000 0 0 0 600_000) (JUST `the will of Bernard Sim`)
        FALSE (LIST `the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company`)
        (LIST `Ethan, no Guardian record having been made for his mother`)
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Ethan, no Guardian record having been made for his mother` EQUALS LIST "grace"
#ASSERT NOT `s 21(2) — letters of administration may be granted to the guardian of the person and property of the infant` `the Sim testacy, no record having been made for the mother` `the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company` `the day the Sim grant is applied for`
#ASSERT `whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this grant` `the Sim testacy, no record having been made for the mother` `the grant with the will annexed to Grace and a trust company` `the day the Sim grant is applied for` EQUALS
            `the Guardianship of Infants Act counts the grantee as a guardian of the infant, and the Probate and Administration Act does not open section 21(2) on this grant`

-- STEP 5, s 17 ON THE WORD IT USES FIRST — "any legacy". Ethan's $600,000
-- comes to him under a will and not on an intestacy, and s 17 is in the same
-- terms about both. His mother holds the grant, and she cannot give a good
-- discharge for his legacy unless the court orders otherwise.
#ASSERT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `Grace, guardian of Ethan by virtue of surviving the father`
#ASSERT NOT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `Grace, the court having authorised her to discharge the legacy`
`Ethan, the court having authorised the discharge of his legacy` MEANS
    `Ethan, an infant concerned in the estate` `Grace, the court having authorised her to discharge the legacy`
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Ethan as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS
            `no guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`
#ASSERT `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` `Ethan, the court having authorised the discharge of his legacy` EQUALS
            `a guardian whom the Guardianship of Infants Act counts could give a good discharge for the infant’s share`

-- AND THE MONEY JOIN IS SILENT HERE, WHICH IS ITSELF THE ANSWER. Ethan's
-- $600,000 comes to him under a WILL, and the decision that turns a share into
-- a sum reads the Intestate Succession Act — which has no effect on this
-- estate, because the residuary gift disposes of the whole of it. So the
-- figure it returns is nil, and it is nil because the Intestate Succession Act
-- pays him nothing, not because anybody may give a good discharge. Section 17
-- reaches his legacy just the same: "any legacy or OTHER capital moneys". What
-- the corpus cannot do is put a number on a legacy, because nothing in it
-- computes what a will pays out.
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the Sim testacy`
#ASSERT `the capital moneys of this infant that no guardian may give a good discharge for` `the Sim testacy` `Ethan as an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS 0

-- AND THE ANSWER FOR THIS FAMILY, IN ONE SENTENCE: the will is valid, the boy
-- is the executor and the residuary legatee, nobody may grant him probate
-- until 9 September 2029, his mother may take letters of administration with
-- the will annexed limited until he obtains a grant to himself — and she
-- cannot give a good discharge for his legacy in the meantime.
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Ethan as an infant concerned in the estate` (YMD 2029 9 8) EQUALS
            `the person is an infant on that date, and the guardians of the infant are` (LIST "grace")
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Ethan as an infant concerned in the estate` (YMD 2029 9 9) EQUALS
            `the person has attained the age of majority on that date, so this Act has no infant to govern`
#ASSERT NOT `s 21(1) — no probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a person while he is an infant` `Ethan Sim` (YMD 2029 9 9)


§ `The joins, audited — what each one turned out to be`

-- THE PART OF THIS FILE WORTH READING HARDEST, AND THE PART AN ADVERSARIAL
-- REVIEW ON 2026-08-26 REWROTE. It found that this file claimed eight
-- hand-offs MADE and had made none of them: every one was verified by a human
-- reading two adjacent single-Act assertions, and no assertion anywhere could
-- fail because two Acts disagreed. The ledger below says, for each claim, what
-- it IS — and the joins that could be made have since been made, in
-- `The joins, as expressions` above.
--
-- THE FOUR KINDS, AND WHY THE LABELS MATTER. A COMPOSITION is one expression
-- in which one Act's answer is an input to the other's. A CROSS-CHECK is one
-- expression that reads two independently written answers to one question and
-- fails if they part company — genuinely valuable, and not a hand-off, because
-- neither side feeds the other. A DUPLICATED DEFINITION is one rule with two
-- texts; it can be pinned but never joined, and it is closed by moving the
-- definition and not by carrying a value. And BLOCKED is blocked. Mislabelling
-- any of these as a hand-off is what let the file report that its seams were
-- tested when they were not.
--
-- J1. INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT ENTITLEMENT -> GUARDIANSHIP ACT s 17.
--     WAS: adjacency. `the shares taken by an infant concerned in the estate,
--     read out` and `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good
--     discharge` were asserted three lines apart and neither could fail
--     because of the other; what they shared was an INPUT, the reference
--     "divya", and not an output.
--     IS NOW: COMPOSED. `the capital moneys of this infant that no guardian may
--     give a good discharge for` puts the Intestate Succession Act's figure and
--     the Guardianship Act's verdict into one number, asserted in scenario 1
--     step 5, in §5C and in §5F. Change either module and the figure moves.
--     THOSE THREE ARE THE WHOLE OF THE SITE LIST, AND THERE IS A FOURTH PLACE
--     THE VALUE IS ASSERTED THAT IS NOT ONE OF THEM: scenario 6 step 5, where
--     it is nil on Ethan's legacy. That assertion cannot move for the reason
--     this row gives, because neither module's answer about him is reaching it
--     — the figure is nil because the Intestate Succession Act pays a legatee
--     nothing, and it would move only if that Act stopped reading his father's
--     will. Scenario 6 says so at the site, in terms; this row says it here so
--     that a reader counting seven assertions against a list of three does not
--     have to work out which is wrong. (ADVERSARIAL REVIEW 2026-08-26, the
--     cross-module join audit: the over-stated list was in the report of the
--     round before, not in this file, and nothing about the join changed.)
--
-- J2. PROBATE ACT s 21(2) (WHETHER) -> GUARDIANSHIP ACT (WHO).
--     WAS: adjacency, and — worse — NOT EXERCISED ON A CASE WHERE THE TWO ACTS
--     DIVERGE. The file asserted `elem "meera" (...)` and the s 21(2) answer
--     separately and said "The two Acts agree about the same woman on the same
--     day" on the strength of it.
--     IS NOW: COMPOSED. `whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants
--     Act agree about this grant` is a four-member value that reads both Acts,
--     and all four members are asserted: both satisfied (scenario 1, scenario
--     6), neither (the removed mother), the Probate Act alone (the uncle the
--     surviving mother objects to, GIA s 7(3)), and the Guardianship Act alone
--     (the mother for whom no `Guardian` record was typed, GIA s 6(1)). The
--     last two are the divergences, and neither existed in this file before.
--
-- J3. THE SAME JOIN IN THE NEGATIVE: THE COURT REMOVES THE MOTHER UNDER s 10.
--     WAS: adjacency — real content with the wrong label. Both modules read one
--     shared field, `removed from his guardianship by the court`, by two
--     independently written paths, and each assertion pinned its own module.
--     IS NOW: COMPOSED, as the `neither Act is satisfied on this grant` member
--     of the same value. One expression, and it moves if either path changes.
--
-- J4. PROBATE ACT "NEXT OF KIN" <-> INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT s 3 "child".
--     WAS, AND REMAINS, A DUPLICATED DEFINITION. `probate-administration-act.l4`
--     does not import the other module; it restates s 3's two limbs over
--     `Child`'s own fields and says so. Nothing flows and nothing can: there
--     are two texts of one definition, and only moving the definition into
--     `family-domain.l4` would close it — the domain gap both modules report.
--     WHAT CHANGED: the four pointwise assertions in §5E are joined by
--     `the two modules agree whether this child is a “child” within the meaning
--     of section 3`, one expression over both texts, run over every child of
--     three whole case files.
--
-- J5. WILLS ACT s 27(3) APPOINTMENTS <-> THE ESTATE-SIDE GUARDIANS.
--     WAS, AND REMAINS, A CROSS-CHECK INSIDE ONE MODULE. Both halves are
--     decisions of `wills-act.l4`, which labels them "NEITHER OF THE TWO
--     DECISIONS BELOW IS A RULE OF THE WILLS ACT", and both read data this file
--     typed; the equality checks that the author wrote one name twice. Worth
--     having, not evidence that two Acts compose.
--     WHAT CHANGED: the real Wills Act -> Guardianship Act join is now made
--     separately, at J10.
--
-- J6. PROBATE ACT / GUARDIANSHIP ACT INFANCY WHERE THE MAJORITY DATE IS IN
--     EVIDENCE.
--     WAS, AND REMAINS, A CROSS-CHECK: two independently written comparisons
--     over one ontology field, neither an input to the other.
--     WHAT CHANGED: it was pinned POINTWISE, by four assertions on two dates,
--     so any other input was untested — which is how J2's divergence escaped.
--     `the two Acts agree whether this person is an infant on this date` states
--     it as an invariant and §5A asserts it on seven dates across four people,
--     and asserts its FAILURE on the person whose age is not in evidence.
--
-- J7. ONE DEATH, THREE ACTS.
--     WAS, AND REMAINS, A SHARED INPUT — recorded here as a CROSS-CHECK
--     because that is the nearest honest label, and stated at its site in
--     scenario 1 step 4 as what it is. One `Person` value, one `vital status`,
--     read by the Intestate Succession Act to open its distribution, by the
--     Probate Act to open its grant and by the Guardianship Act to make the
--     mother guardian. Three readings of one fact; no answer is an input to
--     any other, and each of the three assertions could pass while the others
--     failed.
--
-- J8. "INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT SHARES -> A SUM OF MONEY, AND THAT SUM ->
--     PROBATE ACT s 6(2)'s MINORITY AND s 29(2)'s BOND."
--     WAS WRONG, AND THE CLAIM IS WITHDRAWN. No sum reaches either provision:
--     `s 6(2) — there is a minority` is an age test over `the infants concerned
--     in the estate`, and s 29(2) reads `Estate` values less the secured
--     creditors. And the premise was bad law — Divya's quarter comes from
--     s 7 rule 3's per stirpes division among the children, so she would take a
--     quarter at forty. §5C asserts that independence, and the file used to
--     contradict itself about it.
--     IS NOW: the correction is written at the s 6(2) assertion in scenario 1
--     step 3, and what is really there — one recorded majority date read by two
--     Acts for unrelated purposes — is asserted as the J6 cross-check.
--
-- J9. INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT s 7 -> PROBATE ACT s 29(5)'s ANTECEDENT.
--     Not on the original list at all, and it is the one join in this file
--     that is a LITERAL OUTPUT-TO-INPUT. `the further facts, the section 29(5)
--     antecedent taken from the Intestate Succession Act` builds the Probate
--     Act's own input record with that Act's computed
--     `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act`
--     in the field the Probate Act would otherwise have taken on oath, and
--     s 29 is evaluated on it. Both modules asked for this in terms and neither
--     could do it. §5D asserts it on an estate where the answer is FALSE and on
--     one where it is TRUE, and asserts what the unsworn oath would have cost.
--     WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT SUPPLY: "after payment of the debts" — G5.
--
-- J10. WILLS ACT s 27(3) -> GUARDIANSHIP ACT s 7(1)-(2).
--     Reported BLOCKED in an earlier draft, and blocked it is not — see G3
--     below for what remains blocked and what does not.
--     IS NOW: COMPOSED, as a CHECK on a case file. `every testamentary guardian
--     the Guardianship of Infants Act counts holds under an appointment the
--     Wills Act has made good` takes the Wills Act's list as the input to a
--     membership test over the Guardianship Act's counted guardians. Scenario 3
--     step 3 asserts it TRUE on the soldier's file and FALSE on the civilian's,
--     which is the disagreement that section used to describe in prose.
--
-- J11. THE WILLS ACT'S VERDICT vs THE CASE FILE'S `the will`.
--     Reported BLOCKED in an earlier draft — see G1.
--     IS NOW: a CHECK, in `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act
--     about the will` and the two decisions beside it, asserted across
--     scenarios 2A, 2B, 3, 4 and 6. All four states are reached, including the
--     one that costs money: the Wills Act has defeated the will and the
--     Intestate Succession Act, reading the same file, still reports that the
--     deceased was not an "intestate".


§ `Hand-offs that could not be made`

-- THE ONES THAT REMAIN BLOCKED. Each entry names the MODULE and the FACT that
-- is missing, says what the composition asserted instead, and says what would
-- close it. Nothing below is worked around: where a bridge does not exist this
-- file does not build one, because a bridge built in the composition is a rule
-- of law written where no lawyer would look for it.
--
-- AND THE LINE BETWEEN A RULE AND A CHECK IS WHERE TWO OF THESE MOVED. G1 and
-- G3 were reported blocked outright; they are blocked as RULES — nothing may
-- recompute a recorded fact from another module's verdict — and they were
-- never blocked as CHECKS. Both are now built as checks, at J10 and J11, and
-- their entries below say what remains missing after that.
--
-- G1. THE WILLS ACT'S VERDICT DOES NOT REACH THE OTHER THREE ACTS.
--     MISSING: any field, anywhere in `family-domain.l4`, that records whether
--     a will is formally valid or has been revoked. `Deceased person`'s
--     `the will` is a `MAYBE Will` and it is a FACT — the document exists or
--     it does not — while `wills-act.l4`'s `the formal validity of the will`
--     and `s 15 — the will has been revoked` are CONCLUSIONS about that
--     document. `intestate-succession-act.l4`'s
--     `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` and
--     `probate-administration-act.l4`'s `s 18(1) — the person has died
--     intestate` each read the fact and neither consults the conclusion.
--     BLOCKED AS A RULE, AND NOT BLOCKED AS A CHECK — which an earlier draft
--     of this entry ran together, and so declined both. Nothing here may
--     recompute `the will` from the verdict; a decision that READS both and
--     reports that they disagree changes nothing and decides nothing.
--     ASSERTED INSTEAD: scenarios 2A and 2B assert the answers on BOTH case
--     files — the will in the record and the will struck out — and the pair
--     differs in TWO fields, `Deceased person`'s `the will` and `Estate`'s
--     `the value of the property beneficially disposed of by the will`. (An
--     earlier draft said one; the second was found by the review of
--     2026-08-26, and it strengthens the entry: a file-builder must keep TWO
--     derived facts in step with a conclusion nothing computes.)
--     AND NOW CHECKED: `whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about
--     the will`, `the Wills Act has left no operative will and the Intestate
--     Succession Act still reads one`, and its Probate Act twin — see J11.
--     WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT: a `Deceased person` field of the shape
--     "the will admitted to probate", computed rather than recorded, or a
--     ruling in the shared ontology that `the will` means the operative will
--     and that whoever builds a case file must apply the Wills Act first. The
--     second is cheaper and is a discipline, not a computation; the first is
--     the only one a machine can check. The checks above are the third
--     alternative and the weakest of the three — they catch a file that has
--     already gone wrong, in a file a consumer may never run.
--
-- G2. THE WILLS ACT CANNOT BE ASKED ABOUT AN ESTATE WITH NO WILL.
--     MISSING: nothing, strictly — this is a consequence of the module's
--     shape. `the formal validity of the will` takes a `A case under this Act`
--     which contains a `Will`, so on an intestacy there is no value to pass
--     it. The composition's very first step is therefore answered by reading
--     the ontology and not by asking the Act.
--     ASSERTED INSTEAD: scenario 1 step 1 asserts `isNothing` of the field,
--     and says in terms that this is not a Wills Act answer.
--
-- G3. WILLS ACT s 27(3) IS WRITTEN AS A HAND-OFF AND THE GUARDIANSHIP ACT
--     MODULE CANNOT TAKE IT — THOUGH THIS FILE CAN.
--     MISSING: any link from a `Guardian` to the instrument that appointed
--     him. `How the guardian was appointed` has members
--     `appointed by the father by will` and `appointed by the mother by will`
--     and neither carries a will. Measured: `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`
--     contains no decision whose parameter is a `Will` or a `MAYBE Will` — the
--     count of `IS A Will` and `IS A MAYBE Will` in that file is zero — so
--     there is no shape in which it could read `wills-act.l4`'s answer even if
--     it wanted to.
--     THAT MEASUREMENT IS TRUE OF THAT MODULE AND DOES NOT REACH THIS ONE,
--     which is where an earlier draft of this entry went wrong. This file
--     imports both Acts, and `wills-act.l4` invites the bridge in terms: "the
--     hand-off is a VALUE — the list-valued decision below — that a module
--     seeing both Acts can call directly, per appointment."
--     ASSERTED INSTEAD, AND NOW COMPOSED: scenario 3 step 3 changes one fact
--     about the testator, shows the Wills Act's answer flipping from
--     LIST "zarina" to EMPTY, shows the Guardianship Act's answers standing
--     unmoved on BOTH the soldier's and the civilian's `Infant` record — and
--     then asserts the disagreement itself, at J10.
--     WHAT WOULD STILL CLOSE IT PROPERLY: `Guardian` would need a field naming
--     the will — `Will` has no identity in this ontology either, so the honest
--     minimum is a `MAYBE STRING` reference to the instrument, or a boolean
--     recording the Wills Act's own conclusion. Either is a change to
--     `family-domain.l4`, which is why neither is made here. Until one is made,
--     the Guardianship Act module cannot ask the question at all and only a
--     composition can — which means a consumer that uses the four modules
--     without this file gets no answer.
--
-- G4. `Entitlement` IS COMPUTED AND NEVER STORED, AND TWO PROVISIONS PAY FOR
--     IT. `family-domain.l4:1853` declares `Entitlement`; no field anywhere in
--     that file has its type. `intestate-succession-act.l4` returns
--     `LIST OF Entitlement` from twelve decisions and nothing can carry the
--     answer anywhere.
--     WHAT IT COSTS, TWICE OVER — AND BOTH HALVES ARE NOW BRIDGED BY
--     EXPRESSION, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS BEING CLOSED:
--       (a) Guardianship of Infants Act s 17 governs the payment of "any
--           legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by an
--           infant", and the legacy is exactly what no FIELD can carry to it.
--           An earlier draft joined the two by the REFERENCE STRING alone —
--           "divya" appearing both in the distribution and in `the infants
--           concerned in the estate` — and said so: "a join over identity and
--           not over the money". `the capital moneys of this infant that no
--           guardian may give a good discharge for` now computes the money
--           (J1). What has not changed is the ontology: the figure is an
--           expression in this file, so a consumer that never calls it still
--           has no route from a `Deceased person` to a sum an infant cannot be
--           paid.
--       (b) Probate and Administration Act s 29(5) takes its antecedent as a
--           sworn boolean on `The further facts on which the grant machinery
--           turns`, and `probate-administration-act.l4` reports the gap from
--           its own side. Scenario 5D exhibits a file where the oath and the
--           computation contradict each other and the sureties on a $1,200,000
--           bond are dispensed with anyway — and then supplies the antecedent
--           from the Intestate Succession Act's own answer instead (J9).
--     WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT: `Deceased person` needs a `LIST OF Entitlement`,
--     and s 29(5)'s antecedent needs to become a computation over it. Both
--     bridges above are made in the composition, where only a reader of this
--     file benefits from them; the field is what would make them structural.
--
-- G5. THE DEBTS HAVE NO READER ANYWHERE IN THE CORPUS.
--     MEASURED: `the debts and liabilities of the deceased`, `the value of the
--     interest in property which at the time of his death is charged with the
--     payment of money`, `the amount of the charge on that interest` and
--     `the deceased has by will, deed or other document signified a contrary
--     or other intention` occur in the four Act modules only in fixture
--     assignments and in comments. No rule reads any of them, because
--     `probate-administration-act.l4` puts ss 57-58 and both Schedules out of
--     scope and the Intestate Succession Act deducts the expenses of due
--     administration and not the debts.
--     ASSERTED INSTEAD: scenario 5C runs the same family twice, owing nil and
--     owing $900,000 against $1,200,000 of assets, and every answer in every
--     module is identical.
--     WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT: the Probate and Administration Act's ss 57-58 and
--     its Second Schedule, which is a decision for that module and not a
--     defect in the ontology.
--
-- G6. THE THREE INFANCY SHAPES ARE NOT A GAP AND CANNOT BE JOINED AWAY.
--     `wills-act.l4` yields a third value, `probate-administration-act.l4`
--     folds to FALSE, `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` yields NOTHING, and all
--     three are argued at their own sites. Scenario 5A asserts all three on
--     one person. A consumer of this corpus must choose its own fold and say
--     which; this file does not choose one.
--     WHAT CHANGED: the agreement between the two that share the ontology's
--     field was pinned POINTWISE, on two dates, so a divergence anywhere else
--     went unnoticed. It is now an INVARIANT — `the two Acts agree whether this
--     person is an infant on this date` — asserted on seven dates across four
--     people, and asserted to FAIL on the person whose majority date is not in
--     evidence, which is the one place the two Acts really do part company.
--     That is J6: a cross-check, not a hand-off, because neither module's
--     answer is an input to the other's.
--
-- G7. THE PROBATE ACT'S "NEXT OF KIN" IS DELIBERATELY WIDER THAN THE INTESTATE
--     SUCCESSION ACT'S TAKERS, and scenario 5B asserts the divergence rather
--     than smoothing it. A grant may be made to a person who takes nothing.
--     Nothing needs closing; what needs saying is that the Probate Act's class
--     must never be presented to a family as a list of beneficiaries.
--
-- G8. NO ACT IN THIS CORPUS DISTRIBUTES A MUSLIM ESTATE. Intestate Succession
--     Act s 2 switches its own Act off and points at "any rules of the Muslim
--     law"; the Act that supplies them, the Administration of Muslim Law Act
--     1966, is not encoded here. Scenario 4 asserts the stop and asserts that
--     the other three Acts go on running, which is correct.
--     AND IT IS NOT THE ONLY STOP, which an earlier draft of this file missed:
--     `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate` keyed its
--     stop on s 2 alone, so an estate whose movables s 4(1) sends to a foreign
--     law of domicile, and a death before 2 June 1967, both came back as
--     "applies to the estate but has no effect on these facts". Two members
--     were added for them and §4B asserts both.
--     AND THE FIRST OF THOSE TWO WAS ITSELF WRONG AS FIRST WRITTEN, which is
--     worth recording because it was a worse failure than the one it repaired:
--     keyed on the failure of s 5's two limbs and reading no domicile, it told
--     a Singapore-domiciled deceased who left no property here that "the
--     deceased was domiciled outside Singapore" — a member stating a fact the
--     case file denies. The gate now carries the domicile and a sixth member
--     takes the Singapore-domiciled case, where s 4(1) names the law of
--     Singapore and s 5 reaches nothing. §4B asserts that side too, on the same
--     man with the domicile flipped.
--     WHAT AN EARLIER DRAFT ALSO GOT WRONG, and it was wrong about the LAW: it
--     called the `valid` verdict on a Muslim's will an over-answer. AMLA
--     s 111(2)(a) expressly preserves "the provisions of the Wills Act 1838,
--     other than section 3 thereof", and formal validity is ss 4, 5, 6 and 27
--     and not s 3. The corrected statement is at scenario 4.
--
-- G9. WHAT KIND OF GUARDIAN THE SURVIVING PARENT IS, NOBODY DECIDES. Found by
--     building this file rather than by the audit; scenario 5F sets it out and
--     measures it. The one unfixed enum member decides both whether the mother
--     may take the grant under Probate and Administration Act s 21(2) and
--     whether Guardianship of Infants Act s 17 stops her giving a discharge,
--     and it decides them in opposite directions.
--     STILL OPEN, DELIBERATELY. Guardianship of Infants Act s 6(1) says "be
--     guardian of the infant" unqualified while ss 16(1), 17 and 18(1) each
--     open "A guardian of the property of an infant" and s 15(1) speaks of "the
--     guardian of an infant’s property". That is a fact about the Act and this
--     file does not resolve it; what would resolve it is a fourth member of
--     `Kind of guardianship` meaning that no appointment fixed it, and a ruling
--     in `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` about what ss 15 to 18 do with it.
--     WHAT CHANGED: the composition no longer PRETENDS the question is closed.
--     `whether some guardian of this infant could give a good discharge for the
--     infant’s share` has a third member for the case where the Guardianship
--     Act counts somebody as guardian and the file holds no record fixing his
--     kind — which is the ordinary case of a surviving parent, since s 6 needs
--     no record. Scenario 3 step 4 asserts it on Adam, whose mother is guardian
--     by force of s 6(1) and has no `Guardian` record. An earlier draft
--     answered that question with a raw traversal of the recorded guardians,
--     which over-counted the removed and the not-yet-appointed and ignored the
--     guardian the Act names.


§ `What is deliberately not asserted, and why`

-- DEONTIC OUTCOMES. `probate-administration-act.l4` states four of its
-- provisions as regulative rules with parties and acts, and a `DEONTIC` cannot
-- be `EQUALS`-compared. Nothing in this file asserts one. The composition's
-- questions are all constitutive — who takes, who may be granted, who is
-- guardian, who may discharge — and the duties that follow a grant are within
-- one Act and are exercised in that Act's own file.
--
-- THE COURT'S DISCRETIONS. Every scenario stops where the four modules stop.
-- "such person as the court thinks fit" (Probate and Administration Act
-- s 18(4)(d)), "if it thinks fit appoint a guardian" (Guardianship of Infants
-- Act s 6(1)), "may order that a will be rectified" (Wills Act s 28(1)) and
-- the rest are named as out of scope in the modules that own them, and a
-- composition that guessed at them would be inventing law at the one point
-- where the Acts have declined to state it.
--
-- MONEY THE ACTS DO NOT COMPUTE. `the sum taken under the Intestate Succession
-- Act by the person whose reference is` multiplies a fraction the Act does
-- state by a fund the Act does define, and it is labelled as not being a rule
-- of any Act. Nothing else here turns a fraction into a dollar figure, and in
-- particular no scenario reports what an infant will actually RECEIVE — see
-- G5: the corpus cannot subtract the debts.
--
-- A SECOND OPINION ON ANY ACT. Where two modules answer differently this file
-- asserts both answers and names the divergence. It never picks a winner, and
-- none of the decisions it defines decides anything that any of the four Acts
-- decides. They fall into three kinds and each is labelled at its site:
--   READING HELPERS, which restate one Act's answer in a shape a family can
--     use — the two fraction-and-sum helpers, and
--     `what this corpus can say about the distribution of the estate`;
--   COMPOSITIONS, which put two Acts' answers into one expression so that a
--     disagreement between them fails an assertion — the nine decisions in
--     `The joins, as expressions`;
--   CHECKS ON A CASE FILE, which report that a file and a module's verdict
--     disagree and change neither — the three Wills Act decisions among them.
-- A COMPOSITION IS NOT A THIRD OPINION and a check is not a rule. Where one of
-- them reports a disagreement, the answer is that the file is inconsistent or
-- the ontology is short of a field, and never that one of the Acts is wrong.


§ `Conventions in this file`

-- NAMES. Every fixture and constructor introduced here is named so that it
-- could not collide with the four Act modules, which were de-collided from one
-- another before this file could be written at all. The constructors carry
-- "in the case file"; the people are named for families that appear nowhere
-- else in the corpus. No decision here carries a tag, because none of them is
-- a rule and none of them shares a name with anything.
--
-- FIXTURES BORROWED RATHER THAN REBUILT. Where an Act module's own constructor
-- fills only fields that Act reads, this file uses its own; where the
-- constructor is complete for the purpose, the Act module's is called
-- directly. From `wills-act.l4`: `a case`, `a will, in full`,
-- `an attesting witness`, `a gift`, `an executor appointed by the will`,
-- `the plain facts about`, `the facts about a will`,
-- `no conformity to any law outside this Act`,
-- `not revoked in any of those ways` and `no revival`. From
-- `probate-administration-act.l4`: `a PAA executor`, `Acme Trust Company`,
-- `the further facts, nothing being made out` and the record type
-- `The further facts on which the grant machinery turns`. That is deliberate:
-- a composition that rebuilt everything would not be evidence that the four
-- modules compose.
--
-- ASSERTIONS, NOT EVALUATIONS. Every directive in this file is an `#ASSERT`.
-- The expected answer was written down from the section first and the file run
-- afterwards, which is the only order in which an assertion can find anything.
-- Where a scenario turns on a threshold — a date of majority, a count of
-- administrators, a fraction — both sides of the boundary are asserted.
--
-- MINIMAL PAIRS ARE THE METHOD, AND A PAIR THAT DIVIDES TWO ACTS IS THE POINT
-- OF THEM. Findings in this file are made by running one family twice with one
-- field changed: the will in the record against the will struck out (2A, 2B),
-- the soldier against the civilian (3), the Muslim against the non-Muslim (4),
-- the domicile and the date of death against the ordinary case (4B), the
-- secured creditor against the unsecured one (5C), and the guardian of the
-- person against the guardian of the person and property (5F). A single case
-- file can only show that a module answers; a pair shows WHICH FACT the answer
-- turned on, and therefore which fact a neighbouring module is failing to read.
--
-- AND FOUR OF THE PAIRS ARE BUILT SPECIFICALLY TO MAKE TWO ACTS DISAGREE,
-- which is a stronger thing than exhibiting one module's behaviour: the widow
-- with and without a redundant `Guardian` record (scenario 1 step 4, and again
-- in scenario 6), the uncle the surviving parent objects to (step 4), the
-- soldier's will against the civilian's put to the Guardianship Act (3), and
-- the person whose majority date is nowhere in evidence (5A). On each of them
-- a composition-level assertion FAILS or changes value, where before the review
-- of 2026-08-26 nothing in this file could have.
--
-- WHAT WAS MEASURED, AND WHEN. On 2026-08-26, with all four Act modules and
-- the shared ontology imported into this one file, `l4 check` succeeds and
-- `l4 run` exits 0 with 352 assertions satisfied and none failing. (The file
-- held 234 before the review and 343 before the join audit's repairs; no
-- assertion was deleted, and the ones that were rewritten were rewritten from
-- the section and not from what the evaluator printed.) Every member of every type this file declares is reached by an
-- assertion:
--   `What this corpus can say about the distribution of an estate` — the
--     governed case in scenario 1, the s 2 stop in scenario 4, the no-effect
--     case in 2A and 6, and the s 4(1) stop, the s 4(1) empty fund and the s 5
--     stop in 4B;
--   `Whether a guardian could give a good discharge for the infant’s share` —
--     the affirmative in scenario 1 and 5F, the negative in 1 and 6, and the
--     cannot-be-asked case in scenario 3 step 4;
--   `Whether section 21(2) and the Guardianship of Infants Act agree about this
--     grant` — all four, in scenario 1 step 4 and scenario 6;
--   `Whether the case file agrees with the Wills Act about the will` — all
--     four, across 2A, 2B, 3 and 4.
-- The two reading helpers are asserted at a nil fraction and at a positive one.
--
-- WHAT WAS NOT MEASURED: mutation coverage. The four Act modules report having
-- been mutation-tested conjunct by conjunct; this file has not been, and its
-- assertions should be read as evidence that the joins hold on the families
-- below and not as evidence that every conjunct of every joined rule has a
-- failing case here.
